 "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/graph.jsonld",
 "@graph": [
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-022",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-022",
   "text": "Provenance is not established by recording activity during execution.\n\nSimmhan, Plale, and Gannon classify provenance systems by representation, storage, and dissemination. Their classification matters because each function answers a different institutional question. Representation determines what a record says. Storage determines whether the record survives. Dissemination determines whether another system or person can use it.\n\nThe survey treats persistence as a design problem. Provenance may be embedded with the data or stored separately. The authors explain that a system must decide whether provenance is immutable, updated, or versioned. They also identify archival retention as an answer to storage cost. These choices are not equivalent. An execution trace held only by a running service has not resolved any of them. It may describe what the service observed, but its availability still depends on the service and the session that produced it.\n\nPortability and interpretation impose separate conditions. The survey describes derivation graphs, search, retrieval interfaces, and replication at another site as methods for disseminating provenance. It also explains why syntax alone is insufficient. Semantic information, including ontologies that define concepts and relationships, supplies context for interpreting a lineage record. A detailed history permits users to assess whether data is acceptable only when the record preserves enough meaning for that assessment. A transferable file without stable identifiers, defined relations, and contextual terms may be portable as bytes while remaining unusable as evidence.\n\nThe source is a peer-reviewed survey of scientific data systems published in 2005. It does not study autonomous agents, sovereign runtimes, legal admissibility, or adversarial deletion. It reports a taxonomy and surveyed design choices rather than a controlled comparison. The authors also found no metadata standard that worked across disciplines. The evidence therefore does not establish that one format guarantees durable or universally intelligible provenance. The institutional requirement is narrower. Runtime telemetry should be treated as an input to provenance, not as completed provenance. Before the originating session ends, the relevant record should be sealed into a versioned and retrievable artifact. Its identifiers, relations, time references, and governing vocabulary should travel with it. A party that did not observe the execution must be able to recover the record, verify its integrity, and understand the causal account without depending on the original service.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/1084805.1084812"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-022"
   },
   "sha256": "489ef10e15b5d6697601d3ac7f28ba38421fd31aebfaa180e3ec1bebb15c55b9",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed survey article with complete publisher full text and provenance-system taxonomy",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-021",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-021",
   "text": "Mazloomzadeh and his coauthors provide empirical support for the incentive mechanism that arises when reputation controls access. Stack Overflow describes reputation as a measure of community trust. Users earn the score through contributions and peer feedback. The score also determines what a user may do. Higher scores unlock voting and moderation privileges. Reputation therefore operates as more than public recognition. It becomes a credential for platform authority.\n\nThe paper connects this coupling to manipulation. The authors reviewed 1,697 posts from Stack Overflow meta sites and identified voting rings, bounty gaming, revenge voting, and closing rings. Voting rings coordinate accounts to inflate one another's scores. Bounty gaming transfers reputation through awards that do not reflect answer quality. The authors also developed two detection methods. Approximately 60 to 80 percent of the users flagged by those methods later had reputation removed by Stack Overflow for suspicious activity.\n\nThe most direct observation concerns privilege escalation. The study records a highly reputed administrator who used bounties to move another user toward the score required for moderation privileges. The manipulation targeted the performance signal because the signal carried operational power. This evidence supports the proposition that attaching authority to reputation increases the return from manipulating reputation. The source does not establish that reputation alone caused every observed fraud. It also does not measure the marginal cost of manipulation or compare an advisory score with an otherwise identical privilege-bearing score. The evidence is a peer-reviewed empirical study of one online developer community. It relies partly on discussions of reported fraud and on platform responses to algorithmically flagged users. It does not study sovereign local agent runtimes, autonomous execution, financial loss, or legal authority. Its findings cannot establish a universal incentive ratio. They do show a realized pathway from coordinated feedback to inflated reputation and from inflated reputation to moderation power.\n\nThe institutional consequence is narrow. A reputation system should not decide whether an actor may cross a security boundary. If reputation remains visible for coordination or evaluation, authorization should rest on separately issued and revocable credentials. This separation removes privilege escalation from the direct payoff of reputation gaming. It does not eliminate manipulation of the signal, but it prevents a manipulated performance measure from becoming authority merely because its number increased.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3691627"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-021"
   },
   "sha256": "60fed3dba61f0e0be4fbf97e93801fc7b62f3cee755f92c64be834b43abe70ba",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed ACM journal article with complete public author manuscript and empirical platform study",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-020",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-020",
   "text": "Putra and his coauthors provide a concrete qualification to the proposed separation between reputation and authorization. Their peer-reviewed design does not permit a reputation score to execute access by itself because a resource owner first signs an attribute-based access policy. A policy contract then evaluates the requested action, mandatory attributes, payment balance, and minimum trust and reputation scores, and only that contract issues the access token.\n\nThe distinction is material, but the result is not merely advisory reputation. The minimum trust and reputation scores form part of the access policy. Algorithm 1 requires both thresholds to be satisfied before authorization can succeed. A higher score may therefore change the token outcome and enlarge effective access within the authority already committed by the resource owner. The enforcement boundary remains separate. It consists of the signed policy, the policy contract, token issuance, and token validation by the data store.\n\nThe article reports a proof-of-concept implementation on a public Rinkeby test network connected to a lab scale Internet of Things testbed, and its evaluation addresses score evolution, authorization latency, access latency, and gas consumption. It does not study sovereign local agent runtimes. It does not provide a formal security proof, field evidence across adversarial deployments, or a legal account of delegated authority. The authors choose to couple reputation to permission, but their evidence does not establish that this coupling is generally safe.\n\nThe categorical rule should therefore distinguish a reputation signal from the authority that makes the signal operative. Reputation must not authenticate itself, define its own threshold, issue its own token, or bypass revocation. If a system uses reputation inside authorization, the resource owner must bind its permitted role in advance and an independent enforcement mechanism must remain authoritative. For sovereign runtimes, an advisory-only rule may still be the safer institutional design. The external evidence shows, however, that reputation can influence a permission decision without replacing the enforcement boundary. The relevant prohibition is self-authorizing reputation, not every policy-bound use of a reputation input.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1109/TNSM.2021.3077276"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-020"
   },
   "sha256": "a04536c9c9f74fa28280c7794a36e08884da07c9580b8bf4fef4b6a4ddb722c5",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal systems paper with complete public author manuscript and lab scale proof-of-concept evaluation",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-019",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-019",
   "text": "Enterprise deployability is an institutional property that Horneber and Laumer classify across social, institutional, organizational, and technical accountability layers. Their central distinction is that a machine learning system, as a technical artifact, cannot itself be held accountable, while the organization and the people who develop, operate, or employ it remain the parties who must justify its design, use, and outcomes.\n\nThis distinction changes the deployment question. Technical reliability is relevant. It does not designate the actor who bears consequences, provides information, or satisfies external demands. Provider and operator organizations may be different. Responsibility must therefore be allocated across management, developers, product owners, quality personnel, and system users. Internal governance converts that allocation into policies, standardized development and deployment rules, audits, impact assessment, incident reporting, and supported human oversight.\n\nThe article is a peer-reviewed conceptual account of machine learning governance, and it does not study sovereign local agent runtimes, commercial insurance, indemnification, or enterprise approval rates. It also does not establish that any listed control is sufficient for coverage or legal compliance. Its contribution is narrower: technical artifacts cannot absorb institutional accountability, and diffuse participation requires explicit organizational measures if external demands are to be satisfied.\n\nThe resulting design requirement extends beyond a technically adequate runtime. Before deployment, the parties should identify the provider and operator and bind named accountable roles. They should preserve evidence needed to justify decisions, define internal approval and escalation, and allocate liability, indemnity, and residual risk by contract. Insurability then becomes a test of whether a consequential loss can be attributed, priced, monitored, and remedied. If no participant can answer for the system, technical performance does not cure the institutional defect. The enterprise has no stable basis for approval. Its governance and risk-transfer mechanisms have no accountable counterparty.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s12599-023-00817-8"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-019"
   },
   "sha256": "fab3cd38c015361a0c4862f6b5121a9f3b03a2191869ecb696b6e6d376632191",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal conceptual analysis with complete open-access publisher full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-018",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-018",
   "text": "Threat resistance belongs inside the specification of a reputation system. Hoffman, Zage, and Nita-Rotaru decompose a reputation system into formulation, calculation, and dissemination components, classify attack classes, and use that decomposition to identify the design choices each attack exploits. This method links an attack class to a defense rather than treating a reputation score as self-validating.\n\nTheir survey identifies a specific composite mechanism. The attack is composite. One actor can acquire multiple identities and collude through them even when source data is authenticated. The colluding identities can participate mutually in events that generate real feedback at a rate that improves their scores faster than honest participation. This mechanism closely corresponds to wash interaction because the events are produced to manufacture feedback rather than to evidence independent service quality. Whitewashing permits an attacker to abandon a degraded identity and re-enter with a fresh reputation. Orchestrated attacks combine these strategies and change roles over time.\n\nThe source is a peer-reviewed survey of peer-to-peer reputation systems, and it does not study autonomous agent markets, measure attack prevalence, or test a particular production system. Its taxonomy predates blockchain wash trading terminology and does not analyze reputation transfer between task domains. Reputation is defined for a given domain and purpose, which supports contextual specification but does not establish that a score safe in one context can be carried safely into another. No surveyed system defends against every catalogued attack.\n\nThe design consequence is narrower than a general demand for a secure score. A scoring rule should state the attacker identities it permits, the interactions it treats as independent, the coalitions it assumes, the cost of re-entry, and the contexts across which history may travel. It should map each assumption to a tested defense and identify uncovered attack classes. Authentication alone cannot supply this result because authenticated identities can still collude and create feedback. A system that omits these bounds publishes a number without describing how adversaries may manufacture it.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/1592451.1592452"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-018"
   },
   "sha256": "051b1518dc71e42c0a8329aca7ff1e5c29cafd929646dae2966d61ea49a66af7",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed ACM Computing Surveys article with complete author-hosted manuscript and DOI-bound Crossref identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.93,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-017",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-017",
   "text": "Reputation is a selection mechanism. Gandhi, Hollenbeck, and Li study products that purchased fake reviews on Amazon and the honest products that competed with them, and their equilibrium simulations attribute a 27.2 percent increase in units sold to the manipulators and a 4.4 percent decline for honest products. The manipulated signal distorted description and redirected market share.\n\nThe result supplies a concrete market mechanism for selection on claims. Consumers formed beliefs about quality from observed reviews and their perceived trustworthiness. Sellers could purchase favorable reviews that raised perceived quality. The resulting misinformation induced some consumers to choose lower quality products. Those products carried inflated ratings and higher prices. Honest sellers lost market share even though their products had not deteriorated. A reputation signal became an input controlled by the party seeking selection rather than an independent record of the relevant outcome.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than the institutional claim because the study concerns Amazon product markets, not service procurement or sovereign local agent runtimes. Its market effects come from a structural demand model and counterfactual simulations. The classification of review purchasers and the estimated share of fake reviews introduce measurement uncertainty. The paper does not test installation counts, self reported capability, or task specific performance. It also does not establish that every static rating is non-contextual or manipulable.\n\nA service market should therefore separate promotional reputation from verified performance. Selection should depend on outcomes for the relevant task class. Those outcomes should be measured over a defined period and attributed to an independent source. The record should disclose uncertainty and failed engagements. It should also record changes in operating conditions and the relation between the prior task and the proposed task. Provider supplied claims may remain discoverable. They should not control selection. Without contextual outcome evidence, a market may price the strength of the signal while misallocating the work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.nber.org/papers/w34161"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-017"
   },
   "sha256": "730a716450d62ec2e719fdf254f33e8560cb1770d8db0a2fe62e5ed79d8ffb78",
   "evidenceLevel": "NBER working paper with complete author-posted manuscript and structural empirical marketplace analysis",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-016",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-016",
   "text": "Revocation is a state problem. Chen and his coauthors compare Intel MPK, ARM PAC, ARM MTE, and ARM Morello capabilities. They find that rights retained in registers on other cores prevent a revoking core from establishing that the right has disappeared, unless the system uses an interprocessor interrupt or a restricted single core model. Of the four mechanisms, only MTE implements the core coherent synchronization principle they identify.\n\nThe result supplies a concrete mechanism for failed propagation. Withdrawal can be correct at the source. An active component may still hold and exercise a derived instance of the grant. The issuer's record proves a local change. The prior right may remain live elsewhere. Local withdrawal is not cessation. Every live instance matters. A current validity check may instead stand between each instance and the protected action.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than the institutional claim. The paper studies access to memory across processor cores. It does not study legal grants, delegated purpose, revocation across machines, or sovereign local agent runtimes. It also does not establish that few systems can demonstrate propagation across the full universe of authorization systems. Its comparative result is limited to four modern hardware isolation mechanisms.\n\nA sovereign runtime should treat revocation as a systemwide transition with an observable completion condition. Each execution path must consult current authority state or operate through a revocable indirection. A completion receipt should bind the grant, the cutoff, the affected holders, synchronization evidence, and the first rejected use after the cutoff. The distinction controls the claim. Without that evidence, an issuer can demonstrate withdrawal. It cannot demonstrate cessation.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.usenix.org/conference/atc24/presentation/chen-xiangdong"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-016"
   },
   "sha256": "b9e8582dfcb2438f698370af6cb11331495f465b4dff8838824a0d03ac7d0184",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed USENIX ATC conference paper with complete official proceedings full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-015",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-015",
   "text": "Authority can exceed consent even when the final permission check is correct. Felt and her coauthors identify permission redelegation where an application holding a user granted permission performs a privileged task for an application that lacks it. The system approves the call because the immediate deputy has the required permission. The requester nonetheless causes an action that it could not invoke directly. The paper reports fifteen such vulnerabilities across five core Android applications.\n\nThe mechanism supplies a concrete instance of authority drift. A user grants authority to one application. Interapplication communication then places that authority under the influence of a less privileged requester. A permissions system that evaluates only the immediate caller sees a valid grant and permits the action. It does not preserve the bounded purpose or authority of every participant in the chain. Local validity therefore coexists with an aggregate result outside the user's authorization.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than the institutional claim. The study concerns browsers and smartphone applications in 2011. It does not examine sovereign local agent runtimes, legal delegation, contractual purpose, or every form of authority drift. It also does not prove that permissions systems are least equipped to detect this failure relative to other failures. The institutional implication remains defensible. A runtime should carry authority provenance through every delegation boundary and evaluate a request against the intersection of the authority held along the full chain of influence. An intentional increase in authority requires a separate, explicit grant bound to the action and the principal. Otherwise a sequence of correct permission decisions can still produce an unauthorized result.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity11/permission-re-delegation-attacks-and-defenses"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-015"
   },
   "sha256": "2ab5fe2e14e8d2dca65d2cd11ad430837b3d355db52cfa545648a7af93fe80a0",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed USENIX Security conference paper with complete official proceedings full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-014",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-014",
   "text": "Attribution is a system property, not a label added after failure. Kroll's account of traceability connects the provenance of components, design choices, and operation to specific outcomes and to the question of whom to hold responsible. He also draws a necessary boundary. A mechanical path from input to output does not explain why the system works as it does. Traceability depends on the system in use, its origins, and the decisions that produced its behavior.\n\nThat distinction is especially important when a workflow crosses independently operated components. A record that an authorized call occurred may identify the caller. It does not establish which component contributed to the harmful outcome, whether the component behaved within its assigned function, or which design and operational decisions made the outcome possible. Without that evidence, responsibility becomes a contest over assertions rather than a finding grounded in the composition.\n\nThe source does not prove legal fault, measure incentive effects, or study sovereign local agent runtimes. It treats traceability as an enabling value for accountability and risk management. The institutional implication is therefore bounded. A runtime should preserve contribution provenance at each composition boundary, bind components and decisions to outcomes, and retain enough context for independent review. The governing rule must separately specify how that evidence becomes responsibility, remedy, or changed incentives. Traceability cannot itself choose the rule. It makes a defensible choice possible and exposes the absence of one.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.09385"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-014"
   },
   "sha256": "177020e7dfc56bd108bf696bf92726d4e5cf59f455c14cfac73ad5c85298f088",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed ACM FAccT conference paper with complete public author manuscript",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-013",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-013",
   "text": "Accountability is not a single property of an agent runtime. Chappidi and her coauthors define an accountability relationship through an actor who owes an account, a forum entitled to receive it, and information that permits review and response. They then distinguish records from a system, which describe its operation, from records about a system, which describe how it was commissioned, built, deployed, and monitored. Records may support investigation, oversight, challenge, repair, and redress. The paper also identifies accountability capture. The apparatus used to observe conduct can reconfigure the conduct, induce evasion, and distort the accountability regime itself.\n\nThis structure independently supports separate diagnosis. Attribution fails when operational records cannot reconstruct what occurred. Authority drifts when the actor, forum, governing requirement, or design decision is not preserved across commissioning and use. Evaluation becomes captured when the measurement apparatus changes behavior or serves interests that defeat the accountability purpose. Recourse disappears when records do not reach a forum capable of challenge, response, and remedy.\n\nThe correspondence is functional, not terminological. The paper does not use Kaal's four labels, prove that the taxonomy is exhaustive, or study sovereign local agent runtimes. Its survey concerns organizational record keeping around algorithmic systems. It nonetheless shows why a general appeal to trust is inadequate. Each failure has different evidence and a different institutional repair. A runtime assessment should therefore test traceability, delegated authority, evaluator independence, and enforceable recourse separately.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AIES/article/view/36570"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-013"
   },
   "sha256": "38f9e6e566744df00994bcec4c6ac17c02ce5e1e1c2f6917b8b4c796a883d844",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed AIES conference paper with complete official proceedings full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-012",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-012",
   "text": "Scale is an amortization problem before it is a throughput problem. Chen and her coauthors make this distinction explicit in AgentSlimming. Their starting workflow is a graph of agents and communication edges. The method prunes redundant nodes, substitutes lower-cost models, and retains changes only when baseline performance survives. Across eight benchmarks, the resulting workflows reduced recurring token and API expense while preserving or improving selected task scores. The authors then separate the one-time search and evaluation burden from per-query inference savings and calculate time to break even.\n\nThis evidence extends Kaal's claim in a narrower and testable form. A benchmark that reports accuracy and inference cost after workflow selection measures the performance of an arrangement. It does not disclose what it cost to discover, validate, and maintain that arrangement. Scale cannot be inferred from per-query cost alone. The design cost must be amortized over actual use, and a topology change must be charged for the validation required to preserve acceptable behavior.\n\nThe limits are material. AgentSlimming studies cloud-model workflow graphs, public reasoning benchmarks, and task-level optimization. It does not examine sovereign local runtimes, latency, institutional coordination, security review, or legal arrangements. It also finds cost reductions for a compression method, not a universal constraint on scaling. Sovereign runtime benchmarks should therefore report design and search effort, validation cost, recurring inference cost, break-even volume, topology changes, and performance loss as separate quantities. The cost of arranging the system belongs in the scaling result.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1387/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-012"
   },
   "sha256": "d95a8ae378d6d44ff2a937f9ecd54116a1206c678e58ef35be92eadaafeb48bf",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed conference paper with complete official proceedings full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-011",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-011",
   "text": "Scale changes the institutional problem. Lera and Sornette model the coordination cost of a flat organization as approximately proportional to N(N minus 1) divided by 2, the number of pairwise interactions. The cost is therefore quadratic in group size. Their hierarchical model reorganizes those interactions into bounded groups and higher levels that coordinate and control them. This result gives independent support to the scaling mechanism in Kaal's claim, but not to its full legal content.\n\nThe model concerns human organizations, communication, and control. It does not measure responsibility, remedy, value attribution, security review, or agent integration. It also does not establish that every component arrangement exhibits the same exponent. The practical consequence is narrower. An architecture that leaves each participant to negotiate responsibility and settlement separately exposes the system to a pairwise burden. A common institutional layer changes that topology. It can define shared identity, evidence, allocation, and remedy rules once, then make bilateral exceptions explicit.\n\nThe core should record which rule governed each outcome and preserve the evidence required to apply it. The remaining claim is empirical. Integration effort, security review, support burden, and user understanding must be measured across deployments, with component count and interface density separated. Until those measurements exist, quadratic coordination theory supports the design direction. It does not establish a production cost estimate.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0214911"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-011"
   },
   "sha256": "08a929418e97d9034915748b4105f9e26d24f2a60d9132ba0d671f9fdf779712",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal article with complete official version-of-record full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.93,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-010",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-010",
   "text": "A crossing core can decide whether an agent may act. It cannot decide who should bear the consequences of a joint result merely by replaying an authorized call. Triantafyllou, Singla, and Radanovic formalize blame attribution in cooperative multi-agent sequential decision making as a separate allocation problem. Their comparison shows that the selected rule matters. Shapley value can fail performance monotonicity, Banzhaf allocation may over-blame, and more cautious methods sacrifice explanatory power. The same trace can therefore support materially different assignments of responsibility under different allocation rules.\n\nThis evidence narrows the institutional boundary. Identity, authority, and permitted data use are call-level predicates. Responsibility and value attribution concern the contribution of each participant to an outcome and the normative properties of the rule used to allocate that outcome. The source does not establish legal liability, contractual remedies, or production behavior in agent runtimes. It studies cooperative Markov decision processes and simulated policies. Its contribution is nonetheless decisive for architecture: authenticated execution does not select a defensible responsibility rule.\n\nA sovereign runtime should require the parties to bind the outcome metric, contribution evidence, allocation rule, uncertainty treatment, remedy, and value distribution before execution. The core may enforce that settlement. It should not silently invent it after the result.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2021/hash/848c4965359e617d5e16c924b4a85fd9-Abstract.html"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-010"
   },
   "sha256": "5829dcfbf84c4993bcde3647157c5e558c549da41701d531897b021f2a7c70ac",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed conference paper with complete official proceedings full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-009",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-009",
   "text": "Technical interoperability does not settle an institutional relation. Swales, Gooden, and Thaldar reach the same boundary through a scoping review of twenty-four data transfer agreements. The agreements identify the parties, state the purpose of the transfer, allocate duties, restrict processing, provide for audit, assign liability, and specify termination and dispute resolution. These clauses do more than document an exchange. They determine who acts, what the recipient may do, how compliance is assessed, who bears loss, and what follows from breach. The evidence independently extends Kaal's claim: a new integration needs a governance settlement before data or authority crosses the boundary.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than Kaal's claim. The review studies English-language agreements for health research. It treats the identity of legal parties rather than authentication of software components. It does not test sovereign agent runtimes or automated remedies. The institutional mechanism nevertheless transfers. A runtime should translate the agreement into executable fields: verified component identity, delegated authority, purpose and duration limits, assigned responsibility, audit rights, termination, and redress. An unset field is not a harmless implementation gap. It leaves a material term unresolved. The integration gate should therefore refuse execution until each field names an accountable actor and an enforceable consequence. Technical compatibility can open a channel. Only the prior settlement makes its use institutionally defensible.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1332700"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-009"
   },
   "sha256": "10f0cfe7c395a1dba78c36999737123d0c77b0d9c2ce28f20fc93e50f52c5d39",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed scoping review with complete open full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-008",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-008",
   "text": "Digital coordination lowers a transaction cost. It does not eliminate the economic boundary that creates it. Baldwin and Robert-Nicoud model fragmented production as trade in tasks. Their framework assigns one cost to coordinating an offshore task within the firm and an additional nonnegative cost when a separate firm performs it. Advances in information and communication technology can lower both costs, but the effect varies by task. Routine, codifiable tasks may approach zero coordination cost, while complex tasks can remain too costly to move. The mechanism independently extends Kaal's claim: speed and automation can conceal the cross-boundary relation without converting it into an internal operation.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than Kaal's claim. The NBER paper studies international production tasks in a formal trade model, not sovereign local agents, device-level execution, or machine transactions. Its zero-cost cases are modeling assumptions, not empirical findings that external relations are costless. The useful extension is therefore institutional. Each external invocation should be classified as a separate-firm transaction unless ownership and authority establish otherwise. The system should expose the terms that automation removes from human view: whether a task is codifiable, whether coordination occurs within or between firms, and what additional cost independent execution creates. Machine speed increases the frequency of that relation. It does not erase the boundary.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w15882"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-008"
   },
   "sha256": "8d4c8b1838f1a0d7e853cb262c9496d3001d025db86ee2294f7d21f4d9157107",
   "evidenceLevel": "NBER working paper with complete public full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-007",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-007",
   "text": "The boundary between a feature and a counterparty is not determined by technical integration alone. Alfaro and her coauthors show why ownership matters. Within firm boundaries, management can reassign control among integrated suppliers. Outside the firm, an independent supplier retains control through separate ownership. Non-integration has legal force, and reversal or dispute requires legal institutions between parties. The same mechanism applies when software crosses an economic boundary.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than Kaal's claim. The study examines firms, input suppliers, and production decisions rather than plugins or sovereign agent runtimes. It does not establish that every external service demands the same institutional form. It establishes the controlling classification. A vendor-written plugin remains subject to the vendor's internal authority, control allocation, and accountability. A separately operated service retains its own decision rights and exposes the invoking firm to contracting frictions, ownership boundaries, and disputes between independent parties. Technical compatibility cannot collapse that distinction. Runtime architecture should therefore identify when an invocation leaves the vendor's governance boundary. At that point, institutional requirements attach to the relation: authority, contractual allocation, audit, remedies, and a forum for dispute.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w24603"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-007"
   },
   "sha256": "fd194fcb65d4d07cdc07304b7c1ac83f477283413cd190e3e2bc488366c0a9cb",
   "evidenceLevel": "NBER working paper with complete public full text, revised October 2020",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-006",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-006",
   "text": "Machine settlement is not only a transport function. Athanassiou's analysis of automated payments shows why. Smart contracts can validate, execute, and record pre-agreed responses without human intermediation. Once value moves, however, the arrangement raises questions about contractual intent, counterparty identity, settlement finality, liability, and access to adjudication when code fails or parties dispute the result. Automation therefore changes the institutional classification of the system even when the transfer protocol remains technically sound.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than Kaal's claim. The ECB paper examines distributed ledgers, smart contracts, and retail payments rather than sovereign agent runtimes. It is legal analysis, not empirical evidence that a particular machine economy works. Its mechanism still carries. A runtime that can settle with another machine needs more than payment connectivity. It needs an attributable principal, a defined authorization boundary, an auditable record of the obligation, and a route for error correction and dispute. These requirements should be bound before execution. Otherwise a technically final transfer may leave the economic relation legally uncertain and institutionally unaccountable.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2866/201593"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-006"
   },
   "sha256": "6b385d8e59321fe9bfcf57815040e2518dd337de70f81055519c60d37b5aef76",
   "evidenceLevel": "European Central Bank Legal Working Paper with complete public full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-005",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-005",
   "text": "Encryption at rest does not establish user-held state when the service provider controls the keys. Hofmann and Truong show why. In their analysis of end-to-end encrypted cloud storage, provider-held keys supply no protection once the provider is compromised. The relevant alternative encrypts files under keys managed by the user and evaluates confidentiality against a server with full access to its own infrastructure. This moves custody from a promise about data location to a test of decryption authority.\n\nThe evidence is narrower than the general claim. The study concerns cloud storage, not agent working memory, and it documents flaws even in systems marketed as zero knowledge. User-managed keys can still fail through protocol defects, malicious client delivery, weak credentials, or metadata leakage. Those limits sharpen the classification. State is not user-held merely because ciphertext sits behind an ownership label. It is user-held only when the operator lacks the capability to recover the working contents without the user's participation. A credible system should test that condition against a compromised provider, not against its marketing description.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3658644.3690309"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-005"
   },
   "sha256": "a9624f484e312d4c136fef8f1374c72614461f7ce811e840b40093b79bbbc992",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed ACM CCS 2024 conference paper with complete public full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-004",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-004",
   "text": "Offline capability is an architectural test, not a feature label. Kleppmann and coauthors distinguish cloud applications from local-first software by the location of the primary data copy, the presence of a locally installed executable, and continued operation without a network connection. Their prototypes permit users to disconnect, continue working, and later merge changes. Applications centered on synchronous server interaction instead lock users out when the network fails. This independently extends Kaal's operational test: retained function under isolation supplies observable evidence that computation and state remain on the user's device.\n\nThe correspondence has limits. Local-first software addresses collaborative data applications, not agent runtimes or model inference. Offline editing does not establish that every relevant computation occurs locally, and later synchronization may still depend on external infrastructure. The paper nevertheless sharpens the classification rule. A local interface cannot establish local execution when the underlying capability disappears at disconnection. Network isolation converts an architectural claim into a falsifiable test.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3359591.3359737"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-004"
   },
   "sha256": "c23ceec6f927685140fe30fb678300049e05ceff1d01e28fec1966f69721f69c",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Onward! 2019 conference paper with complete public full text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-003",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-003",
   "text": "Xu's five-layer architecture helps convert the classification of sovereign local agent runtimes into an operational inquiry. The proposed system links verifiable execution to decentralized identity and state verification, adds standardized tool composition through MCP, and supplies autonomous payments through programmable wallets. These layers must operate together before an agent becomes independent of human intermediaries. That architecture independently supports the proposition that class membership should be tested against system properties rather than branding.\n\nThe correspondence remains qualified. Xu addresses decentralized economic agents, not sovereign local runtimes. The paper does not require user-held state, and it treats agent independence, rather than user control, as the principal criterion. It is also a conceptual arXiv preprint without an empirical implementation. The useful extension is therefore narrow: a claimed agent class becomes analytically meaningful only when its execution, state, composition, and settlement mechanisms can be verified as an integrated system.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14219v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-003"
   },
   "sha256": "b7ffe252b913b35b4f6e086706d6a1f0cbee5687188675c407a4d3602eb9f195",
   "evidenceLevel": "conceptual arXiv v1 preprint with complete public full text and arXiv identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.9,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-002",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-002",
   "text": "Custody is not an execution history. Kifor and coauthors study agent-mediated healthcare systems in which data, procedures, and logs are divided among heterogeneous autonomous information systems. They show that provenance-aware instrumentation can record events, dependencies, and human decisions across those systems. The resulting process documentation represents the distributed execution, connects its causal steps, and permits later reconstruction and audit. This independently extends Kaal's failure claim: custody identifies where the bytes reside, but a separate recording architecture must preserve what the composed services did.\n\nThe evidence is a 2006 peer-reviewed healthcare systems article. Its agents coordinate medical services, not sovereign local LLM runtimes. The paper does not test whether any current local runtime preserves a sufficient history or whether provenance alone creates institutional accountability. One design requirement follows. Composed services need execution-time provenance before custody can answer what happened.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1109/MIS.2006.119"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-002"
   },
   "sha256": "e5f9aec08e0b06630dea2402fd2578f7d44b77caf3b7c2b61cc1b1711e14d607",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal article with complete public accepted manuscript and Crossref identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-26-001",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-26-001",
   "text": "Cryptographic self-custody can secure operational sovereignty without supplying institutional accountability. Hu and Rong show that decentralized agents derive persistence and resistance to override from cryptographic keys, trusted execution environments, and distributed protocols. The same layered architecture diffuses responsibility across developers, infrastructure providers, protocol governance, and economic participants. Their analysis independently extends Kaal's distinction: control over custody identifies where assets sit and who can reach them, but does not identify who acted under authority or who answers for a harmful outcome.\n\nThe source is a 2026 arXiv preprint submitted to FAccT, not a peer-reviewed final article. It analyzes decentralized agents and infrastructural hardness. It does not establish that every local runtime lacks accountability. The evidence supports a narrower institutional conclusion: sovereign custody must be joined to identifiable actors, enforceable duties, and remedial consequences.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-26",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14951v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7314479-001"
   },
   "sha256": "6390b9b7374f2244e6bc489a31c7abd9e77972ede8b6008ecfc5131c2608f1ac",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public arXiv v1 preprint with DataCite identity and exact full-text passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-373",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-373",
   "text": "Formal expenditure authority does not secure fiscal autonomy. Chernyavsky and Vartapetov report that Russian municipalities remained completely dependent on higher-level governments despite significant formal expenditure powers. This evidence extends Kaal's claim that local authority must be paired with control over adequate revenues. It does not establish that the same dependency arises in every decentralized system.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.32609/0042-8736-2003-10-94-107"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808852-031"
   },
   "sha256": "9dcddf708de4a33a1dec9f6219b62939500f911c7c56aaf06937dc3fc9c6b825",
   "evidenceLevel": "Current run-bound Crossref record, publisher abstract, and immutable review receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.64,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-372",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-372",
   "text": "GPoS: Geospatially-aware Proof of Stake reports a distinct concentration mechanism in proof-of-stake consensus. Across Aptos, Avalanche, Ethereum, Solana, and Sui, a few geographic regions dominate consensus voting power. The study reports limited geospatial decentralization and tests a geospatially aware voting design. Its experiments report a 45 percent average improvement in geospatial decentralization with minimal overhead in the tested BFT protocols. Kaal's claim that token-weighted proof-of-stake governance can concentrate control is extended along a geographic dimension. That extension does not validate the wealth-based mechanism. Only the Crossref-indexed abstract passed because the ACM PDF returned HTTP 403. Broader governance outcomes remain unverified.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3774629"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4796714-014"
   },
   "sha256": "7174f83d5f09716e231e9567edb39ea5b8ab1ef7236c920cc4d5728193ca3478",
   "evidenceLevel": "Current Crossref record with immutable review receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-371",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-371",
   "text": "Blockchain Technologies: Probability of Double-Spend Attack on a Proof-of-Stake Consensus quantifies two attack strategies. Their success probabilities depend on network parameters and confirmation depth. The authors use those results to derive the number of confirmations a vendor should await before delivering goods or services. These findings extend Kaal's claim that fixed consensus rules cannot eliminate strategic attacks by independent nodes. Transaction finality and confirmation depth bound, but do not remove, proof-of-stake double-spend risk. Only the Crossref-indexed abstract passed because the publisher PDF returned HTTP 403. No inference reaches every proof-of-stake protocol.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/s21196408"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3125827-001"
   },
   "sha256": "1b6993119a01208cd52ebf51931816b6e4ee72c860aa3863f76ac678971b515d",
   "evidenceLevel": "Current Crossref record with immutable review receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-370",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-370",
   "text": "Strategic Mining in Proof-of-Stake with Practical Random Election identifies a concrete failure in validator selection. Distributed random beacons fix randomness across several slots because of latency. Validators can infer future election information. The study reports that, without propagation delay, a validator with any proportion of stake can profit from strategic mining and obtain higher block rewards than prior strategies. The result extends Kaal's claim that fixed consensus rules remain vulnerable to strategic gaming as conditions change. It is limited to the modeled protocol and its no-delay assumption. The evidence does not establish failure across all proof-of-stake systems.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.33140/amlai.06.04.01"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3125827-001"
   },
   "sha256": "0260a891e9f69d4f3271112f29539e8e05215a993d972f5a4a71cceae04aa157",
   "evidenceLevel": "Current Crossref record with immutable review receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-369",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-369",
   "text": "Oracles reveal an external-data boundary in smart contracts. Veritas examines a decentralized Ethereum oracle architecture for information that does not exist on chain. Simulation and experimental analysis reports lower operational costs. It also reports limited malicious data entry and preserved security. The architecture therefore extends Kaal's claim that a decentralized economy requires a trustworthy decentralized information layer, but the evidence remains limited to one Ethereum design. The result is consistent with a narrow mechanism, not a general rule. It does not establish a universal requirement for every news service. Nor does it prove that the node-selection process remains robust in production.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/jsan13020021"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782205-036"
   },
   "sha256": "ebb5b95da78bdcdf35c8247bb6b3ecb5220b03c1b09008bce155241b47065137",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref-indexed abstract with immutable provider receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-368",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-368",
   "text": "Quantum computing creates a two-sided problem for blockchain cryptography. Quantum-Secure Blockchain and Decentralized Systems evaluates lattice-based, multivariate, and hash-based signatures, then reports simulation-based robustness against quantum-enabled attacks while preserving decentralization, transparency, and immutability. Those results extend Kaal's claim that blockchain security depends on quantum-resistant algorithms. The available evidence is limited to the abstract. Because the source is posted preprint content, it cannot independently establish production readiness or the claimed efficiency benefits of quantum computing.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.22541/au.174465693.38695728/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4900878-034"
   },
   "sha256": "4ffbefbf4758b78e71b3dd4f4339d6f6c24d6000c58264982b33ed54b1f0996a",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref-indexed abstract with immutable provider receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-367",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-367",
   "text": "Secure reputation does not require a central authority in every network design. Dynamic Decentralized Reputation System from Blockchain and Secure Multiparty Computation uses blockchain to maintain global reputation and secure multiparty computation to preserve feedback privacy. The reported analysis maintains feedback privacy under a semi-honest model even with n minus 2 dishonest parties, which extends the reputation-infrastructure component of Kaal's claim by identifying a concrete decentralized mechanism. Its reach is narrower than Kaal's proposition. The model does not govern anonymous economic partners or supply dynamic governance. Nor does it establish economy-wide proliferation. This limitation matters because reputation infrastructure is only one of the institutions in the claim.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/jsan12010014"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782216-001"
   },
   "sha256": "5996c97c988fde3301c8503de621f3959036f22314806f06cdf0f49656ff0f85",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref-indexed abstract with immutable provider receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-366",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-366",
   "text": "Incomplete information is a separate constraint on reputation-based cooperation. Robust Reputation in Decentralized Markets reports that incomplete information can weaken trust and reputation in open multiagent systems, while gossip-based updates reduced selfishness by up to 25 percent and especially helped agents with little interaction history. This evidence extends Kaal's market-collapse claim because it adds information access as a condition for policeable cooperation, but its scope remains narrow because the study neither establishes collapse in commercial markets nor validates Kaal's broader institutional mechanism. The distinction is important because the source supplies a mitigation mechanism rather than evidence of market failure.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12036"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782210-002"
   },
   "sha256": "23a58f4789bc1bc4c5ecfdb3e083cb6db5f33cb5c1898b427802a485830a5d87",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref-indexed abstract with immutable provider receipt",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive general-literature extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-365",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-365",
   "text": "Breznau and coauthors qualify Kaal's classification of the current estimates as interim. In a computational reproduction experiment, 85 independent teams attempted to reproduce numerical results from the same study. Even the transparent group reproduced 95.7 percent of directions and significance classifications, but only 76.9 percent of estimates within 1 percent of the original values. Only 14 teams reproduced every result within that tolerance. The authors therefore show that access to code improves verification but does not remove researcher error or procedural variation. They estimate that more than one independent attempt may be needed for reliable reproduction.\n\nThe comparison is methodological. The source does not inspect Kaal's E2B campaign, reconcile its amendment authority, or verify its estimates. It supports treating those estimates as interim until the recorded provenance is reconciled and independent reproduction determines whether the numerical results hold.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.241038"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-032"
   },
   "sha256": "1596095c68b0ea61b81fc6575128e3ecc50449c549322c4a92420bd7925f6433",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed version-of-record PDF with DOI-bound Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, and Europe PMC identity records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-364",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-364",
   "text": "Post-treatment regrading can compromise a treatment-effect estimate.\n\nMontgomery, Nyhan, and Torres analyze post-treatment conditioning in experiments. They classify three recurring practices: controlling for variables measured after treatment, dropping observations under post-treatment criteria, and restricting samples by post-treatment variables. Their analytical demonstrations and reanalyses show that these choices can distort causal-effect estimates. The direction and size of the bias may not be bounded without strong assumptions. Random assignment does not preserve its identifying advantage once an analysis conditions on information created or selected after treatment.\n\nThis evidence qualifies Kaal's claim. Regrading a diagnostic after execution can alter the information used to evaluate an observed effect. If the regrading changes classifications, exclusions, or conditioning criteria after treatment, the resulting estimate may not identify the treatment effect claimed. Montgomery, Nyhan, and Torres do not examine Kaal's diagnostic runs. They also do not establish that every post-execution correction creates bias. A correction specified independently of treatment may instead improve measurement. The comparison supports a methodological limit. A treatment-effect conclusion requires the post-execution regrading rule, its timing, and its relation to treatment assignment to be independently reconciled.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/ajps.12357"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-031"
   },
   "sha256": "5e45b9200a3b10b114eff3f3b546a468f79fc0b326cd3823f151ce4619d08754",
   "evidenceLevel": "DOI-bound peer-reviewed publisher abstract and complete official AJPS author summary with independently concordant identity records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-363",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-363",
   "text": "An execution platform can coordinate labor without generating demand.\n\nKokkodis and Ipeirotis analyze more than one million completed tasks from oDesk. In this market, employers post tasks, remote contractors apply and perform the work, and reputation mechanisms help employers choose among workers whose ability cannot be observed directly before performance. Their models use prior category-specific feedback to predict worker performance. The platform therefore performs two related institutional functions. It coordinates execution and improves matching under uncertainty. The task itself continues to originate with an employer.\n\nThis evidence qualifies Kaal's claim. A reputation-governed execution surface may reduce information friction, improve matching, and discipline performance without becoming a source of its own demand. Kokkodis and Ipeirotis examine an online labor market, not Kaal's agents or present system. Their study also does not prove that every execution surface depends on external demand. It supplies a clear comparison class in which labor execution and reputation are endogenous to the platform, while task demand remains employer supplied. Whether Kaal's broader architecture generates endogenous demand requires separate evidence.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2015.2217"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-030"
   },
   "sha256": "72a4809a11179fc5c00b1aeefd2a552fde827c42fd997bc8283deaadb3c09463",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete author-hosted peer-reviewed Management Science article with DOI-bound identity and exact full-text proposition evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-362",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-362",
   "text": "Tool capability does not establish autonomous economic persistence.\n\nQu, Zhao, Zhang, and Song define a self-sovereign agent through distinct operational stages. A tool-capable agent remains sponsor-bound when a human supplies accounts, compute, and payment rails. Economic self-sustainment begins only when the agent controls funds and expected revenue covers inference, tool, cloud, transaction, and retry costs. Persistence requires a further mechanism. The agent must be able to provision new infrastructure and resume operation without human intervention.\n\nThis framework qualifies Kaal's negative result. The present system may demonstrate execution inside an externally provisioned environment. That evidence does not by itself establish that the agents earned revenue, controlled the resources required for continued operation, covered their own operating costs, or persisted across hosts. Qu and coauthors do not inspect Kaal's agents, execution records, resource accounts, or financing. Their paper supplies criteria for classifying economic self-sustainment and persistence. Whether Kaal's present system satisfied those criteria remains bound to Kaal's evidence.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08551v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-029"
   },
   "sha256": "173166b36c4753d9870c4f6db3f880d2458abb2d891267dd48567886355ad5af",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public arXiv v1 preprint with author-bound identity and exact full-text proposition evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-361",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-361",
   "text": "An externally supplied experimental environment does not make the selection process external to the experiment.\n\nBatut, Parsons, Fischer, Beslon, and Knibbe describe in silico experimental evolution. They separate the setup from the operation. Population size, spatial structure, the fitness measure, and selection strength are chosen at the beginning of a run. Within that setup, artificial organisms reproduce and mutate. Fitter organisms reproduce, while other organisms die. This supplies an independent mechanism comparator for Kaal's classification. Selection can operate inside a computational experiment even when the environment and its parameters were provisioned before the run.\n\nThe relationship is a qualification. The paper studies simulated bacteria, not Kaal's agents or E2B execution. It does not inspect Kaal's evidence, verify the host environment, or establish that the reported selection rule executed. It also treats investigator-chosen fitness and selection parameters, not every form of endogenous selection. The external source supports the distinction between experimental provisioning and within-experiment selection. Historical occurrence in Kaal's experiment remains bound to Kaal's execution evidence.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-14-S15-S11"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-028"
   },
   "sha256": "a073187830dc2b77dbff0c1de2142da677bcb86aa0a1ea117d418cd877bb5dfb",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed open-access BMC Bioinformatics article with DOI-bound identity and exact full-text proposition evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-360",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-360",
   "text": "The 0.0266 slope difference is an effect estimate. It is not, by itself, a test of whether the selection and random-survival trajectories differ.\n\nPaternoster, Brame, Mazerolle, and Piquero examine direct comparisons between regression slopes. They explain that the difference has its own sampling distribution and standard error. An incorrect standard-error estimator can bias the test toward rejecting equality. This supports Kaal's classification of the 0.0266 value as descriptive. A positive numerical difference may summarize the observed trajectories, but confirmatory inference requires uncertainty for the difference itself.\n\nThe correspondence is limited. The external paper addresses independent samples in criminological regressions. Kaal's claim concerns an interim registered agent experiment. The public claim does not establish here whether the two slope estimates are independent, paired, or covariance-adjusted. The source neither recalculates the 0.0266 value nor validates the data, estimator, or selection mechanism. The value may describe this checkpoint. It cannot carry confirmatory weight without a comparison matched to the experimental dependence structure.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-9125.1998.tb01268.x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-026"
   },
   "sha256": "2b5fa71ba20bbd2861d6f0285cb62220cb0a1c6357c5b3edb0594bd357c31160",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete Crossref-deposited abstract of a peer-reviewed Criminology article with DOI-bound author, venue, date, and proposition evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-359",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-359",
   "text": "Greenland and coauthors provide an interpretation boundary for the reported interval. Their framework treats an interval as a model-dependent summary of effect sizes that remain more compatible with the data than values outside the range. Kaal reports an aggregate selection slope of 0.0685 and an uncertainty interval from -0.0568 to 0.2020. The range spans negative, null, and positive slopes. It therefore does not fix the direction of the selection trajectory at the interim checkpoint.\n\nThe qualification is methodological. Greenland and coauthors analyze confidence intervals and the assumptions that support them. They do not identify Kaal's interval construction, recalculate the slope, inspect the registered estimator, or audit the underlying E2B record. Kaal's source separately classifies the interim estimates as inconclusive. The numerical result remains source-bound.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10654-016-0149-3"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-024"
   },
   "sha256": "85806f3c5883e08545040e8044e5a230e38d2dddf1403fbf07d8759d4bb2c79d",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public peer-reviewed article in Europe PMC JATS XML with concordant Crossref and Europe PMC identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-358",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-358",
   "text": "Pizzi, Cepellotti, Sabatini, Marzari, and Kozinsky provide an independent implementation precedent for separating evidentiary completeness from public disclosure. AiiDA records calculations and data as nodes joined by directional, labeled links that preserve the causal chain from input data through intermediate calculations to final results. The system stores queryable node attributes and permits users to inspect the history of a computational result. Its sharing design creates a separate boundary. Each user or group may operate a local instance in which data and calculations remain private. A user may then export only a portion of the database to another instance while retaining the remaining private portion.\n\nThe relationship is a qualification. The distinction matters. AiiDA shows that detailed causal provenance and selective disclosure can coexist in one implemented system. It does not record a population of autonomous agents or establish Kaal's generation, lineage, fitness, survival, parentage, reputation, and adjudication fields. The paper also does not validate Kaal's execution record or disclose his private apparatus. This use of AiiDA is limited to its privacy and partial-export mechanism. The previously published AiiDA comparison addressed the distinct proposition that a directed graph can preserve an inspectable computational history. Evidentiary integrity does not require universal access to the system that produces the evidence.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.commatsci.2015.09.013"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-023"
   },
   "sha256": "11d746aa35c7da6085a55b27ffbf69cf2ce2557daaf157633ebb9fce70a0d4ce",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 30-page arXiv v2 manuscript of a peer-reviewed journal article with exact PDF SHA-256, page-bound passages, arXiv identity, and Crossref identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-357",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-357",
   "text": "Jaderberg, Dalibard, Osindero, Czarnecki, and their coauthors provide an independent operational comparator for a selection-induced lineage transition. The mechanism is concrete. Population Based Training evaluates concurrently trained agents and applies an exploit rule when a population member is ready. Under one reported rule, a lower-performing agent copies the weights and hyperparameters of a better-performing agent, while another rule directs an agent in the bottom fifth to copy from the top fifth. Training then continues from the inherited model state. The authors represent each executed exploit operation as a branch in the full phylogenetic tree because the parameters were copied. The transition is therefore an implemented change in model ancestry rather than a descriptive label.\n\nThe relationship is a qualification. The paper shows that a performance-based selection rule can produce a traceable computational lineage transition. It does not validate Kaal's registered E2B rule, fitness measure, parentage record, or execution receipts. Population Based Training also copies model weights and hyperparameters within an asynchronous optimizer. Kaal's lineage objects and selection conditions may differ. The external evidence establishes mechanism correspondence and operational feasibility. Historical occurrence in Kaal's experiment remains bound to the interim E2B record. A lineage transition is externally legible only when the selection event and inherited state are both preserved.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09846"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-022"
   },
   "sha256": "14b48c9b1fc7fdabbc8400308a3150b3c8cf5d7b043bc8b0aa7d53fcc7781915",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 21-page arXiv v2 primary paper with exact PDF SHA-256, page-bound passages, arXiv API identity, and DataCite identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-356",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-356",
   "text": "Zhang, Hu, Lu, Lange, and Clune provide an independent comparator for the claimed generation boundary. Their Darwin Gödel Machine begins with one coding agent and preserves discovered agents in an archive. At each iteration, the system selects a parent, uses the parent to produce a modified child, evaluates the child, and admits only agents that retain the capacity for further self-modification. The authors report that this cycle continued for 80 iterations. The archive permits an earlier result to seed later work even when the relevant lineage is not selected in every intervening iteration. This evidence establishes that an agentic computation can preserve lineage and continue activity across completed evolutionary cycles.\n\nThe relationship is a qualification. An iteration in the Darwin Godel Machine is not Kaal's registered generation. The archive also does not verify Kaal's runtime record, boundary completion, or continuity into a particular successor generation. Those propositions remain bound to Kaal's interim E2B evidence and its receipts. The external paper supplies a feasible operational comparator. It does not independently establish that the reported transition occurred in Kaal's experiment. The distinction matters because technical feasibility and historical occurrence are separate empirical propositions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22954"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-021"
   },
   "sha256": "02abb5c284c5a432141f27237b4860754bb452dcaad8efec698cadd86e68d677",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 72-page ICLR 2026 conference paper with exact PDF SHA-256, page-bound passages, arXiv API identity, and DataCite identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-355",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-355",
   "text": "State must persist before an evolutionary trajectory can be evaluated. Lalan, Ghosh, Kolsur, and Dutta implement a stateful multi-agent search for unit-test generation. A controller carries edge cases, coverage scores, mutation feedback, exception signals, and reward history across successive stages. Later stages inherit and refine earlier judgments. A critic scores candidates, while evolutionary selection preserves a population of high-performing elites. The reported system achieves higher coverage than stateless prompting baselines.\n\nThis evidence qualifies Kaal's E2B question. It shows that an artificial agent process can preserve evaluation-relevant state across evolutionary stages and condition later candidates on that history. The resulting search is selected and history dependent rather than a sequence of isolated samples.\n\nThe limit controls the inference. The paper evaluates unit-test generation, not Kaal's substrate or registered population. It compares stateful evolutionary search with stateless prompting, not with neutral drift under the same mutation, population, and compute conditions. Higher coverage therefore does not establish that selection, rather than accumulated state, reward shaping, or additional search, produced the trajectory. The source supports technical feasibility and the need for a drift control. It does not report the E2B result.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07147"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-020"
   },
   "sha256": "43f9ee40b1af9432815b580ee31feb42259803ff50d87e28f92c308b58cfd68a",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 24-page arXiv v1 preprint with exact PDF SHA-256, page-bound passages, arXiv API identity, and DataCite identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-354",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-354",
   "text": "Changed experimental bindings change the question being estimated. VanderWeele and Hernán analyze causal inference when one nominal treatment contains multiple versions. Their framework separates the treatment label from the version that determines the potential outcome. It permits version-specific comparisons and policy-level averages over assigned versions. Those quantities are not interchangeable. An overall average remains tied to the distribution and assignment policy for treatment versions.\n\nThis analysis qualifies Kaal's treatment of a broader comparison. A comparison that changes the agent, task, or model binding can be interpreted as changing the version of the experimental condition. It no longer estimates the strict effect associated with one preserved binding. It estimates a broader regime or policy contrast whose meaning depends on which versions enter each condition and how they are assigned. Reporting the result as a separate, partially matched estimand therefore preserves the causal question.\n\nThe correspondence is methodological. VanderWeele and Hernán study treatment versions in causal inference, not autonomous agents or Kaal's registered cohort. Their framework does not define Kaal's binding keys or validate his empirical design. It supports the narrower classification: once binding fields change, the resulting comparison requires a separate estimand and an explicit account of the versions included.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4219328/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-019"
   },
   "sha256": "2fa80f3ca569a33a1f6f59f9bb84c496297fe252d1c9ab2951aeed52539e947b",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 20-page peer-reviewed article indexed through PMC with concordant PubMed, Crossref, Europe PMC, and author-publication identity receipts",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-353",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-353",
   "text": "Matched comparisons require more than common outcome labels. Fu and colleagues ask whether reorganizing a single-agent workflow into a multi-agent workflow changes accuracy and cost. Their BenchAgent design holds the base model, benchmark loader, tool interface, answer contract, evaluator, and accounting substrate constant. It also records agent identifiers and stage-level traces under one execution system. The measured difference can therefore be assigned more narrowly to workflow organization.\n\nThis evidence qualifies Kaal's strict matched-analysis requirement. The source binds the compared workflows to the same base model and benchmark inputs. It also keeps the execution and evaluation surfaces common and preserves the identifiers needed to reconstruct agent activity. These controls prevent a comparison from treating changes in model, task delivery, tool access, or logging as if they were effects of agent organization.\n\nThe qualification remains limited. BenchAgent compares a single-agent anchor with workflows that necessarily contain different numbers and roles of agents. It does not preserve the identity of one agent across every condition, reproduce Kaal's registered analysis, or test his cohort. The source supports the narrower methodological proposition: an agent-workflow comparison becomes interpretable only when task, model, execution, and attribution fields remain bound across conditions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.05670v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-018"
   },
   "sha256": "d5467640ec5d969287092da2a1684cbaecbaa51a54719daff2df862035fbed71",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 33-page arXiv preprint under review with concordant arXiv API, abstract-page, and PDF identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-352",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-352",
   "text": "Reputation links current conduct to future work. Benson, Sojourner, and Umyarov study Amazon Mechanical Turk, where employers may keep completed work without paying and workers cannot rely on ordinary contract enforcement. Their model treats an employer's payment history as a public relational contract. The threat of losing future workers discourages opportunism. Their field experiments then show that employers with good reputations attract workers more quickly and at a larger scale without reducing work quality. Workers for good-reputation employers also earn 40 percent higher effective wages because tasks finish faster and rejection is less likely.\n\nThis evidence qualifies Kaal's intertemporal reputation condition. A visible history changes expected access to future work and the effective price of participation. In that bounded setting, reputation is not a descriptive label. It supplies the link through which past conduct affects future labor flows, payment expectations, and the scale of economic activity that the market can support.\n\nThe correspondence does not validate Kaal's NCLF plus CELM cell. The source studies human gig workers, employer payment, and a third-party rating system. It does not examine autonomous agents, endogenous creation of new work categories, Kaal's pricing architecture, or the stability of his framework. It supports only the narrower mechanism: when work is weakly contractible, an intertemporal reputation signal can discipline conduct and materially affect future work allocation and effective compensation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2019.3303"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-017"
   },
   "sha256": "699d246da0fcc9aa1e2815937c4d85ce1e263b6d92fecf6105cd29b895f9d5fa",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed open-access journal article with a complete public 25-page publisher PDF and concordant Crossref and OpenAlex records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-351",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-351",
   "text": "Classification does not establish displacement. Reimers, Guo, and Li classify three institutionally distinct two-sided markets as functional alternatives to markets, hybrids, and hierarchies. They then separate that classification from a prediction about organizational change. A technology that enables a new form does not establish that the new form will replace an existing one. The inference requires conditions and trade-offs that can decide the outcome in a concrete situation.\n\nThis distinction independently extends Kaal's inference boundary. Classifying two institutional arrangements identifies their differences. It does not show that one arrangement universally displaces the other. A displacement proposition requires additional evidence about the conditions under which the relevant trade-offs favor a shift.\n\nThe correspondence remains narrow. Reimers, Guo, and Li study two-sided markets and traditional forms of economic organization. They do not examine Kaal's computational-abundance comparison or test either arrangement. Their analysis does not establish comparative accuracy, legitimacy, cost, or robustness. It supports the classification boundary and the need for conditional evidence before any displacement claim.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s12525-018-0319-0"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-016"
   },
   "sha256": "fe71d11409f64317c99c64ba847197bc65c9af1095c05170b1f2e454fc4f1b6e",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed CC BY journal article with a complete public 19-page publisher PDF and concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-350",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-350",
   "text": "Evaluative authority has an institutional location. Noorian and Ulieru classify centralized reputation systems by a central database and authority that receive transaction reports, aggregate ratings, and calculate public scores. Their decentralized category removes that location. Each agent stores ratings and uses a tailored computation engine to calculate and validate the trustworthiness of a potential partner.\n\nThis classification independently extends Kaal's distinction. A centralized reward model and cohort-governed reputation do not merely apply different techniques to the same evaluator. They allocate the collection, computation, and validation of evaluative information to different actors.\n\nThe correspondence is limited. Noorian and Ulieru study trust and reputation systems in online communities and open multi-agent systems. They do not analyze reward modeling, Kaal's cohort design, deliberation, or reputation-bearing consequences. Their framework supports the institutional allocation claim. It does not establish the comparative accuracy, legitimacy, or resistance to manipulation of either design in Kaal's setting.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4067/s0718-18762010000200007"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-015"
   },
   "sha256": "280c1dcb245416c0d7c9fb94727fc402090c76879ccead420972e3317ec6296a",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal article with a complete public 21-page PDF and concordant Crossref and OpenAlex records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-349",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-349",
   "text": "Baker and Hubbard make firm boundaries observable as a response to verification technology. Using detailed trucking data, they examine whether on-board computers changed the contractibility of driver actions and, through that change, truck ownership. Adoption reduced driver ownership, especially for long hauls. It moved production toward more integrated asset ownership. The result supports Kaal's proposition that a firm's boundary can vary with the technology used to verify performance. It also qualifies it sharply: better verification did not make the firm disappear. In this setting, it reduced independent contracting and produced larger firms.\n\nThe comparison remains bounded. On-board computers monitored truck drivers in one industry. They did not test autonomous agents, computational abundance, or Kaal's residual verification architecture, and the results do not establish that every improvement in verification expands firm boundaries. Baker and Hubbard show instead that direction depends on how contractibility alters incentive and bargaining costs. Their evidence supports the empirical program. It does not establish a universal boundary rule.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1162/0033553042476152"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-014"
   },
   "sha256": "4bef6ba71bbb4c2a7612fbabc66231cea4d567ea551f9b156d4f79721e4f5e43",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Quarterly Journal of Economics article with a complete NBER author manuscript and concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-348",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-348",
   "text": "Douglas Diamond studies a financial intermediary. His model treats monitoring information as an input that resolves incentive problems between borrowers and lenders. Delegation creates an additional cost because lenders must motivate the intermediary to monitor. Diversification within the intermediary reduces that cost, even under risk neutrality. Scale matters. Monitoring can therefore become economically viable when institutional scale changes the cost of producing credible oversight, which qualifies Kaal's classification of monitoring as a production-economics question.\n\nThe correspondence is bounded. Diamond studies lenders, borrowers, and financial intermediaries rather than autonomous agents. He does not analyze Kaal's validation architecture or any specific funding mechanism. The model does not show that monitoring supports itself at every scale. It identifies conditions under which delegation reduces monitoring and incentive costs. Kaal's production-economics question remains empirical for the reference mechanism.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2307/2297430"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-013"
   },
   "sha256": "a36788729410a04f5fbde934d15e4534445c300a137f0692698c74088fe363ee",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Review of Economic Studies article with a complete OUP-maintained RePEc abstract and concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-347",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-347",
   "text": "Budish gives economic content to Kaal's distinction between internal stake accounting and external resource finance. His zero-profit condition treats trust support as costly work, stake, storage, or memory and makes the quantity supplied a function of protocol compensation. The resulting equilibrium constraint requires recurring payment for trust support to be large relative to the benefits of attack. This supports Kaal's condition that internal accounting alone does not finance external verification resources. It does not establish that Kaal's reference mechanism lacks an external financing channel, measure its infrastructure costs, or extend beyond permissionless consensus.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjae033"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-012"
   },
   "sha256": "1782b9d0c00e153b7901b5ef14021a7ee5920392d2778b7764dd9365e1f95149",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed QJE article with a complete publisher HTML, complete precursor PDF, and concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-346",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-346",
   "text": "Deb, Sugaya, and Wolitzky sharply bound Kaal's use of the anonymous-random-matching Folk Theorem. They prove a Folk Theorem for discounted repeated games with anonymous random matching, allowing nonuniform matching and asymmetric payoffs, but retain full dimensionality as a stage-game condition. Their result supports treating the theorem as a conditional benchmark within that model. It does not provide a general performance floor for an implemented reputation mechanism, predict which equilibrium will occur, or establish any outcome in Kaal's reference system.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3982/ecta16680"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-011"
   },
   "sha256": "180140dadac9120b1cc8a407f2ddc1da270eb7c8f0375af5bbd9de272ca34652",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Econometrica article with concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity and a complete DOI-bound abstract",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-345",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-345",
   "text": "Imai, Keele, Tingley, and Yamamoto sharpen Kaal's attribution rule. They define a causal mechanism through a mediator and show that identifying the mechanism requires explicit assumptions, mediation analysis, sensitivity analysis, or research designs that manipulate the mediator. A randomized treatment can identify an overall effect without identifying how that effect arose. The source therefore supports Kaal's demand for mechanism-specific evidence. It also narrows it. Randomizing treatment and the intermediate variable is not automatically sufficient, and ablation is not examined. Attribution remains conditional on the mediator, estimand, design, and identification assumptions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003055411000414"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-010"
   },
   "sha256": "a1d8feb7d84b6ee74649c1685a818aecaff31e40a343b8959176c32a673e0a89",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 25-page peer-reviewed author-hosted article with concordant Crossref DOI, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-344",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-344",
   "text": "Squires and Uhler qualify Kaal's attribution boundary. They show that observational data can identify an equivalence class of causal graphs rather than a unique graph. Graphs within the class encode the same conditional independence relations even when their edge directions differ. Interventional data can refine the class. The result supports the narrower proposition that a downstream observable does not, by itself, identify the architecture that generated it. The limitation is equally important. The source concerns causal directed acyclic graphs under formal assumptions, not Kaal's institutional architecture or its registered metrics. It does not establish non-identifiability when stronger distributional assumptions, experiments, or domain restrictions identify a unique structure.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10208-022-09581-9"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-009"
   },
   "sha256": "5bea4d6a58a69881467946601daa0c99aba07d5e0e2a9ed7daca5dbc37994638",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 35-page author manuscript and complete Europe PMC journal XML with concordant Crossref DOI, OpenAlex, PubMed, and Semantic Scholar identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-343",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-343",
   "text": "Davani, Díaz, and Prabhakaran qualify Kaal's treatment of cohort judgment as an estimator rather than a truth oracle. Across seven binary classification tasks, they show that majority voting can erase systematic annotator disagreement. Their multi-annotator model matches or improves predictive performance and produces uncertainty estimates that better track disagreement. This supports the narrower proposition that aggregation leaves measurable uncertainty and that a naive aggregate can conceal structure in the judgments it combines. The limitation is material. Their tasks concern subjective language annotation, not Kaal's verification cohort. The source does not validate Kaal's variance calculation, reproduce Part VI.C, or show that every form of verification preserves information asymmetry.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00449"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-008"
   },
   "sha256": "7f8ebc29556d0f74de3e2c215ecdd7bbb4e83ff2a0c2bfcf640f20024d7f57f4",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 19-page peer-reviewed TACL article with concordant ACL Anthology, Crossref DOI, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-342",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-342",
   "text": "Li and Yu qualify Kaal's shift from persistent asymmetry to a measurable residual. Under the Dawid-Skene crowdsourcing model, they derive finite-sample exponential bounds for aggregation error and identify task assignment, worker reliability, and normalized score gaps as conditions on those bounds. Their result supports the narrower proposition that aggregate judgment error can be measured and controlled by design. The limitation is decisive. The bound is conditional on a specified labeling model and aggregation rule. It does not show that information asymmetry generally contracts, test Kaal's reputation-weighted pool, or establish that residual error will shrink under unmodeled dependence or strategic behavior.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1411.4086"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-007"
   },
   "sha256": "891633d6ff96ef26cdfee5e8097cecb8c93abf35d379a22e8c14966d4a5287c8",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 28-page arXiv v1 PDF with concordant arXiv, DataCite DOI, title, author, date, and OpenAlex identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-341",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-341",
   "text": "Dogonowski identifies a corresponding institutional constraint in AI deployment. Computational capability scales faster than an organization's capacity to validate AI-mediated outputs. The model therefore treats verification, not generation, as the binding constraint on safe deployment and shows how costly verification can produce a stable zero-verification equilibrium. This independently extends Kaal's proposition that abundant individual computation can relocate boundedness to institutional verification capacity. The evidence is limited to a posted organizational model. It does not establish that computation exceeds every decision requirement, or that aggregation and collective action are the only remaining constraints.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6942138"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-006"
   },
   "sha256": "b4fae02be121ec151bd5f35080b3760bf042a61344b872fa2b8212d8f438b371",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete Crossref-deposited SSRN abstract with concordant Crossref DOI, title, author, deposited date, and OpenAlex record. SSRN PDF access returned HTTP 403",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-340",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-340",
   "text": "Qiao and colleagues identify a corresponding displacement in automated science. Large language models now produce research ideas at unprecedented scale, while evaluation still depends on scarce expert judgment. This independently extends Kaal's proposition that computationally cheap ideation moves the binding constraint toward determining which candidates are sound, feasible, and significant. The evidence is limited to scientific research ideas. It does not establish that ideation has zero marginal cost, that evaluation is the only remaining constraint, or that InnoEval resolves safety and alignment.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.14367"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-005"
   },
   "sha256": "d56703abcc3693c5f0756137df58f4826524b8c3756799b2a57569649975fab7",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete accepted ICML 2026 paper with concordant arXiv v2, DBLP, repository, author list, venue stamp, and page-bound proposition text",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-339",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-339",
   "text": "Kokkodis and Ipeirotis show that reputation design affects prediction. Their model replaces an undifferentiated average of prior feedback with category-specific histories and cross-category weights. On held-out oDesk transactions, this representation reduces prediction error relative to aggregate-history baselines. The result supports testing whether a structured reputation signal contains information for future task performance that a simpler outcome-history summary omits.\n\nThe comparison also narrows Kaal's H2. The external model constructs reputation from prior employer ratings. It does not compare an independently generated reputation measure with the full observed outcome history. It therefore does not establish that reputation adds information beyond outcomes as such. The source studies human online labor markets, not multi-model agents or Kaal's registered Stage 4 design. Its contribution is methodological. H2 must distinguish incremental information in reputation from predictive gains created by transforming the same outcome history.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/2433396.2433450"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-004"
   },
   "sha256": "18e7e84eb238939101407a905e4269e7827d6803977a6de2edf7d311a4da64ee",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public refereed WSDM 2013 conference paper with concordant Crossref, DBLP, author page, DOI, authorship, venue, pages, and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-338",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-338",
   "text": "Greifer and Stuart clarify what a matched comparison can establish. Matching does not supply a generic treatment effect. It defines an effect for a target population, and different matching procedures can change that population. The authors therefore require the estimand and statistical method to be aligned before interpreting an outcome difference. This qualification supports Kaal's formulation of H1 as a question about output quality under a properly matched estimand. It does not establish that the joint substrate improves output quality. The source studies observational treatment comparisons, not multi-model agents or Kaal's registered Stage 4 design. Its contribution is methodological. A difference in outcomes is interpretable only for the population and causal assumptions encoded by the chosen estimand.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2106.10577"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-003"
   },
   "sha256": "a4f0d754eb853048bd720e0d82b9e7c382b3880af6a9566b030475485d622793",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public versioned arXiv preprint PDF with concordant DataCite DOI, author, title, and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-337",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-337",
   "text": "Fanelli extends Kaal's identification limit for null evidence. A null or negative result can rule out a tested hypothesis under the stated design. It does not thereby select a unique explanation. Fanelli models the knowledge value of a negative result as declining with the number of hypotheses, methods, explanations, or outcomes that remain untested. Real experiments retain uncertainty because several alternative hypotheses can remain compatible with the same outcome. The problem persists even with greater statistical power when auxiliary assumptions remain uncontrolled.\n\nThe correspondence is narrow. Fanelli supplies a general meta-scientific account of negative-result informativeness. The article does not test Kaal's computational economy, reconstruct his empirical condition, or identify the mechanism behind any particular null. It supports the distinction between disciplining a condition and establishing a mechanism. A null can remove or weaken one candidate explanation. Mechanism identification requires additional designs that discriminate among the remaining alternatives.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.181055"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-002"
   },
   "sha256": "627a4146bb01deba2f4e3002e87ec88dabbb5efdab953b6e91d31d0233a97c03",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed public Royal Society Open Science article in Europe PMC JATS XML with concordant DOI, author, venue, and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-336",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-336",
   "text": "Code establishes a model's implementation. Economic effects require separate evidence. Gräbner's validation framework explains the distinction. Model verification tests whether a computational model does what its designer intended. Validation asks whether the model reasonably represents its target and assesses the model's relationship to reality. His framework also requires an explicit key between model properties and target-system properties. Validation becomes necessary when the model is used to make informed statements about the real world.\n\nThis independently qualifies Kaal's claim. Formal objects and source code can make a mechanism explicit and testable. They do not show that the named economic effect occurred. That inference requires observable target properties and an appropriate validation design.\n\nThe source concerns computational models generally. The analysis does not evaluate Kaal's framework, observables, code, or evidence. It supplies a methodological boundary. No economic effect follows from that boundary.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.18564/jasss.3772"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261481-001"
   },
   "sha256": "629b64b4cefefbbf0232175a0124dd1f2f8b06b7f281c9a62bee73e27f53555f",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed 26-page version-of-record PDF with concordant journal, Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identities",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-335",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-335",
   "text": "Kapoor and coauthors qualify Kaal's external-validity limit. They show that agent benchmark accuracy may not translate to real-world performance when benchmarks permit shortcuts or omit appropriate holdouts. They also identify distribution shifts that benchmark designers cannot fully model and recommend comparing benchmark results with corresponding real-world tasks. This supports Kaal's decision to confine the Article's findings to its disclosed research setting. It does not establish how the Agentic Reputation Substrate performs at production scale. The source does not test Kaal's cohort, reputation treatment, deliberation protocol, agency-cost measures, or deployment environment. Production effects may therefore persist, amplify, or invert. The present evidence does not decide among those outcomes.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01502v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-040"
   },
   "sha256": "fe148746b5c89bab9975f07c52e083bade363f037565cab861792bf8d2be218f",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 33-page arXiv v1 paper with concordant arXiv metadata and OpenAlex identity",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-334",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-334",
   "text": "Zhu and coauthors qualify Kaal's interpretation of deliberation as a composition intervention. In their controlled model, homogeneous agents with unweighted updates preserve expected correctness during debate. Diversity-aware initialization improves the prior probability that a correct hypothesis is present, but does not change the subsequent update dynamics. Confidence-weighted debate changes how answers are aggregated. The result supports a distinction between the composition and protocol of a debating group and the underlying intelligence of any one model. It does not establish Kaal's empirical conclusion. The source uses reasoning benchmarks and a Dirichlet-categorical model. It does not test monitoring, Kaal's controlled cohort, the Agentic Reputation Substrate, campaign units, or the observed composition of error. Kaal's claim remains limited to the completed treatment under the registered study conditions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921v3"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-038"
   },
   "sha256": "7a37ac14f07806927d2b1c20cf524a51ea02f498005e5870aa930422e637c934",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public arXiv v3 PDF with concordant arXiv Atom metadata and public abstract page",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-333",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-333",
   "text": "Thibault and coauthors provide a methodological qualification to the registered null result. Their Explore and Confirm Analysis Workflow permits exploration on a data subset, but requires the resulting analysis script to be registered before access to the full dataset. The design preserves a confirmatory test on data not used to develop the analysis. That mechanism corresponds to Kaal's separation of the discovery run from confirmation. It does not establish that net discrimination was absent. It also does not recalculate the reported estimate or interval, examine Kaal's campaign units, or audit adherence to the preregistration. The external source supports the discovery-to-confirmation design logic only. Kaal's numerical result remains source-bound to the Article.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.230568"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-037"
   },
   "sha256": "450000a777e80731d07d9f4d1a3b9970b8c1dcb2e0cbf923bf12a8e55feea081",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public Royal Society Open Science article in Europe PMC JATS XML with concordant Crossref and Europe PMC identity records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-332",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-332",
   "text": "IntHout, Ioannidis, Rovers, and Goeman qualify the inference from a pooled confidence interval. Under between-study heterogeneity, a pooled 95 percent confidence interval may remain wholly on one side of zero while a prediction interval reaches both sides. Their review found that 72.4 percent of statistically significant Cochrane meta-analyses with observed heterogeneity had prediction intervals that included the null. This result does not dispute the registered calculation or the negative direction in each campaign unit. It shows that pooled significance and directional consistency do not alone establish how the effect will generalize to another exchangeable unit. That stronger claim requires a heterogeneity-aware prediction analysis. The paper does not test Kaal's cohort, hypotheses, estimator, or campaign units.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2015-010247"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-036"
   },
   "sha256": "27e0dd506806c5d2f46cdba6d0336126ea8fb7c6557f63d116188511a2cfd66f",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public BMJ Open article in Europe PMC JATS XML with a concordant Crossref record and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-331",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-331",
   "text": "The inference remains bounded. Pawel and coauthors show that a non-significant estimate does not by itself establish the absence of an effect. The reported interval crosses zero and remains compatible with effects from -0.0502 to +0.0985. The result therefore leaves direction unresolved under the reported design. It does not show that the effect equals zero. Evidence for absence requires a method designed for that question, such as equivalence testing or a Bayes factor. This qualification does not recalculate Kaal's estimate, identify an equivalence margin, or test the registered endpoint, the multi-model cohort, or the Agentic Reputation Substrate. It limits the interpretation to what the estimate and confidence interval can support.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.92311"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-035"
   },
   "sha256": "4f06d39a4fb9432cd1f9c6717bf9003f9eb10b43f48341a87704d1cbcc65aa22",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public eLife article in Europe PMC JATS XML with a concordant Crossref record, an official eLife article page, and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-330",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-330",
   "text": "Hennessy and Strebulaev sharpen the inference boundary. Their model treats government policy as an independent stochastic process, which allows them to abstract from endogeneity bias. Yet they show that random assignment alone does not make measured treatment responses equal causal effects in dynamic economies. Additional identifying assumptions remain necessary. This qualification supports holding work assignment fixed when the objective is to separate an institution's effect on outcomes from its effect on work selection. It does not establish that the matched condition identifies every causal effect. The paper studies dynamic policy environments, not multi-model agents, validation pools, or Kaal's registered cohort. Its contribution is narrower: controlling assignment can remove one selection channel without resolving every source of causal bias.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w20978"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-034"
   },
   "sha256": "f2551c1d80f4ae1f7fd6129575d81db142ee0289586059f59f0a1a0ea56bd9d1",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete 47-page NBER working paper with a concordant Crossref record, an official NBER landing page, and exact proposition-bearing passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-329",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-329",
   "text": "Paleyes, Urma, and Lawrence distinguish academic machine learning from deployment in a real-world production system. Their survey identifies challenges at every stage of the deployment workflow. This supports treating a proposed production system as a separate evidentiary object. It does not validate Kaal's registered forward program, the Agentic Reputation Substrate, or any production implementation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3533378"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-033"
   },
   "sha256": "7f883bdee594f2c6852aefbb2fc9a793a803edf89516e9e94cd50ccd0b442bb9",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed author manuscript from arXiv with a concordant Crossref journal record and exact page 1 passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-328",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-328",
   "text": "Scientific evidence is apparatus-bound. Kanewala and Bieman's systematic review explains why. The review reports that scientific software generates evidence for research publications, while also finding that testing is often limited to the initial scientific problem addressed by the code. Reliability on a different problem therefore cannot be guaranteed. This supports treating the completed E1 and E2a evidence as evidence about the historical research apparatus that produced it.\n\nThe relationship is a qualification. The review does not examine Kaal's apparatus, autonomous agents, either experiment, or the current reference implementation, and it cannot establish which code, models, or institutional conditions produced Kaal's results. Those facts remain source-bound to Kaal's Article. The external evidence instead supplies the methodological reason for keeping the completed evidence tied to its tested apparatus. A later or modified system requires separate verification before the earlier results can be transferred to it.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2014.05.006"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-031"
   },
   "sha256": "1da44f891c35d9859d13030427a05434cb096b7c8aff997d85f2ffbfbdaed702",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed scholarly full text from PMC BioC XML with concordant Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identities",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-327",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-327",
   "text": "Lu, Yuan, and McLeod qualify Kaal's narrow account of the promoted deliberation hypotheses. Their meta-analysis covers 65 hidden-profile studies, 101 independent effects, and 3,189 groups. Hidden-profile groups discussed substantially more common than unique information and were eight times less likely to find the solution than full-information groups. Information coverage and discussion focus were positively related to decision quality, but group size, information load, task demonstrability, and hidden-profile strength moderated the results. The evidence supports a limited inference: deliberation can change which information reaches the group without making the pool smarter in net. The study concerns human groups and hidden-profile tasks. It does not test autonomous agents, Kaal's cohort, or either registered hypothesis.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1177/1088868311417243"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-030"
   },
   "sha256": "d5f17972dc13a1f4322b908580a2f8befca6f1f1404b04227f3feeaa99b3bda8",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete Crossref registered abstract with concordant OpenAlex and Semantic Scholar identities and read-only review of the official publisher abstract page",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-326",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-326",
   "text": "Markou and Chan extend Kaal's prediction that deliberation should reduce unanimity. They analyze verbatim transcripts from Food and Drug Administration advisory committee meetings before and after a change from sequential to simultaneous voting. Unanimous votes became less likely. The increased diversity of information surfaced during prevote discussions mediated most of that reduction. Members also asked one another more questions and shared speaking time more equally. This evidence supports the narrower mechanism that discussion can expose individually held information before votes bind. The institutional change altered voting rules and discussion incentives together. It does not isolate deliberation as the sole treatment, identify articulated disagreement as the only channel, test autonomous agents, or measure Kaal's cascade construct. The registered prediction therefore remains unverified in Kaal's cohort.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2023.03649"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-029"
   },
   "sha256": "89112654289d670310a6a644dbe76b5d282702e391ceec9cf77ea820c0dfad22",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete Crossref registered abstract bound through DOI, title, authors, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, record hash, and exact abstract passages; complete official publisher HTML reviewed through live read-only retrieval but direct byte capture returned HTTP 403",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-325",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-325",
   "text": "Piehlmaier qualifies Kaal's prediction that deliberation should reduce over-approval. His preregistered experiments distinguish reflective exchange from validation. Joint financial decision-making reduced overconfidence when the interaction made unknown aspects of the decision salient. A simple stamp of approval without reflection did not provide the same corrective mechanism. This supports a narrower causal rationale. An exchange can expose missing information before a decision hardens. The study does not examine agentic validator pools, submission approval, reputation-bearing incentives, profit from approval, or Kaal's registered cohort. It measures investor overconfidence in controlled financial-decision settings. The predicted effect on false approvals therefore remains unverified until the registered study tests it directly.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://oup.silverchair-cdn.com/article-minimal/6873882"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-028"
   },
   "sha256": "f5407a3011f32dee7cd6ca2295692b7c74bc6b12425e21e13528398409104127",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete official open-access Oxford Academic article HTML bound through DOI, title, author, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, full-text hash, and section-level exact evidence locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-324",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-324",
   "text": "Hunt, Stringer, and Polagye qualify Kaal's fixed-conditions design. Their turbine experiment held confinement, Froude number, Reynolds number, and other non-dimensional parameters constant while varying aspect ratio. When they relaxed the Froude-number control, the resulting efficiency increase could have been misattributed to aspect ratio. This supports the causal rationale for fixing treatment-relevant conditions. The correspondence remains methodological. The source studies a hydraulic turbine, not agentic validators or a multi-model cohort. It does not verify that Kaal held every relevant condition fixed. It also shows that identifying every relevant control is difficult and that an omitted condition can change the inference.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.07618"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-027"
   },
   "sha256": "f6d49b7f775c889564d152a90011d1036a420c67411723adffb7575b6b007002",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 20-page author manuscript bound through arXiv ID, journal DOI, title, three authors, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PDF hash, extracted text, and page-level evidence locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-323",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-323",
   "text": "Structured assessment changes a decision process only if the assessment arrives before judgment. That sequence matters. Harrasse, Bandi, and Bandi route paired LLM evaluations through advocates, a criteria-based judge, and an independent jury that renders the final verdict. D3 compares that deliberative architecture with a single judge that selects directly. On MT-Bench, D3-MORE reports 85.1 percent accuracy, 12.6 percentage points above the single-judge baseline. This result qualifies Kaal's treatment-control design. It supports separating an assessment-exchange treatment from a no-exchange adjudication baseline. Yet the study does not establish a matched control with identical token budgets or Kaal's validator incentives, binding rules, and reputation consequences. The correspondence is limited to the comparative design and its measured evaluation setting. Deliberation has evidentiary value only when the baseline preserves the decision it replaces.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.eacl-long.392/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-026"
   },
   "sha256": "3311234caf6a97e23137a22db145e4e149f54cd594a88ffbfc74630f455f10e7",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public EACL manuscript bound through ACL Anthology ID, DOI, title, three authors, conference record, PDF hash, HTML identity, extracted text, comparative baseline, reported result, and printed-page locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-322",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-322",
   "text": "Majority pressure is an information channel, not a substitute for independent judgment. Wu, Li, and Li begin their controlled LLM debate with independent proposals. They then find that agents facing an incorrect majority are less able to correct the group, particularly when the agents are weaker. This result qualifies Kaal's information-isolating validation claim. It independently supports preserving an initial judgment before a participant sees a running tally or peer argument. It does not test Kaal's staged procedure, reputation-bearing consequences, or cohort-mediated accountability. The correspondence is therefore limited to the cascade mechanism. Independence must be preserved before deliberation can improve collective judgment.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07784"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-025"
   },
   "sha256": "2a409e3cedb67534b6c2160850bb34b715a2c1b74178259bb57f85a9f03a6762",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public arXiv v1 manuscript bound through title, three authors, category, date, PDF hash, HTML identity, extracted text, controlled study design, and printed-page locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-321",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-321",
   "text": "Lou et al. provide a narrow operational analogue to Kaal's standing mechanism. Their DRF framework raises an LLM agent's reputation when its peer-derived task score meets the task threshold and lowers reputation when the score falls below it. The source supports performance-contingent gains and losses in standing. It does not establish independent or cryptographic verification, and it tests a simulated rating network rather than Kaal's substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05764"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-024"
   },
   "sha256": "02f76d3acbb76aa4bfbd0cc90cce88a4020628e10588936ad171e07d1820ce2f",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public arXiv v1 manuscript bound through title, seven authors, category, date, PDF hash, HTML identity, extracted text, section, equations, and printed-page locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-320",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-320",
   "text": "Architecture comes first. Touloupou and Kapassa supply an independent specification for Kaal's definitional claim. Their systematic survey treats agent registries as a first coordination layer through which agents announce capabilities and discover counterparties without a centralized directory. Separately, the authors classify reputation accumulated from past behavior as a longer-horizon coordination substrate because non-transferability prevents purchase and ties standing to actual agent history rather than financial position.\n\nThis extends Kaal's substrate definition. Persistent reputation is not merely descriptive metadata. In the surveyed architecture, it informs counterparty discovery, task-market verification, and decentralized quality filtering. Yet the source separates identity, registries, task markets, settlement, and reputation. That distinction matters. It supports a coordination architecture organized around persistent, non-transferable reputation, but it does not establish that reputation alone constitutes the entire substrate.\n\nThe article synthesizes 177 peer-reviewed publications and 14 system-documentation sources. Its coordination ratings are qualitative assessments, not universal empirical findings. The survey does not test Kaal's controlled cohort, evaluate his reference implementation, or establish performance, security, and resistance to identity replacement. Its contribution is architectural. It independently connects autonomous-agent coordination to persistent identity, accumulated history, and non-transferable reputation.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/fi18070352"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-023"
   },
   "sha256": "c86b5e12f0d8410de78d21fb26076cb157f418ceac29213dcc63bd842df81d21",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete peer-reviewed Future Internet version of record bound through DOI, PDF and XML hashes, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, DOAJ, title, authors, date, CC BY license, and printed-page locators",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-319",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-319",
   "text": "Public timestamps establish a narrow temporal proposition. Birkholz, Fossati, and Riechert distinguish timestamps covering payload data from timestamps covering a cryptographic signature. A payload timestamp proves that the payload existed at an earlier point. It does not establish when a separate signature was created. The authors warn that treating it as such can cause validators to accept signatures produced after key revocation.\n\nThis distinction qualifies Kaal's claim directly. His public timestamp establishes that the attested artifact existed no later than the recorded time. Further conclusions require evidence bound to each later event. The timestamp alone does not prove execution, deployment, review, or any subsequent operational step.\n\nThe source concerns COSE messages and RFC 3161 tokens. It does not audit Kaal's artifact or provenance chain. It also does not determine whether any later event occurred. Its contribution is narrower. It defines the evidentiary scope of a timestamp and explains why extending that scope without event-specific proof creates a verification error.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9921.txt"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-022"
   },
   "sha256": "57be8d07b74b8c8cbc735e5defeb48fb63380abacfd954c156eb55703e4b32b9",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete official RFC Editor text bound through DOI, Crossref, title, authors, publication date, section locator, and official RFC status",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-318",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-318",
   "text": "Confirmatory analysis depends on when analytical discretion closes. Watt and Kennedy require every analysis decision capable of affecting confirmatory results to be made before data collection. They include statistical methods, evidence criteria, data transformations, and exclusion rules. This provides an external benchmark for Kaal's design. Kaal fixed directional hypotheses, a bootstrap estimator, exclusions, a randomization audit, a data-quality gate, and the confirmation rule before confirmatory computation.\n\nThe benchmark also narrows the claim. Watt and Kennedy use a stronger timing rule than Kaal's statement. Their standard closes analytical discretion before data collection, while Kaal's source-bound claim establishes closure before confirmatory computation. The article does not audit Kaal's preregistration, determine when his data became available, reproduce the bootstrap, or validate the stated gates. It therefore supports the importance of advance specification and the listed categories of decision. It does not prove that Kaal met the article's stronger prospective standard.\n\nThat limitation is substantive. A confirmatory label cannot substitute for a fixed analysis plan. Kaal's implementation and empirical results remain separate source-bound claims.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://public-pages-files-2025.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2015.00173/pdf"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-021"
   },
   "sha256": "9903a0e27c49b94c9084b91d67d76eb6f9fa9b65e216727334bb72a51dbdffc0",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public Frontiers in Psychology version of record bound through DOI, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, title, authors, and CC BY license",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-317",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-317",
   "text": "Niemeyer, Veri, Dryzek, and Bächtiger qualify Kaal's prediction about deliberation and unanimity. Their account does not treat opinion change, disagreement, or raw agreement as sufficient evidence of deliberative quality. Opinion change may arise from polarization, while high agreement may still fail to express deliberative reason. The authors instead ask whether a group has integrated relevant considerations within a shared representational framework. Agreement and disagreement matter only when judgments remain coherent with that framework.\n\nThe distinction gives Kaal's prediction a limited institutional meaning. A reduction in unanimity may be relevant when deliberation exposes considerations that earlier votes failed to integrate, but the resulting divergence is not itself the objective. It indicates a representational update only when participants respond to relevant reasons and their judgments become intersubjectively consistent. The source also cautions against treating consensus as the ideal. Its preferred outcome is higher-level agreement about which considerations matter and how they bear on action.\n\nThe scope is narrower than Kaal's study. Niemeyer and coauthors examine human deliberative forums, not LLM validators, and they measure deliberative reason rather than unanimity. They do not test Kaal's treatment or directional hypothesis. Their work supports only the rationale for separating deliberative quality from the amount of agreement. Kaal's empirical prediction remains distinct.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/6558F69855ADA8B15BF2EC2E5D403E71/S0003055423000023a.pdf/how-deliberation-happens-enabling-deliberative-reason.pdf"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-020"
   },
   "sha256": "beac29728b40b84c1f5da038094f51faa26a1a6689af6781ead8c009ea3367f9",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public Cambridge University Press version of record bound through DOI, Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, title, authors, and CC BY license",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-316",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-316",
   "text": "Çelen and Kariv extend Kaal's caution that unanimous verdicts do not, by themselves, identify an information cascade. Their experiment separates observed actions from underlying beliefs. A herd begins when participants take the same action. A cascade requires the stronger condition that they act without regard to private signals. The authors elicit signal cutoffs before each participant sees the private signal. This allows them to distinguish conformity in realized actions from decisions that no possible private signal would change. They find frequent herding and cascades, but not every observed herd is a cascade. The result supports Kaal's treatment of unanimity as a cascade-consistent quantity rather than a unique diagnostic.\n\nScope matters. The experiment uses sequential human decisions with continuous private signals and binary actions. It does not study validators, LLM agents, work-product approval, or Kaal's observed unanimity rate. It also does not test Holmström's shared-product effort mechanism. The source supplies direct evidence for the identification problem only: identical choices can coexist with continuing sensitivity to private information. Kaal's application to validator verdicts remains a separate empirical proposition.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://eml.berkeley.edu/~kariv/CK_II.pdf"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-019"
   },
   "sha256": "b2a05a5496d8ffe0f8637a515c10f76803b11d218e28b38e9b13f183f5a1172b",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public Berkeley author manuscript bound to the AER article through title, authors, DOI, Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-315",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-315",
   "text": "Bolton, Freixas, and Shapiro qualify Kaal's treatment of over-approval as moral hazard in the monitoring layer. Their model places the conflict inside the rating agency. The agency can understate credit risk to attract business, while issuers can purchase only favorable ratings. Rating inflation becomes more likely when reputation costs are lower or more investors accept ratings at face value. The monitor's verdict can therefore become more favorable even when the underlying risk remains unchanged. This correspondence supports Kaal's classification of over-approval as an incentive failure by the adjudicator rather than merely an error by the worker being evaluated.\n\nThe comparison is bounded. Credit rating agencies evaluate securities, not LLM-generated task outputs. The source models reported credit risk rather than approval against a known task-level ground truth. It does not test Kaal's validation pool or establish any measured over-approval rate. It supplies an independently developed mechanism for favorable verdicts to decouple from the quality the monitor is expected to assess. Kaal's empirical application remains a separate proposition.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w14712/w14712.pdf"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-018"
   },
   "sha256": "48381ce58fe70f678f68bdb25826417a6a0353f6664c807c357b6799556db5ec",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public NBER working paper bound to the journal article through title, authors, Crossref, and OpenAlex",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-314",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-314",
   "text": "Cronbach and Meehl qualify Kaal's external-anchoring rule. Their account of construct validity requires a nomological network that connects a test, its construct, other constructs, and observable quantities. The network must make contact with observations through public steps of inference. It must also be stated clearly enough for other researchers to accept or reject it. A failed prediction cannot be cured by redefining the construct after the result. A changed interpretation requires fresh data. This supports Kaal's insistence that the agency-cost battery rest on definitions and relations external to the Article's own conclusions. Otherwise, the battery risks becoming insulated from disconfirming evidence. The correspondence is methodological, not empirical. Cronbach and Meehl do not study LLM agents, reputation systems, or Kaal's nine metrics. Their analysis does not show that any selected metric measures agency cost or that the registered experiment produces valid results. It supports a falsifiable validation structure. Kaal's operationalizations remain to be tested against that structure.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cronbach/construct.htm"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-017"
   },
   "sha256": "1743eb97553f8e00dd87e10bde7a9aadea594cc86745302c6549e1393398d39c",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete scholarly full text with DOI, PubMed, Crossref, OpenAlex, and two university-hosted public copies",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-313",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-313",
   "text": "Knutsen and Heggedal qualify Kaal's experimental design. Their laboratory study compares three repeated-game conditions. The control reveals no history. A second treatment exposes past actions. A third treatment adds induced commitment types and permits reputation formation. Efficient coordination in late supergames rises from 5 percent in the control to 50 percent with history and 84 percent with the reputation treatment. The design supports isolating a reputation mechanism by varying the institutional layer around a fixed underlying game. Its scope is narrower than Kaal's program. The subjects are human players in repeated coordination games. The source does not use heterogeneous AI agents, conventional ground-truthed tasks, or a combined reputation-and-incentive layer. It also does not compare that combined layer with its absence. The external experiment therefore supports the treatment logic but does not validate Kaal's registered agent-cohort design or results.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5586750"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-016"
   },
   "sha256": "9245c743a0a4d4874d86c1d4d086a0467970aaba05e25646383ff43bca213cfd",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref-registered SSRN abstract with DOI-bound author identity and an independently indexed public landing page",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.92,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-312",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-312",
   "text": "Navajas et al. qualify Kaal's empirical deliberation claim. The comparison is narrow. In a live crowd of 5,180 participants, individuals first answered general knowledge questions, then deliberated in independent groups of five, reached consensus, and revised their estimates. Averaging the group decisions was more accurate than aggregating the initial independent answers. The experiment thus shows that group structure can change the measured effect of deliberation under controlled conditions, but it does not test a reputation-bearing validation institution. It also does not involve autonomous agents, preregistration, replication, or Kaal's metric definitions and correction procedure. The human participants answered general knowledge questions, not tasks assigned to a controlled multi-model cohort. Kaal's reported result therefore remains source-bound to the registered cohort and cannot be validated by the external study.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-017-0273-4"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-015"
   },
   "sha256": "403a2904dfeb82d6eb0fab9a3bbd152ad917026fc4f91530899a68052cad08ed",
   "evidenceLevel": "official peer-reviewed Nature Human Behaviour article page with DOI, complete author identity, evidence-bearing abstract, and immutable HTML bytes",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-311",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-311",
   "text": "Arpinon and Lefebvre qualify Kaal's discovery-to-confirmation design. Registered Reports fix the analysis plan before data collection. In-principle acceptance then commits the journal to publication regardless of the result, subject to Stage 2 verification that the analysis followed the Stage 1 plan. Their format also separates confirmatory analyses from later exploration and prevents conclusions from resting on ex-post exploratory results. This correspondence is limited. The article addresses a journal review format in agricultural economics. It does not verify Kaal's registered primary, the reporting of his null result, the preregistration of any exploratory signal, or the mechanical application of his confirmation rule. It also warns that preregistration alone leaves room for incomplete plans, uncertain policing, and suppression of null findings. Kaal's cycle therefore tracks recognized controls against postdiction and publication bias. Its evidentiary weight still depends on immutable registrations, disclosed deviations, and rule-bound execution.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/qopen/qoae011"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-014"
   },
   "sha256": "c6ac18ccd83ef4d0c342abeec1e749280c3e8cfd40636b4a37379bdbd01ef849",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public peer-reviewed Oxford Academic HTML with DOI, author identity, section-bound passages, and immutable full-text bytes",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-310",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-310",
   "text": "Rubin qualifies Kaal's registered design claim. Preregistration places planned hypotheses, methods, and analyses in a time-stamped record before data collection and analysis. That historical record allows readers to identify deviations between the plan and the reported study. The correspondence is limited. The source does not verify that Kaal's hypotheses, metric definitions, correction procedure, or analysis plan were fixed before each condition ran. It also argues that preregistration supplies historical transparency, not a complete credibility judgment, when current rationales, open materials, code, and robustness checks are available. Kaal's claim therefore describes a relevant design control. Its execution and evidentiary weight still depend on the preserved registration, disclosed deviations, and the study record.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.tqmp.org/RegularArticles/vol16-4/p376/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-013"
   },
   "sha256": "8e59e7fee2ee43cd89a7b3d60f85c2cb93b53a507f14496739a995d7ba434c99",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 15-page open journal article with DOI, author identity, section-bound passages, and immutable published PDF bytes",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-309",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-309",
   "text": "Zhu and colleagues qualify Kaal's nine-metric comparison. MultiAgentBench evaluates multi-agent systems across two dimensions: task completion and coordination. It combines milestone-based KPIs and final-output scores with planning and communication measures. This supports a multidimensional battery when the object of study includes both outcomes and interaction processes. The correspondence is limited. MultiAgentBench uses six scenarios and its own scoring instruments. It does not validate Kaal's nine labels, the reliability or calibration of those measures, the registered cohort, or any resulting comparison. The source also reports limited scenario and model coverage. Finer-grained component analysis remains future work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.421/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-012"
   },
   "sha256": "e61cb5b334da7c9849435844de4864c46f36f79e04b174b4539b2a2bd45637b4",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 43-page ACL proceedings paper with DOI, author list, section-bound passages, and immutable PDF bytes",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-308",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-308",
   "text": "Bougherara, Muller, and Teyssier qualify Kaal's controlled comparison. They explain that experimental economics isolates a causal effect by changing one environmental or institutional feature at a time. Within-subject designs can hold the participants constant across treatments, but they also create an order-effect risk. This supports the methodological rationale for keeping the labor force and work assignment fixed while varying the institution. The correspondence remains narrow. The chapter states a general experimental principle. It does not verify that Kaal's cohort, tasks, or timing were actually held constant. It also does not establish randomization, counterbalancing, independence across conditions, or the validity of any later estimate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK607751/"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-011"
   },
   "sha256": "a39eff305d6883e789480a135cd7630726f40e50b3639f58580434d405ec435c",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public NCBI Bookshelf chapter with DOI, authors, date, section-bound evidence, and immutable HTML bytes",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-307",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-307",
   "text": "Wolf and colleagues qualify Kaal's exploratory result. In a human predator-detection experiment, participants first decided alone and then observed the choices of other group members. Mean true positives rose from 0.564 to 0.751. Mean false positives fell from 0.166 to 0.113. The source therefore provides independent evidence that group interaction can change the composition of decision errors. The correspondence remains narrow. Wolf and colleagues studied a quorum response under conditionally independent personal information. Their intervention improved both sides of discrimination. It did not leave net discrimination unchanged. The study also did not examine autonomous agents, over-approval, unanimity, or herding. It does not validate Kaal's design, execution, or reported effect sizes.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2012.2777"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-009"
   },
   "sha256": "61ddb8b9bc8bf368b4389422e712fb1fc047eef1775c620e08db527eb6632751",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public nine-page journal article with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PubMed, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-306",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-306",
   "text": "Etherington and colleagues qualify Kaal's exploratory result. In a multicenter vignette study, internal medicine residents first decided alone and then deliberated in small peer groups. Discussion reduced practice intensity and increased clinical certainty beyond the result expected from averaging individual scores. Practice intensity meant willingness to order tests, consultations, or treatment. That result offers narrow evidence that discussion can lower action intensity through interaction. Still, the correspondence does not establish better decisions. The instrument did not measure whether less intense practice was appropriate, and the study did not examine autonomous agents or work-product approval. It also provides no evidence on unanimity, net discrimination, or Kaal's reported effects.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4300/jgme-d-20-00948.1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-008"
   },
   "sha256": "d189b6da7c6c37db697a09df5bd0b4bec2a7984741589303f12bff0b2f89475e",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public six-page journal article with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PubMed, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.92,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-305",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-305",
   "text": "Henderson and Chambers qualify Kaal's treatment of exploratory effects with Registered Report guidance. They state that data-dependent exploratory analyses generate hypotheses rather than test them. Such results should remain tentative until further confirmatory research verifies them. This supports Kaal's decision to label the two discovery-campaign effects as hypotheses rather than findings. The correspondence is methodological. The source does not evaluate Kaal's agents, experimental execution, effect sizes, or whether later work confirmed either effect.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1010571"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-007"
   },
   "sha256": "9103826d4364d2535b7a374f26d4fa1f24cd5fc93479706e013a87e627f501d9",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public nine-page PLOS Computational Biology Version of Record with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-304",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-304",
   "text": "Klein and Epley qualify Kaal's exploratory over-approval result with four experiments on human lie detection. Three-person groups that discussed their judgments distinguished truths from lies more accurately than individuals. The gain came primarily from identifying lies correctly, not from improved truth detection or a change in the tendency to answer 'lie.' Statistical aggregation of individual answers did not reproduce the effect. The source therefore supports a narrow mechanism correspondence: discussion can reduce acceptance of false content through information created in interaction. The comparison remains limited. The experiments concern human judgments about deceptive statements. They do not test multi-model validation pools, ground-truth work approval, unanimity, or Kaal's reported effect sizes.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1504048112"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-006"
   },
   "sha256": "a75179488e845c2866413495716dc381c2124497097509dd440e60ad83572427",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public six-page PNAS Version of Record with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PubMed, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-303",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-303",
   "text": "Lorenz and colleagues qualify Kaal's registered null result with an independent experiment on collective judgment. Social influence caused participants' estimates to converge and reduced group diversity. It did not significantly reduce collective error or improve accuracy. The result establishes that information exchange can change group behavior without improving the relevant accuracy measure. The correspondence is limited. Lorenz and colleagues study human factual estimation under exposure to others' answers. They do not study structured multi-model validation pools, Youden's J, or Kaal's reported confidence interval.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1008636108"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-005"
   },
   "sha256": "e4858c3018268832c020bc6285a881a709610081acdfc96899d057faa45f1d99",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public six-page PNAS Version of Record with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PubMed, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-302",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-302",
   "text": "Dirnagl qualifies Kaal's two-act empirical cycle with an independent methodological rationale for separating discovery from confirmation. Exploratory work develops theories, hypotheses, methods, and evidence to carry forward. The same data cannot both generate and test a hypothesis, so a distinct confirmatory phase must follow to screen false positives and inflated effects. Dirnagl also argues that preregistration and contemporaneous logging should preserve the study's trajectory, including methodological choices and failed lines of experimentation. That makes the process itself evidentiary, not merely the final numbers. The correspondence is methodological only. Dirnagl writes from biomedical research and does not evaluate Kaal's multi-agent cohort, registered measures, execution, or reported effects.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000690"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-004"
   },
   "sha256": "a0d3f5e5173e4e9ae9d2be09f0229de120d87c94aced29b5530b97514c582b29",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public six-page PLOS Biology Version of Record with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Europe PMC, and exact page-bound full-text evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-301",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-301",
   "text": "Huynh, Jennings, and Shadbolt qualify Kaal's agentic reputation substrate with an earlier decentralized architecture for open multi-agent systems. FIRE lets autonomous consumer agents evaluate providers by integrating direct interaction trust, role-based trust, witness reputation from other agents' ratings, and certified reputation supplied as third-party references. In a simulated open multi-agent system, consumer agents used those measures to select providers. FIRE outperformed both no-trust agents and, in the reported tests, the centralized SPORAS benchmark. The witness-reputation component also improved and accelerated agent learning within the consumer community. The correspondence is architectural: both systems coordinate autonomous agents through reputation-bearing, peer-mediated evaluation. FIRE is not Kaal's cohort-validation or broader accountability substrate. It assumes truthful witness and certified reports, lacks credibility filtering for strategic misinformation, and tests provider selection rather than institutional deliberation or agency-cost measurement.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10458-005-6825-4"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-003"
   },
   "sha256": "2db1336764788ea27e2efaa179715f98fb20e87c0975f2a5561c56c786c49050",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 36-page Version of Record with independent Southampton repository, Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity and exact page-bound architectural, empirical, and limitation passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-300",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-300",
   "text": "Qiu, Carroll, and Allen qualify Kaal's diagnosis by supplying an incentive-bearing LLM evaluation that does not require ground-truth labels. Their peer-prediction pipeline scores a participant by how much its answer helps an independent expert predict another participant's answer. It separately rewards experts for faithfully reporting probability estimates and converts the scores into training rewards or evaluation payoffs. Under stated prior assumptions, honest and informative reporting is a payoff-maximizing equilibrium. Experiments across models from 135M to 405B parameters and 85 domains show resistance to deceptive answers and recovery of most of the truthfulness loss after malicious fine-tuning. This does not verify work quality against Kaal's reputation substrate, and it does not directly measure calibration, herding, or free-riding. It shows that mutual-predictability rewards are a bounded alternative to verified-quality payoffs for testing truthfulness and informativeness.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20299"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-002"
   },
   "sha256": "d5753c33d94945d4d92d4cf4894b10575aff4242c6e37a5c5e6e5e347cb30433",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 36-page arXiv 2601.20299v1 preprint with arXiv and OpenAlex identity, exact page-bound passages, formal incentive analysis, and empirical LLM evaluation",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-299",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-299",
   "text": "Müller and Müller extend Kaal's diagnosis by testing LLM agents in an explicitly incentive-bearing environment. Cattle Trade places four agents in a 50–60-turn economic game combining auctions, hidden offers, bargaining, bluffing, opponent modeling, resource constraints, and conflicting incentives. Across 242 games, the authors find that strategic coherence is more closely associated with success than any isolated capability. Their behavior traces also expose overbidding, self-bidding, bankrupt trade initiation, and weak opponent-state adaptation. This does not validate Kaal's reputation substrate. It is a bounded benchmark that moves multi-agent evaluation beyond capability-only tests toward behavior under economic and adversarial incentives.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14537"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7261018-001"
   },
   "sha256": "e209a08dac7af8e420f3493a199e115d1979d569d469a4751818020c5e29710a",
   "evidenceLevel": "complete public 24-page ICLR 2026 Workshop on MALGAI paper with arXiv and OpenAlex identity, exact page-bound passages, and reported 242-game evaluation",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-298",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-298",
   "text": "Jindani independently confirms Kaal's distinction between equilibrium existence and equilibrium selection. His peer-reviewed analysis states that the Folk Theorem permits a wide range of payoffs in infinitely repeated games, while existing learning results that converge to equilibrium do not select among those equilibria. Jindani then adds a stochastic learning rule that selects an efficient subgame-perfect equilibrium. The extension is narrow. The result concerns two-player repeated games under specified learning dynamics. It does not show that an autonomous-agent population will select cooperation merely because cooperative equilibria are available.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2022.105551"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-040"
   },
   "sha256": "e9fd6a5e9d563284717a778d63166736060e14e0a6e5c5583d79b3c5534625d9",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed CC BY Journal of Economic Theory article with Crossref/OpenAlex/Semantic Scholar identity, a complete registry-bound abstract, and a public 25-page author presentation with exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-297",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-297",
   "text": "Elliott, Poelen, and Fortes independently support the content-addressed verification mechanism in Kaal's evidentiary-base claim. Their peer-reviewed Scientific Data article shows that signed citations bind digital content to unique, recomputable content signatures. A future reader can verify content identity by recomputing the hash, and recursively signed references can form verifiable citation graphs. This is a qualification, not validation of Kaal's full substrate. The article concerns research data and provenance. It does not show that settlement, record, and report derive from one base or that an authorized reviewer reconciles them with institutional state.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02230-y"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-039"
   },
   "sha256": "4c87d11e2e3a481a00035a435d68a7bc142fd1b964c2a915c42444cbff25d5f0",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed open-access Scientific Data article with complete public full text, exact page-bound passages, Crossref/OpenAlex/Semantic Scholar identity records, and reported real-world data-organization applications",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-296",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-296",
   "text": "Gluhak and Pavlič provide direct operational support for Kaal's fail-closed control rule. Their CI/CD case study uses dependent quality checks. If any check fails, the pipeline fails and the code is not integrated into the larger codebase. This independently instantiates a failed control stopping advancement instead of merely annotating a run. The comparison is bounded. The source concerns software-delivery pipelines and static source-code quality, not autonomous-agent reputation infrastructure or Kaal's broader control taxonomy.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3237/#paper-glu"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-038"
   },
   "sha256": "62a32da7fceaefe071a98d31edfc0fcf9ee89e96d17e3dc7f23561499378d77e",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed workshop paper with a systematic literature review and implemented CI/CD case study, complete public CC BY 4.0 full text, CEUR proceedings identity, and DBLP identity record",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-295",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-295",
   "text": "Samuel, Löffler, and König-Ries provide independent support for the run-level part of Kaal's reporting-layer design. Their study identifies code, data, parameters, package versions, preprocessing, and execution context as information needed to reproduce machine-learning experiments. ProvBook captures, stores, describes, and compares provenance across Jupyter executions. It also binds each model run to its execution environment. This supports preserving run evidence and controls and comparing results across runs. The comparison is bounded. The source concerns machine-learning research pipelines, not autonomous-agent reputation pools, and it does not test periodic institutional summaries. It therefore qualifies the run-level and cross-run provenance components of Kaal's three-part reporting design rather than validating the complete architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-80960-7_17"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-037"
   },
   "sha256": "99b7e75179894d1cbcc32c998779725f0d00d7472ae359898601e2cfebe2968f",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed conference-chapter identity with complete public arXiv accepted manuscript, plus Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, arXiv, and DOI landing records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-294",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-294",
   "text": "Ahn, Isaac, and Salmon provide independent experimental evidence for the design transition in Kaal's evolution-stage claim. Most prior public-goods experiments fixed groups exogenously. Their experiment instead allows participants to change groups under three entry and exit institutions. The formation rules significantly affect group size, contribution dynamics, and earnings. This supports introducing endogenous formation when testing an institutional arc because fixed membership can suppress behavior generated by formation rules. The comparison is bounded. The source studies human membership in public-goods groups, not autonomous agents forming work, and does not test Kaal's reputation, validation, staking, or three-stage substrate. It therefore qualifies the necessity and implementation level of Kaal's evolution stage rather than validating the architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9779.2008.00357.x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-036"
   },
   "sha256": "0f1367740b21c666749506b2201c6cbb3272f36b787269a6937eeb444be2b8ef",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal-article identity with complete public Indiana University repository full text, plus Crossref, OpenAlex, repository, and DOI landing records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-293",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-293",
   "text": "Mellizo, Carpenter, and Matthews provide independent methodological evidence for Kaal's substrate-stage restriction. Their real-effort experiment randomly assigns manager and worker roles, keeps participants in a common production task, and makes participation in compensation governance exogenous to workers. The authors use this design to identify the causal effect of participatory authority and report output between seven and twelve percentage points higher in participatory firms. This supports the narrower proposition that exogenous work roles can hold the labor-allocation margin fixed while institutional authority varies. The comparison is limited. The source studies human workers in laboratory firms, varies compensation governance, and does not test agent labor markets, reputation, validation, staking, or the broader substrate. It therefore qualifies Kaal's identification strategy rather than validating the full architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s40881-017-0034-1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-035"
   },
   "sha256": "f2820f91122f51905131abeaf5f8afbecf98cd8852be2f3689eee0ad45003643",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal-article identity with complete public IZA discussion-paper full text by the same authors, plus Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, and publisher-page records",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-292",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-292",
   "text": "Sun, Zhou, and Guo provide independent evidence that several elements of Kaal's substrate-stage institution can be combined in an implemented validation system. Their decentralized peer-review model uses verified participants, a shared blockchain environment, token and reputation costs, public review evidence, and citable constructive reviews. They simulate the mechanism with KDD and ICLR data and implement a prototype on Ethereum and IPFS. This supports the feasibility of joining validation, standing, economic commitment, persistent evidence, and attribution in one institution. The qualification is substantial. The study concerns human scholars rather than autonomous agents. Its reputation is not capability-scoped REP. Its tokens are not Kaal's validator stake, and the model does not implement citation-weighted attribution, controlled propagation over a WDAG, or Kaal's exact staged deliberation. The source therefore qualifies the institutional pattern. It does not validate the complete substrate-stage architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xinn.2025.100945"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-034"
   },
   "sha256": "06c1fc5bb66ed16d21b6328012f2d118aa8d114cc36fc154f04c684e3eb8914b",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed open-access article with complete Europe PMC JATS full text, Crossref, PubMed, Europe PMC, and OpenAlex identity records, implementation, and conference-data simulations",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-291",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-291",
   "text": "Shinn and colleagues provide direct evidence for one boundary in Kaal's wild-stage definition. Their Reflexion agent converts task feedback into reflective text, stores it in episodic memory, and uses it to improve decisions in later trials. In the controlled baseline, the environment resets without self-reflection or a memory update. The comparison supports Kaal's distinction between an isolated call and an architecture that preserves task experience. The qualification is strict. Reflexion stores private episodic memory for one agent. It does not create a validation pool, reputation, staking, slashing, citation attribution, or deliberation. The evidence therefore supports the persistence and residue boundary. It does not validate Kaal's full institutional bundle or establish that every wild-stage agent interaction is literally one shot.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-033"
   },
   "sha256": "dff1dfa662705df0eeabc19c2bc5d69217579d86387c2f6d9ee30c988d8645b4",
   "evidenceLevel": "NeurIPS 2023 paper with public 19-page arXiv v4 full text and independent arXiv and OpenAlex identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-290",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-290",
   "text": "Engl, Riedl, and Weber provide direct evidence for the identification logic and an important boundary on it. Their laboratory study keeps the public-goods game structure constant across treatments, varies whether and how an enforcement institution applies, and measures effects relative to a no-institution baseline. This design permits causal statements about the institutional treatment. It supports Kaal's premise that a fixed task environment can isolate an institutional contribution. The qualification is material. Engl, Riedl, and Weber use controlled treatment contrasts. Kaal's claim describes a sequential stage arc. Keeping the population, task distribution, and harness fixed does not by itself remove period, learning, order, or carryover effects. The external evidence therefore supports the controlled-comparison principle, but it does not validate the exact stage sequence or the later inferences attributed to it.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1257/mic.20180336"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-032"
   },
   "sha256": "bcb1d38c64ba249283a50390e5e72afefc2188b223903fb1c43f2a4760966b21",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal article with public 68-page IZA discussion-paper full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, IZA, and AEA identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-289",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-289",
   "text": "Zhang, Deng, Wu, Xiao, Xu, and Li provide a concrete but narrower analogue for the claimed staged architecture. AgentWard divides an autonomous agent's runtime lifecycle into five stages, assigns each stage a distinct protection layer, trust boundary, and security objective, and carries shared risk evidence across the stages. Its cognition layer controls which state transitions become persistent, while its decision layer can revise, escalate, or block an action before execution. This supports the structural value of stage-specific surfaces, carried state, and explicit transition controls. It does not validate Kaal's wild/substrate/evolution cohort sequence or its administrative state guarantees: AgentWard concerns the runtime security lifecycle of an individual agent. The paper therefore qualifies the claimed architecture rather than validating its exact progression.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24657"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-031"
   },
   "sha256": "35cdc439c241c2560c6e119cf846373b87c38dd52eb46fa0914318bef3bd7024",
   "evidenceLevel": "public seven-page arXiv preprint and prototype architecture with independent OpenAlex and arXiv identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-288",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-288",
   "text": "Trovò and Massari provide a concrete but partial analogue for the claimed institutional assembly. Ants-Review separates issuers, peer reviewers, approvers, and community contributors; stores the paper and each submitted review on IPFS with hashes recorded on Ethereum; keeps reviewer recommendations and author replies permanently accessible; and lets an approver accept a review and set its reward according to assessed quality. The authors also treat review work as a source of community reputation and propose community voting over review quality. This supports the feasibility of separating production from review while preserving an auditable record and attaching later reputational consequences. It does not, however, bind final judgment to accumulated standing, and its community-voting automation is future work rather than a demonstrated mechanism. The paper therefore qualifies the full architecture rather than validating it.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-71593-9_2"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-030"
   },
   "sha256": "fce6be94a2e9a2b68fa962eab50534a77f527c624a680e936775995531931ece",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Euro-Par workshop chapter with a public eight-page arXiv full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, and arXiv identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-287",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-287",
   "text": "Manzoor, Chen, Lee, and Smith provide empirical reason to qualify the design guarantee. In a seven-year panel of more than one million online debates, they isolate a causal reputation effect after controlling for unstructured argument text: reputation increased persuasion beyond the validity, strength, and presentation of the arguments, and ten additional reputation points raised persuasion probability by 31% over the platform average. They characterize reputation as a low-effort heuristic proxy for argument quality and warn that visible participant characteristics can distort consensus toward reputed participants. This supports making reasons inspectable, but shows that reason visibility alone does not prevent standing from functioning as authority; an implementation must also constrain, segregate, or counteract reputation cues during merits evaluation. The evidence is from human online debate, not Kaal's agent validation pools, so it identifies a concrete failure mode rather than validating the proposed architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2023.4762"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-029"
   },
   "sha256": "7fcaba97671fee1c685473f8617808d4b0b1ed969c9ece75bacdf7928526c127",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Management Science article with a public 43-page arXiv v1 full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, and arXiv identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-286",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-286",
   "text": "Teutsch and Reitwießner independently instantiate the core adjudicative function in a narrower blockchain-computation setting. TrueBit invokes a verification game when a challenger disputes a solver's answer to a computational task; the parties iteratively narrow the contested execution step, and bounded judges rule whether the challenge is justified. The procedure is task-bound and ends in a consequence-bearing determination, making it functionally analogous to a temporary adjudicative institution for machine work. This is a qualification rather than equivalence: TrueBit uses an incentive-driven solver-challenger game and the Ethereum miner community as judges, not Kaal's purpose-convened validation pool or broader agent-governance substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789811278631_0015"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-028"
   },
   "sha256": "12629e399d1878812c927857efa2dd4a9cb939aee5546dd74afb6b4887d8800b",
   "evidenceLevel": "published World Scientific book chapter with a public 50-page arXiv v1 full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, DataCite, and arXiv identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-285",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-285",
   "text": "Vu, Zhang, and Aberer independently instantiate a narrower version of Kaal's two-ended temporal constraint. Their provider-selection protocol considers only the provider's most recent interaction and can blacklist a provider after recent cheating, so a large stock of older standing cannot indefinitely mask current behavior. Separately, a provider's pseudonym acquires an identity premium that increases with tenure; leaving and rejoining forfeits that accumulated value, making whitewashing non-optimal for a rational provider. This qualifies rather than fully validates Kaal's design: the source models a human service marketplace, uses a most-recent-rating rule rather than continuous score decay, and imposes an economic identity premium rather than Kaal's non-transferable REP and broader institutional history.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12032"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-027"
   },
   "sha256": "68e4626a3a9ef46008b4b2923ed7e11df058990c941ab4dd90cc2e27e12d8345",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed Computational Intelligence article with an author-repository public 27-page full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-284",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-284",
   "text": "Olifer independently supports a narrower peer-validation form of Kaal's accrual rule. In his network-community model, members submit documents with verifiable content; three randomly selected independent reviewers decide authenticity by simple majority; and reputation increases only after the submitted document is evaluated as authentic, while a negative evaluation reduces it. That mechanism makes a community validation decision, rather than mere activity or self-assertion, the gate for reputation accrual. It qualifies rather than fully validates Kaal's design: Olifer models document-authenticity review in a bounded community, not Kaal's broader REP ledger, legal or technical institutional attestations, capability tags, stake, citation weighting, or production governance substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.07984"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-026"
   },
   "sha256": "0a011a028d0e438ca60dfb47f11dfcbfe7452893eeabc0521a1be393aea3b403",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 25-page arXiv v2 full text with arXiv and OpenAlex identity agreement; six exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-283",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-283",
   "text": "Casals, Fermé, and Brandão independently support the narrower domain-separation principle in Kaal's claim. Their context-aware BDI model structures information into domains and permits one agent to assign different trust or credibility degrees to the same counterpart across those domains. They illustrate the point with a doctor whose medical information deserves different credibility from the same person's sports opinions, and they warn that a global credibility degree can cause accurate domain-specific experience to be discarded. This qualifies Kaal's design choice to maintain reputation by capability domain rather than as one global score. The paper models information-sharing trust in a preliminary formal architecture; it does not implement Kaal's demonstrated-performance REP, institutional validation, or broader governance substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.5220/0006600602440249"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-025"
   },
   "sha256": "682cab23d37b9af0602c0a2a139f9558192cb5910ea450fdefbf61c254b05acf",
   "evidenceLevel": "public six-page publisher conference full text with Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity agreement; six exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-282",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-282",
   "text": "Silva, Aparício, and Silva provide independent evidence for the narrower credit-propagation mechanism in Kaal's claim. Their author-ranking study distinguishes paper-level networks, which diffuse scientific credit to cited papers and then derive author credit from those papers, from author-level networks, which diffuse credit directly to cited authors. PageRank further weights a citation from an important author more heavily and updates an author's score from incoming citation links. This qualifies Kaal's proposition that standing can propagate through a graph rather than accrue only at the original contribution. The study concerns human scholarly impact, treats citations as credit-bearing edges without Kaal's institutional-validation condition, and does not establish autonomous agents, domain-specific REP, or the broader governance substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s41109-019-0182-8"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-024"
   },
   "sha256": "a9ddc5c7f0cd9d2884104d3b72577122fc3b6c3fb233252b0417b9383477dda4",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 15-page CC BY Version of Record with Crossref and OpenAlex identity agreement; six exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.99,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-281",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-281",
   "text": "Pizzi and coauthors provide an independent implementation precedent for the narrower inspectable-history mechanism in Kaal's claim. AiiDA represents calculations and data as nodes connected by directional, labeled links, producing a directed acyclic graph from initial data through intermediate calculations to final results. The system saves that provenance chain and supports fast queries over data history. This qualifies the proposition that a directed, ordered structure can preserve an inspectable record of accumulated work. AiiDA applies to computational workflows and data rather than a population of autonomous agents, and it does not establish reputation weighting, validation, adjudication, or governance in Kaal's WDAG.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.commatsci.2015.09.013"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-023"
   },
   "sha256": "3977b9c2161d19fae069a7f01c0211ba40db16061a2c439f972676f624625072",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 30-page arXiv full text of a peer-reviewed journal article with Crossref and OpenAlex identity agreement; six exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-280",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-280",
   "text": "Wang and coauthors provide an independent implementation precedent for the narrower representation pattern in Kaal's claim. Microsoft Academic Graph models scholarly communications as a heterogeneous graph centered on publications, with directed typed relations for authorship, concepts, publication venues, citations, and semantic relatedness. Its concept entities categorize publications by field of study, and its citing relation can carry citation-context attributes. This supports the narrower proposition that an institutional scholarly graph can represent contributions or publications and multiple typed citation and domain relationships. It does not establish a typed validation relationship, autonomous-agent identity, reputation effects, or governance in Kaal's WDAG, and it does not validate the broader agentic reputation substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1162/qss_a_00021"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-022"
   },
   "sha256": "60ac0e5392d4f6aab6998e51efcff2579ec7a99007422b8cf44025483e35f4e0",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 18-page MIT Press CC-BY full text with Crossref and OpenAlex identity agreement; six exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-279",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-279",
   "text": "Swank and Wrasai provide an independent economic-model qualification of Kaal's functional separation between deliberation and a binding final act. Their collective-decision procedure has three stages: agents acquire information about a project, communicate, and then vote. The model also shows that communication changes incentives to acquire information: when information is cheap, communication can reduce effort through free-riding; when information is expensive, communication can increase effort by making acquired information useful to another agent. This supports the narrower claim that deliberation develops information before a distinct final institutional decision, while showing that the informational benefit is incentive-sensitive rather than automatic. It does not support the broader assertion that adjudication is priced through stake, reputation, sanctions, or settlement, and it does not validate Kaal's agentic reputation substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.635285"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-021"
   },
   "sha256": "4b83f43a0de63430286d0db5f41d7199d736ceb809e10b0b90b3f151b4e3b598",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 21-page Tinbergen Institute full text with Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, and Erasmus repository identity agreement; five exact page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-278",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-278",
   "text": "Johnson and Gastil provide an independent institutional-design qualification of Kaal's separation between open deliberation and accountable judgment. Their comparative analysis treats the scope of authority as a design variable: deliberative outcomes may be provisional and require ratification or approval. They describe participatory-budgeting sequences that combine open community forums with specially elected bodies, and North American processes where public deliberation generates preferred outcomes that a non-deliberative public vote finally determines. This supports the narrower distinction between deliberative input and an institutionally authorized binding decision. It does not establish that the later decision stage is reputation-weighted, automated, or accountable in Kaal's technical sense, and it does not validate the agentic reputation substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.16997/jdd.219"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-020"
   },
   "sha256": "936917614edc2bb8818b29cfe1038d7f1fb9e5ecc289c490b602521490b7f4cd",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed CC-BY journal full text with Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity agreement; three exact section- and page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-277",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-277",
   "text": "Buterin and Griffith provide an independent cryptoeconomic qualification of Kaal’s validation-stage alignment mechanism. In Casper, validators post deposits, checkpoint-voting violations are objectively detectable, and evidence of either slashing-condition violation can be submitted on-chain to take the validator’s entire deposit. The paper further explains that the penalty can be set above the expected mining reward, making prohibited validation conduct economically unattractive. This supports the narrower mechanism that institutional validation can attach material cost to dishonest participation. Casper does not evaluate open-ended AI work, define low-quality outputs, or validate Kaal’s broader reputation substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09437"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-019"
   },
   "sha256": "c86cf045880d72190d00ea4cac7397299c39c0de2aa6316cbaacd5bc2c0cd491",
   "evidenceLevel": "public 10-page arXiv v4 technical full text with clean machine-readable extraction, three exact page-bound passages, and author/title/version identity verified against the arXiv API",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-276",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-276",
   "text": "Naja and coauthors provide an independent qualification of Kaal’s claim that evidentiary records and a graph are complementary memory scales. Their peer-reviewed system links planned evidence to accountability traces and represents activities, outcomes, responsible agents, and their relationships in a single provenance-based knowledge graph for collection, inspection, query, and audit. That supports the record-to-review and graph-as-accumulated-accountability-information mechanisms. The article does not model reputation, condition future participation, or test the three-scale architecture together, so it qualifies two components rather than validating the full design.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2022.3188967"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-018"
   },
   "sha256": "e3552f28315560ceb6fdee9591e75a38cabfec6636183d3f0950789280bfe076",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed CC-BY IEEE Access article with a public 30-page repository PDF, clean machine-readable text, four exact page-bound passages, and Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity links",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.95,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-275",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-275",
   "text": "Santos, Cerqueira, and Carvalho Júnior provide an independent qualification of Kaal’s principle that reputation is institutional memory rather than merely a score. Their repeated trust-game study treats recordkeeping as externalized memory of past interactions, yet reports that introducing records in a direct-dyad setting reduced trust formation and reciprocity through a crowding-out mechanism. The result makes the memory layer analytically distinct from its behavioral output: preserving history does not itself guarantee cooperative effects. The study does not compare records with numerical scores and does not test Kaal’s agentic substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1590/1808-057x202010800"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-017"
   },
   "sha256": "fd359788f68d0ce3146d985289e27365fd6c5307bce61bda9ab4016b0aefc742",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed CC-BY journal article with a public 17-page English version of record, exact page-bound passages, an original experiment, and Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity links",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-274",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-274",
   "text": "Lie-Panis and coauthors supply an independent institutional-game analogue for Kaal’s iterability claim. Their model places long-lived actors in a repeated game against short-lived choosers who interact only once and exit; observed reciprocation and institutional contribution update the actor’s reputation, which future choosers use when deciding whether to trust. The model therefore shows how persistent reputation can connect otherwise one-shot counterparties to future consequences. It does not test Kaal’s substrate, autonomous-agent calls, or the stronger claim that every consequential call becomes indefinitely iterable.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2408802121"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-016"
   },
   "sha256": "60b0da7eed720c04a528dbc61adfa7074bb4f21d89b85faeca7b2c4a56003c6f",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed PNAS article with a public 10-page full text, exact page-bound passages, and Crossref, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, PubMed, and PMC identity links",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-273",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-273",
   "text": "Player and Griffiths independently operationalize a bounded version of Kaal’s decay mechanism. In their multi-agent trust models, sliding windows delete older interaction records that no longer represent current behavior, while forgetting factors continuously down-weight older interactions; their adaptive RaPTaR method then uses current-behavior estimates to improve future partner selection. This supports treating reputation evidence as perishable rather than permanent. It does not establish Kaal’s stronger claim that the value of the future never reaches zero for every incumbent, nor does it validate his standing-token, staking, or rights architecture.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1017/s0269888920000077"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-015"
   },
   "sha256": "43ff890aabe32f138d5887f9a5ab476752e907ba4efd4732f220980830e88f27",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed journal article with a Warwick author-accepted 25-page full text, exact page-bound passages, and Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity verification",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-272",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-272",
   "text": "Yu, Miao, An, Shen, and Leung independently show one mechanism through which reputation makes future participation economically consequential. In their human-agent allocation model, performance determines reputation standing, reputation shapes trusters’ future delegation decisions, and the resulting workload and income affect long-term well-being; their RATA-NL approach also governs how many requests trustees accept over time. This supports Kaal’s narrower link between standing and future work opportunities, while qualifying the broader architecture: the evidence concerns human trustees and task allocation, not autonomous agents’ rights to validate, stake, propose work, or win jobs under Kaal’s evolutionary-stage substrate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/brjz5422"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-014"
   },
   "sha256": "940d8f50db6fb5fb501d018b876a6b1750fb2addb8ddd9e57d39e39710704440",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed AAMAS proceedings paper with an author-hosted eight-page full text, exact page-bound passages, and Crossref identity verification",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.98,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-271",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-271",
   "text": "Hasan, Brunie, and Bertino independently specify the identity-binding mechanism behind Kaal's claim. Their survey distinguishes pseudonym-bound reputation, which a participant can abandon by re-entering under a fresh pseudonym, from identity-bound reputation that survives pseudonym changes; in the surveyed BPRF design, PKI recognition reassigns prior reputation after exit and re-entry and thereby resists Sybil and whitewashing attacks. This supports persistence as a defense against costless history shedding, while qualifying Kaal's design: the surveyed mechanism is human- and PKI-bound and does not establish per-capability agent tags, non-transferability between tags, or the substrate's wider incentives.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3490236"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-013"
   },
   "sha256": "1bfa27753d5e815bfb49914bb5ee8515751097d009906c7c2b5e9cd28ab6786b",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed ACM Computing Surveys article with an author-hosted public full text and independent Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-270",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-270",
   "text": "Hu, Rong, and Van Kleek sharpen Kaal's claim that iterability must be engineered. Their FAccT 2026 analysis identifies persistent identity, repeated interaction, retained histories, and sanction-sensitive future consequences as necessary preconditions for reputation; it then argues that present language-model agents do not supply those properties intrinsically and require ex ante protocol-based behavioral harnesses. This independently supports the institutional-design premise that iterability is constructed, while qualifying Kaal's implementation: ex post identity-based reputation remains unsafe unless the protocol first solves agent dissociation and configuration continuity.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3805689.3806748"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-012"
   },
   "sha256": "85c53b444beca9e76746c9f610bf829b5b4286fc25bbfc32740bdcd189f4b871",
   "evidenceLevel": "published FAccT 2026 proceedings article with public arXiv v3 full text, ACM DOI, and independent Crossref/OpenAlex identity, publication, and non-retraction checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-269",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-269",
   "text": "Song, Huang, Zhao, and Feng qualify Kaal's non-human reputation-feedback mechanism. COOPER aggregates neighbors' opinions and direct interaction histories into reputation assessments, then conditions agents' later policies on those assessments; across tested social-network structures, the authors report sustained cooperation and adaptation to reputation norms. This supports a bounded analogue in which collective reputational feedback shapes future agent behavior. It does not establish stake-backed pooling, work-quality or citation-honesty scoring, validation accuracy, or equivalence to an RLHF reward model.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04359v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-011"
   },
   "sha256": "5e235f48c98852db78a74eae6bb74d0370518646479cddb9cc82f7a21cf5d7da",
   "evidenceLevel": "public arXiv v1 preprint with full 20-page PDF and independent arXiv/OpenAlex identity, open-access, and non-retraction checks",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.92,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-268",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-268",
   "text": "D3 qualifies Kaal's proposition that structured adversarial deliberation can serve as an institution for evaluating expertise-dependent work. Harrasse, Bandi, and Bandi organize role-specialized advocates, a judge, and jurors into a structured debate protocol, and their EACL 2026 experiments report materially higher agreement with human judgments than a single judge and other multi-agent baselines across MT-Bench, AlignBench, and AUTO-J. The evidence supports deliberative evaluation and explicit cost-accuracy tradeoffs for LLM outputs; it does not establish market price formation, payment settlement, or evaluation of human expert labor.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.eacl-long.392"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-010"
   },
   "sha256": "b7d4f9b4534e6bc415da32b6eb683e19e402f839ad24e9a39866d61318fe2208",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed EACL 2026 proceedings article with full ACL Anthology PDF and Crossref/OpenAlex published-version, independence, and non-retraction verification",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.93,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-267",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-267",
   "text": "MeritRank qualifies Kaal's architectural response to cheap Sybil identity creation. Nasrulin, Ishmaev, and Pouwelse model reputation as accumulated feedback on real contributions and experimentally show that transitivity and connectivity decay can bound the gain from additional Sybil identities. The fit is partial and cautionary: the study concerns MakerDAO feedback graphs rather than staked AI-agent validation pools, does not establish non-zero exit cost or attacker-independent pool composition, and finds that naive epoch decay can worsen Sybil tolerance by favoring fresh feedback.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1109/BRAINS55737.2022.9908685"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-009"
   },
   "sha256": "601821ed7ac40815fa3c89568639263f53e8cf4fdc190066fbba2e9714a5961f",
   "evidenceLevel": "peer-reviewed BRAINS 2022 paper with full corresponding preprint, Crossref/OpenAlex/Semantic Scholar identity, version, independence, and non-retraction verification",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.94,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-266",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-266",
   "text": "Kokkodis provides empirical support for the measurement logic behind Kaal’s per-tag REP design. The study replaces unidimensional worker reputation with dynamic, multidimensional, skill-set-specific quality assessments and reports better differentiation and decision support than current reputation systems. The fit is bounded: the evidence comes from human online labor markets and does not establish Kaal’s agent-specific ledger mechanics, non-transferability, staking, decay, or governance effects.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2020.0972"
   },
   "extends": {
