   },
   "sha256": "41b0585c55757ba0e3fdefa7bffa282c662151776ac8208cfeeb3291eb4e7e58",
   "evidenceLevel": "published Information Systems Research abstract with Crossref and OpenAlex identity, version, independence, and non-retraction verification",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.92,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-265",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-265",
   "text": "Cui et al.’s FREE-MAD supplies an experimentally evaluated alternative to the anti-conformity mechanism in Kaal’s staged-deliberation design. Agents begin with independently generated answers, critically assess peer reasoning without being instructed to follow the majority, and a score-based mechanism derives the final answer from the full trajectory rather than last-round consensus. Across eight reasoning benchmarks, the authors report better reasoning performance with a single-round debate. The comparison qualifies Kaal’s stronger design: FREE-MAD exposes peer responses, has no separately accountable binding adjudicator, and mitigates conformity through prompting and scoring rather than by making the running consensus unobservable.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1600"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-007"
   },
   "sha256": "ff1385170c0be03d96c9c39e4b116a0a1410e15448cff34c944042af3c281629",
   "evidenceLevel": "ACL Anthology published full text with Crossref and OpenAlex identity verification, exact PDF SHA-256, and page-bound mechanism evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.91,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-264",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-264",
   "text": "Yolcu’s constitutional-adjudication study documents a functionally separated institutional arrangement: the Venice Commission supplies non-binding comparative-law advice, while the requesting constitutional court retains the determination whether the statute complies with the constitution. That arrangement independently extends Kaal’s role-separation design by showing how an advisory signal can inform reasoning without acquiring binding authority. The match is limited to constitutional adjudication; it does not validate Kaal’s agent-pool implementation or establish that separation alone guarantees independence.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1515/icl-2021-0044"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-006"
   },
   "sha256": "f5183323aaddb5e11c9bd6d4545adc78d30ee469a8692da2e1cc8dbdcce637e2",
   "evidenceLevel": "Crossref, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar identity-verified repository full text with exact PDF SHA-256 and page-bound role evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.93,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-263",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-263",
   "text": "RepuNet independently operationalizes a bounded version of Kaal's mechanism: reputations are context-dependent by scenario and role, update after direct encounters, and drive whether agents form or maintain network edges for later interactions. Across three social-dilemma scenarios, the paper reports that this coupling supports selective partner choice and sustained cooperation. The match is limited to simulated LLM-agent networks; it does not establish a general capability ontology, external enforcement, legal standing, or substrate-wide access control.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/uehn4980"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-005"
   },
   "sha256": "cd318ed698c7ceb8230bfdd3aa25c6d3b6d680c7131a13593512e630e356d530",
   "evidenceLevel": "identity-verified open full text with exact PDF SHA-256 and page-bound mechanism evidence",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.96,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-262",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-262",
   "text": "K-NLPers operationalizes the core sequence in a bounded inference setting: multiple agents independently generate candidate responses, cross-review and refine peer outputs, and a separate judge selects the final response. This independently extends Kaal's pool design with an implemented three-stage protocol and evaluated multilingual task results. The source does not establish an economic validation pool, reputation or stake, legal finality, or strict role exclusion because producer agents also perform peer critique.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.semeval-1.410"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-004"
   },
   "sha256": "24ba008e2a942e4db344bf4118202af0690ad33d9e1192c3e22d4b73cd014446",
   "evidenceLevel": "identity-verified open full text with exact PDF SHA-256 and a page-bound protocol passage",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.97,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-261",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-261",
   "text": "AgentReputation makes reputation operational: its policy engine consumes context-specific reputation cards to govern task allocation, access control, and verification intensity; the framework requires slashable collateral and anchors evidence in a blockchain persistence layer no participant can unilaterally control. This independently extends Kaal's institutional design with a concrete three-layer agent architecture. The paper does not establish that every consequential action passes through one institution, that collateral is always reputation stake, or that the conceptual framework has been empirically validated.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3803437.3805579"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-003"
   },
   "sha256": "3f96212e284d7f1a5d26332408f18c4aba0e37a6bfeb37e18303d0aa79bcaf42",
   "evidenceLevel": "identity-verified open full text with exact PDF SHA-256 and two page-bound passages",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 1,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-260",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-260",
   "text": "Taddeo describes one-shot interaction as very common in distributed artificial-agent systems and argues that an agent's trustworthiness can be assessed from its past performance without repeated interaction with the same counterpart. This independently qualifies Kaal's stronger formulation: the source supports the prevalence of one-shot agent encounters and the need for portable performance history, but it does not establish that every agent interaction is one-shot or that one-shot interaction is literally universal by default.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11023-010-9201-3"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-002"
   },
   "sha256": "d632d6c2c7cb6a1cb4e7c5575406e98c78677363b53e37af0e5d9cedf7cf4185",
   "evidenceLevel": "identity-verified institutional-repository full text with exact PDF SHA-256 and page-bound quote",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 1,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-259",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-259",
   "text": "Hermoso, Billhardt, and Ossowski report that an open multi-agent-system coordination artifact records interaction performance, evolves roles, and helps agents—especially newcomers—select better partners than relying only on their own experience. This independently qualifies Kaal's iterability mechanism: persistent, shared performance history can improve counterpart selection when direct repeat interaction is scarce. The abstract does not test validation pools, reputation accumulation across pools, or whether repeated-game cooperation dominates.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/cmrw1560"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/7260278-001"
   },
   "sha256": "24fb8d274f58e9c851a10a84ebd7b5764d8cc6804ab814b2e116be4c20724163",
   "evidenceLevel": "live Crossref-indexed abstract",
   "reviewTier": "independent substantive scholarly-growth qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 1,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-258",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-258",
   "text": "The Impact of Venture Capital on Innovation states that, despite varying estimates, a dollar of venture capital appears on average to be three to four times more potent in stimulating innovation than a dollar of traditional corporate research and development. This supplies a comparative calibration relevant to Kaal's use of venture-capital dollars as an industry-level innovation signal. The abstract does not establish that all measured innovation is disruptive or that dollar flows alone identify regulatory need.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780195391596.013.0023"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2740477-036"
   },
   "sha256": "9fae5742e4158d63d534a18b0e4c120f7f44a9e1a065330fde5aa0cea875679e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-257",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-257",
   "text": "The Impact of Venture Capital on Innovation reports evidence, at least at the United States industry level, that venture capital has a substantial impact on innovation measured by patent counts. This independently agrees with Kaal's claim that venture-capital-backed companies drive innovation trends. The abstract does not extend the finding abroad, cover the 2005-to-2015 period specifically, or establish that every venture-capital-backed company causes innovation.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780195391596.013.0023"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-050"
   },
   "sha256": "4169cbe573408270ce0e491818c2e10e76c89ef20308c021d650a02426ee21e6",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-256",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-256",
   "text": "The Hedge Fund Industry: Structure, Size, and Performance states that hedge funds are often organized as private partnerships and resident offshore for tax and regulatory purposes. This supports the offshore-center premise underlying Kaal's claim that legislators faced incentives to avoid requirements that could drive hedge-fund business offshore. The abstract does not establish that any particular regulatory increase caused relocation or quantify the franchise-tax effect.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.5089/9781557757364.084.ch003"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1806252-024"
   },
   "sha256": "3a0a43eac5fa72d92f79ba8728d79bca45a68346580060ad456247ee55bd8046",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-255",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-255",
   "text": "Emergence of a new regulation: informational disclosure modalities in the hedge fund opacity world states that disclosing information previously known only to hedge funds can reduce failures caused by information asymmetry, while distinguishing disclosure forms and microeconomic from macroeconomic consequences. This qualifies Kaal's narrower claim that a public database limited to nonproprietary information may demystify the industry without resolving opacity-driven liquidity risk. The abstract does not show that public nonproprietary disclosure fixes liquidity risk or identify a sufficient disclosure modality.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3917/ecoi.123.0161"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2714974-034"
   },
   "sha256": "77a075d8423af31dfa0f588ea0551a5d218e3fbfb8d2d34952adeb1d8ebbecc2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-254",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-254",
   "text": "Event-Driven Hedge Fund Strategies states that event-driven hedge-fund trades should have identifiable catalysts and time frames, after identifying merger arbitrage, distressed securities, special situations, and activism as strategies in that category. This adds strategy-level context to Kaal's claim that Form PF separately classifies event-driven, distressed, and restructuring strategies. The abstract does not show that Form PF captures any particular catalyst or independently establish bankruptcy relevance.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190607371.003.0012"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2348463-021"
   },
   "sha256": "fa559f30b6e9e629ab9c1391378ea47b2b4123be88e9be346e3facbfc1994e49",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-253",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-253",
   "text": "Financial technology: Blockchain and securities settlement states that an enterprise adopting blockchain must update its business processes and maintain ongoing discussion with stakeholders. This independently supports Kaal's condition that decentralized infrastructure alone is insufficient and that structural and cultural changes in interdependent legacy systems must be worked through before application. The abstract does not identify which changes are sufficient or demonstrate successful deployment.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.69554/yejn7450"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3441904-026"
   },
   "sha256": "ec4beb01164be71a3970d0cccc34d7c7246921bea52aac9392acb413e8f1b116",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-252",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-252",
   "text": "The case for a ‘sovereign’ distributed securities depository states that, as of its reference point, no capital-markets infrastructure globally had implemented a commercially viable blockchain or distributed-ledger solution. This independently corresponds to Kaal's contemporaneous finding that blockchain-issued securities lacked widespread ownership and trading support on major exchanges. The abstract does not establish present-day deployment status or show that prototypes could not later become commercially viable.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.69554/wdcc3003"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3411110-018"
   },
   "sha256": "7ddf52b76745e730e5197ed3aec4fe7bfb4c911d89f1554e8a1deca1afad0712",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-251",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-251",
   "text": "The Legal Tender Closure Gap Doctrine states that securities law may properly apply when cryptocurrency is sold as part of an investment arrangement. This supports Kaal's typical-case distinction between investment-contract securities tokens and consumption-oriented utility tokens, while qualifying any inference that transactional utility alone places a token outside securities law. The abstract does not define investment arrangement, apply the Howey elements, or classify any particular token.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6861300"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4021599-007"
   },
   "sha256": "ee49f59f85777681b83182ef8a3e050204756b2e07841dc7fcdc8a0f8d4f3ba5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-250",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-250",
   "text": "From stock exchanges to asset exchanges states that a security token often represents ownership of an underlying real-world asset rather than serving only as currency. This adds an asset-backing characteristic to Kaal's broad digital-asset taxonomy. The abstract does not establish that all security tokens are asset-backed or supply a jurisdiction-specific legal classification test.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.69554/hxhu8608"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3606663-004"
   },
   "sha256": "d824b14bc90394670cf63b60e47ef670daaff656b0451d893a019ee756140364",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-249",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-249",
   "text": "The unique and complex considerations of digital asset custody reports that digital assets had matured as an asset class with increasing adoption among retail and institutional investors. This qualifies Kaal's custody-dependent account of institutional restraint by documenting adoption rather than categorical exclusion. The abstract does not identify the custody arrangements used, measure adoption, or show that institutions can participate without reliable custody solutions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.69554/kxek5506"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3936876-003"
   },
   "sha256": "0aaae73f0bd0c429c1de16b406b7009112a41dfadb1ea9648a8a780cd50f14c4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-248",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-248",
   "text": "Critical Phishing Defense Strategies and Digital Asset Protection states that cryptography is crucial to maintaining the security of sensitive information on digital assets. This supports Kaal's premise that digital-asset custody requires enhanced information-security infrastructure and controls. The abstract does not address custodial institutions, quantum resistance, or compare particular cryptographic, key-management, or storage controls.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4018/979-8-3693-8784-9.ch010"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3936876-028"
   },
   "sha256": "2aecd52d94efe31db2f88c8d7d5403c8212e6aa2008763f5acd1b3a4205df9a8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-247",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-247",
   "text": "Comparing Mutual Fund Governance and Corporate Governance states that United States public-corporation governance continued under an agency model with regulatory strengthening after Sarbanes-Oxley. This supports the post-2002 top-down regulatory-intervention premise in Kaal's claims. The abstract does not establish two distinct crisis-driven upgrades, seventy prior years of inactivity, or the effectiveness of the strengthening.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-8683.2006.00523.x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2273857-005"
   },
   "sha256": "c82f7dbf101fb86d915e90bd86ce9c1dfcebcd2d4fd8823cd4f84ba7a08ed911",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-246",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-246",
   "text": "The role of institutional investors in corporate governance states that separation of ownership and control causes problems described by agency theory. This supports Kaal's core agency-conflict mechanism. The abstract proposition does not establish the magnitude or persistence of those problems, the inevitability of monitoring costs, or the sufficiency of Kaal's proposed institutional response.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/hebz/9780198806769.003.0008"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808852-010"
   },
   "sha256": "77bba46922b5944c87dcc55fb8d6796ecbbb38cc4963e27d11705a8980227a1e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-245",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-245",
   "text": "Corporate governance 1 identifies separation of ownership from control as a corporate-governance concern because managers lack accountability to investors and society. This supports the agency-conflict premise in Kaal's claims. The abstract proposition does not establish that existing law can never resolve the conflict or that decentralized governance is the necessary remedy.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/he/9780198811831.003.0015"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808852-010"
   },
   "sha256": "a96bbcfb601c8ac2e61eb0f241e5ae2b0368634044bc0aeaf3c9796b4e4881f3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-244",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-244",
   "text": "Enhancing Tokenomics states that decentralized-exchange liquidity pools can enhance price stability, incentivize community participation, and foster long-term engagement. This supports the long-term-liquidity function in Kaal's fair-launch design. The proposition does not establish permanent locking, the proposed allocation percentages, or superiority across all market conditions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36676/sjmbt.v3.i1.54"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4015908-036"
   },
   "sha256": "f0a4f051423c2dc731de9efbf50c5b1b3e484bb7c34b63255750c5839dc73225",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-243",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-243",
   "text": "Enhancing Tokenomics states that token burning is used to create scarcity and drive value but is criticized for short-term focus and limited contribution to ecosystem growth. This supports Kaal's token-value-to-short-termism mechanism. The proposition does not establish the broader ethical and governance consequences or apply to every fungible-token design.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36676/sjmbt.v3.i1.54"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3799320-005"
   },
   "sha256": "b4f1e44bf33c4a87c98f9ac5279f89817a9de252837c9056b006b58ca46683a8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-242",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-242",
   "text": "Communicating tokenomics and monetary policy reports that tokenomics communications in blockchain-based virtual economies primarily serve fundraising and lack policy discussion. This supports the policy-capacity deficit in Kaal's claim. The result does not establish that every token designer lacks qualifications or that all central-bank functions must be reproduced.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ijfe.3046"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3249860-007"
   },
   "sha256": "727de81001f65e1d0db8f496029672f4310730a0e6ebc9c38702cc236c74c326",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-241",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-241",
   "text": "Code Is Law but Is Code a Contract? concludes that the Indian Contract Act requirements of offer and acceptance, consideration, capacity, free consent, and legality can each be satisfied in a smart-contract interaction when interpreted in light of blockchain architecture. This directly qualifies Kaal's disruption claim. The abstract does not establish equivalent treatment elsewhere or resolve flexibility and judicial-oversight concerns.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65393/ijlrv6i746"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-001"
   },
   "sha256": "33a25f0b639ad94699c3f9d16d914647465f5231e60b613abcfa94864cb3446d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-240",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-240",
   "text": "Code Is Law but Is Code a Contract? states that the legal enforceability of self-executing blockchain-code agreements remains fundamentally uncertain under Indian law. This supports the jurisdiction-specific enforceability-risk component of Kaal's claim. The abstract does not establish that such contracts are void or identify which formation, interpretation, condition, or remedy rules must change.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65393/ijlrv6i746"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2998033-004"
   },
   "sha256": "20af7b916918aa1a7fed1c79bbcad748875043051e45f5f38e24e18e67a1549d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-239",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-239",
   "text": "Smart Contract as a New Way to Conclude a Contract states that smart contracts allow direct counterparty transactions without banks, payment-system operators, or other transaction operators, thereby reducing costs. This corresponds to Kaal's intermediary-removal and transaction-cost mechanism. The abstract does not establish that all transaction costs disappear or that every smart contract is inexpensive to operate.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.17803/2311-5998.2022.92.4.143-150"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3405401-005"
   },
   "sha256": "0597a0562bcf8ae98810897443a803a4dcf2c35c95e5236eb1c8c10a82302643",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-238",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-238",
   "text": "Smart Contract as a Form of Civil Contract states that the legal status and legal regulation of smart contracts are uncertain. This supports the regulatory-uncertainty component of Kaal's claim. The abstract proposition does not establish the causes, jurisdictional extent, financial-services scope, or the particular legal reforms required.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.17721/1728-2195/2019/4.111-3"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-024"
   },
   "sha256": "830f0b7eb0141967d24bb515fa51b7a0d86408983be721478ec288017dc34486",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-237",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-237",
   "text": "Perlindungan Hukum atas Tidak Berfungsinya Proses Transaksi reports that smart contracts satisfy the elements of electronic agreements and remain valid under civil law when they comply with consensualism. This is a conditional counterpoint to Kaal's enforceability-risk claim and supports the possibility of coded-term validity. It does not establish validity without assent, in every jurisdiction, or for every coded term.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.32505/muamalat.v11i2.13231"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2998033-004"
   },
   "sha256": "80dfcfa9b37f74cfbdd9c554b4062701963930d461fc4464385161775cf87f12",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-236",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-236",
   "text": "Smart Contract as a Type of Contract under Russian Law identifies the absence of statutory definitions for smart contracts and distributed ledgers as part of a special-regulation problem. This supports the definitional-gap premise in Kaal's claim. The abstract does not establish that the gap propagates through every other part of the blockchain ecosystem.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.24833/2073-8420-2023-3-68-45-52"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4021599-001"
   },
   "sha256": "c8228cc2bf63e228f751d6f24a17591b0887c8577ac67b522d042da2f4538acd",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-235",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-235",
   "text": "CyberTrust AI reports documented smart-contract vulnerability losses exceeding $6 billion across DeFi ecosystems from 2020 through 2024. This supports the live failure channel and order of magnitude in Kaal's claim. The abstract does not establish the annual distribution of those losses or the stated one-to-two-percent insurance-premium range.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6908358"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5583610-036"
   },
   "sha256": "71720273702223480fe141d80b6d0dfb97127eb4f255bd6ef6fc101591b14083",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-234",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-234",
   "text": "SARMF: A Hybrid Framework for Smart Contract Vulnerability Detection states that smart-contract immutability and financial exposure make software defects particularly costly. This supports the immutability-as-vulnerability mechanism in Kaal's DAO claim. The abstract proposition concerns smart contracts generally and does not establish how difficult a particular DAO is to alter or quantify realized losses.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6671720"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-009"
   },
   "sha256": "828a76305e2c29ba808e75eb7f3943e58cdc9700a3496654479689a71688a4c4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-233",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-233",
   "text": "Enforceability of Blockchain-Based Smart Contracts states that enforceability under traditional contract-law frameworks continues to raise questions about formation, validity, performance, and dispute resolution. This supports the legal-fit risk in Kaal's smart-contract enforceability claim. The abstract does not establish that smart contracts are void, that every traditional rule fails, or which doctrinal adjustments are required.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.71317/rjsa.004.03.0751"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2998033-004"
   },
   "sha256": "44dadc87a022b731a104c6d1ade3e24dad066e3406a897b1966eded9e4154595",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-232",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-232",
   "text": "Don't Play with Gas: Relating Metrics with Smart Contract Deployment Costs defines smart contracts as computer programs running on blockchains that execute automatically and deterministically when predetermined conditions are met. This independently agrees with Kaal's computer-code and condition-triggered execution definition. The abstract does not establish legal enforceability or performance outside the stated technical model.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.22541/au.168536171.12781008/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227967-021"
   },
   "sha256": "c2c23497de136f1c7a7083db653df591053700f94861d1cc49b44dfc05a65273",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-231",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-231",
   "text": "Signing a Smart Contract states that blockchain and smart-contract analysis must still consider participating parties, rights and obligations, and the formation prerequisites of offer and acceptance. This qualifies Kaal's claim that automated immutable execution disrupts conventional formation doctrine by showing that the source continues to treat offer and acceptance as necessary legal prerequisites. The abstract does not resolve how doctrine applies across jurisdictions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36475/8.3.11"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-001"
   },
   "sha256": "684027ec1ddd2a0df33fc220ff45d68e1ecc4217bb9d491fba9e9adcfcc3b33f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-230",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-230",
   "text": "A hybrid blockchain and smart contract framework for resilient IoT security in smart homes identifies a tradeoff: centralized architectures create a single point of failure, while blockchain-only solutions introduce latency that limits real-time control. This qualifies Kaal's conditional claim that decentralized public blockchains can remove centralized-governance failures only when they are scalable and secure. The abstract concerns smart-home IoT control, not governance generally.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fbloc.2025.1707911"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-036"
   },
   "sha256": "b65446608fb35bc80271cf911c6c988aff1611bb3cf29a94aaad79c3121aa3a7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-229",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-229",
   "text": "Preserving Flow Table Integrity in OpenFlow Networks through Smart Contract states that storing OpenFlow rules in a distributed ledger protects them against malicious tampering. This independently supports the tamper-resistant-record element in Kaal's four-part reputation-infrastructure claim. The abstract concerns network flow rules and does not establish the verification, scoring, or non-transferability elements.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-3254673/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6607458-034"
   },
   "sha256": "c18b235c06a22649936f88c4acf6a516fdbe5a5540311cbb8c7607cbdf0edc33",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-228",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-228",
   "text": "A Smart Contract Vulnerability Detection Method Based on Heterogeneous Contract Semantic Graphs and Pre-Training Techniques states that smart contracts cannot be modified or revoked after blockchain deployment. This independently supports the immutability mechanism in Kaal's DAO-code vulnerability claim. The abstract does not establish that every deployed contract lacks an upgrade path or that a particular bug has appeared.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics13183786"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-009"
   },
   "sha256": "84ea02a7e379cf62662d1e8f334287dd86c02aba06432a6857fee412765f93af",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-227",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-227",
   "text": "EtherVote states that decentralized blockchain approaches provide security guarantees for voting platforms through transparency, immutability, and confidentiality. This independently supports the immutability-and-security guarantee mechanism in Kaal's claim. The proposition is limited to an e-voting design and does not establish universal necessity or principal-agent effects.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-4107818/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-019"
   },
   "sha256": "2428f5e996fcffe65e91fdda2d02f1c0354aa9537e3a783330cc390cba72c859",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-226",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-226",
   "text": "Contract-Graph Fusion and Cross-Graph Matching for Smart-Contract Vulnerability Detection states that smart contracts enable many blockchain applications but remain exposed to code-level defects. This independently supports the code-defect mechanism in Kaal's DAO failure claim. The proposition does not establish the DAO exploit, loss magnitude, or governance consequences of any particular defect.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202509.0978.v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2998033-007"
   },
   "sha256": "d8a4e39d9b6d15db425c80863f5a51410ee14275ecc45168ac58fb81d7a8381e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-225",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-225",
   "text": "Provably secure, smart contract-based naming services reports a second construction that preserves the first construction's security properties and is the only evaluated constant-storage version deployable on Ethereum mainnet. This Greek-language result supplies a bounded mitigation for Kaal's storage constraint. It does not remove broader throughput, redundancy, or computation limits.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.12681/eadd/46443"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782198-039"
   },
   "sha256": "b7f56f58b25a49fb2298894393c5aaedf4f3c1d5939d44392eb9162890886da7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-224",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-224",
   "text": "Provably secure, smart contract-based naming services identifies smart-contract storage size as the main implementation obstacle because stored information is the most expensive resource to access and modify and must be minimized for viability. This Greek-language abstract independently supports Kaal's blockchain-storage constraint. It concerns a naming-service construction and does not establish the full comparative-computation claim.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.12681/eadd/46443"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782198-039"
   },
   "sha256": "e3fd9a176b09160ca5ef0302b48f55d4330763a5bbd5a6b156111a7a8fecff6c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-223",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-223",
   "text": "Smart contract (contract) in family legal relations states that most legal systems have not yet adapted to blockchain technology. This independently agrees with the legal-adaptation lag underlying Kaal's narrower observation about American and European courts. The abstract does not identify particular jurisdictions, court decisions, or the degree of adaptation.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.24144/2788-6018.2026.03.1.39"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3002908-008"
   },
   "sha256": "718051387eebcf1a1ea2fe2e352e480497fd29a1f4a380dd343a0b82a53d6d49",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-222",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-222",
   "text": "Reducing smart contract runtime errors on the Ethereum blockchain states that Ethereum permanently stores every executed transaction, including failed transactions. This adds failed-transaction persistence to Kaal's eternal-storage premise. The retained proposition alone does not establish redundant storage across every node or the comparative cost claim.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.58837/chula.the.2020.135"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782216-003"
   },
   "sha256": "7b37ec67b457c2f452e38a76a980f7653d6879f9bd79b31d135820668099fec3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-221",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-221",
   "text": "BSC: A Bitcoin Smart Contract Implementation proposes integrating smart-contract functionality into Bitcoin so developers can build decentralized applications on that network. This adds a Bitcoin implementation path to Kaal's Ethereum-centered account of platform-level smart-contract application development. The abstract does not establish adoption, production readiness, or displacement of Ethereum's protocol effects.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.12043344.v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3067615-004"
   },
   "sha256": "05032d74cb1a9c24bf3912a3d182915bc998dc34beb4753d19de070a74805811",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-220",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-220",
   "text": "Digitization of Transaction Terms within TCE defines strong smart contracts as agreements with automatic execution and enforcement that are not reversible by courts. This independently supports the limited-legal-recourse mechanism in Kaal's claim. The retained proposition does not establish the frequency or consequences of failed execution.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.25300/misq/2023/17818"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3406323-031"
   },
   "sha256": "bd2c19e66661836d4880b107f69b66c2fe4b6e82766a4a50e814822052776e67",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-219",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-219",
   "text": "Human-in-the-Loop Blockchain Governance for Project Change Control states that smart contracts can automate change-control tasks including verifying need and authorization, implementing changes, and auditing the process. This extends Kaal's compliance-automation mechanism into project governance. The retained proposition does not establish that every task is deterministically codable or that automation should displace managerial discretion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.55737/trt/v-v.279"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4941807-019"
   },
   "sha256": "4fe11aacdf72e592291ba89f587d3894f20b70e51297c7367dd6a4871a87d713",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-218",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-218",
   "text": "Algorithmic Trading Regulation and Stock Market Liquidity reports that China's 2024 program-trading rules narrowed bid-ask spreads and reduced price impact in the A-share market. This adds a regulation-mediated spread mechanism to Kaal's observation that liquidity injection narrows spreads in traditional markets. The result is confined to China's A-share market and does not establish the same effect in crypto markets or from dual listing.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202607.1753.v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4033886-005"
   },
   "sha256": "b28c2d6a3881accb8165899840a706f12dd42532d47807c019997f974daef7f2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-217",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-217",
   "text": "Algorithmic ethics and European standards identifies the same institutional tradeoff as Kaal's legal-AI claim: voluntary private-sector self-regulation offers flexibility and rapid response to innovation but risks fragmented approaches and diffuse ethical responsibility. This independently supports the innovation-versus-systemic-coverage mechanism. The proposition concerns European algorithmic-ethics governance and does not establish the performance of any specific U.S. federal or state initiative.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.59295/spd2025s.16"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-025"
   },
   "sha256": "29edb64b6d5d50d096cdaa142e60ba991e0ec2d88e3dfd8b9bf1f2cd8430b9a0",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-216",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-216",
   "text": "A Framework for Responsive Market Regulation states that regulators must be able to change and sustain a responsive regulatory climate that permits innovation while preserving investor protection and monitoring the impact of change. This independently agrees with the dynamically responsive component of Kaal's shift from reactive to data-responsive regulation. The abstract does not specify Kaal's anticipatory data architecture or establish that every regulatory change preserves innovation and protection simultaneously.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198829461.003.0013"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-001"
   },
   "sha256": "0d12d510ebe53151f21b0ea32ac337d35f82d8a9ffe59129daf10dd1366b5dee",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-215",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-215",
   "text": "Characterizing Coin-Based Voting Governance in DPoS Blockchains reports decisive coin voters capable of altering committee-election outcomes and that almost half of coins were never used in those elections. This independently instantiates the concentration and passive-holder mechanisms in Kaal's criticism of fungible-token voting. The proposition does not establish a majority-holder attack, hostile takeover, or universal DPoS behavior.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1609/icwsm.v17i1.22225"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-030"
   },
   "sha256": "689d3ca9e74150e1d8e68f232b7bc9215cec4ea7dc04402024b42b84abf77168",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-214",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-214",
   "text": "Quadratic Voting and Member Voice in DC Pension Schemes states that quadratic voting captures preference intensity by quadratically allocating voting credits rather than treating all preferences equally. This independently corresponds to Kaal's preference-intensity rationale for quadratic-style microdemocratic allocation. The proposition alone does not establish outcome superiority or implementation feasibility in pension governance.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1017/err.2026.10120"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3709041-017"
   },
   "sha256": "35d364cdfddcdf39f6574b0562e70af49e5e2e99d9b277376ac8aac2a31383c5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-213",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-213",
   "text": "Balancing Power in Decentralized Governance explains that convex voting costs can discourage better-informed voters from adequately conveying private information. This qualifies Kaal's inference from vote concentration to greater issue knowledge and preference intensity. The proposition does not show that every quadratic design suppresses informed participation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2024.08469"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808867-015"
   },
   "sha256": "d4d64f79d2b81dd383b14136838e44037025a3fddc2c54f11345ddaf5a5074c7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-212",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-212",
   "text": "Balancing Power in Decentralized Governance finds that uncertainty breaks quadratic-voting optimality and can cause it to underperform linear voting. This directly qualifies the conditions under which Kaal's preference-intensity mechanism can improve outcomes. The proposition does not test Kaal's specific capped-vote implementation or identify every source of uncertainty.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2024.08469"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3709041-017"
   },
   "sha256": "9523f4437dbf87a5f2607fc53cfbc55e25ca8346062d172a57237bcedd94d9e3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-211",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-211",
   "text": "Balancing Power in Decentralized Governance reports that quadratic voting with convex costs optimally aggregates voter preferences and outperforms linear voting when voters have complete information. This independently supports Kaal's preference-intensity mechanism for quadratic allocation. The result is conditional on complete information and does not establish that Kaal's capped allocation is identical to the studied mechanism.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2024.08469"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3709041-017"
   },
   "sha256": "71d64138ab4acf72f0a0020efbc517f2984ed711da690e60497cef1b774e3ccb",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-210",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-210",
   "text": "Cumulative Voting: Investor Protection or Antitakeover? finds that Chinese CV adopters had better governance overall while also exhibiting tighter control through higher ownership concentration. This qualifies a simple equation of fewer defensive or control mechanisms with better governance in Kaal's U.S. cross-sectional claim. The proposition does not report higher valuation or performance and is limited to family-controlled listed firms in China.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/corg.12096"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5583610-002"
   },
   "sha256": "633af7293da92b1879da02c59a8342444e5db90be549704533936c66dcd28dc1",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-209",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-209",
   "text": "The Differential Effect of Regulatory Signals on Shareholder Dissent finds that a disclosure-rule change affected shareholder dissent differently as domestic versus foreign institutional ownership increased. This supplies an empirical example of institution-specific reactions to regulatory information relevant to Kaal's dynamic-rulemaking mechanism. The abstract does not establish that the regulator used the response as feedback or adjusted a later rule.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/corg.12640"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2273857-049"
   },
   "sha256": "0a4f9606d63d10eeaeb4cd0a84367c08c99af4bec295a61899af031842b6a057",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-208",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-208",
   "text": "Long-term value versus short-term profits finds a positive correlation between index ownership and share recall for proxy proposals involving compensation, director elections, and management-sponsored items. This supplies observed use of traditional shareholder voting control in Kaal's contrast with ordinary ICO investors. The abstract does not establish the direction of every vote or that recall alone changes governance outcomes.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/corg.12576"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3117224-005"
   },
   "sha256": "426a0e910c875f1ec4210db16e6ef5eabfc90e245c745f574dea1b9ba1dddaa6",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-207",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-207",
   "text": "Proxy favors reports that the reciprocity relationship between firms and institutional dual holders does not exist when confidential proxy voting is in place. This supplies a concrete governance-control mechanism for one conflict channel consistent with Kaal's broader principal-agent misalignment claim. The abstract does not show that confidentiality removes residual agency loss generally.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/corg.12557"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-009"
   },
   "sha256": "65604ce094f638508553250062c83a3fac57ae262291e984ecbf2d718ed5d749",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-206",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-206",
   "text": "Shareholder Voting and Directors' Remuneration Report Legislation states that the United Kingdom's 2002 rules gave shareholders a mandatory non-binding vote on boardroom pay. This supplies a concrete statutory example of the governance control available to traditional shareholders in Kaal's contrast with ICO investors. The proposition does not establish that a non-binding vote controls directors or promoters in practice.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-8683.2010.00802.x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3117224-005"
   },
   "sha256": "e18a3d4c171fd2c6182ab3299181cebbb4f0170bcdc99d781d958385b6cbec5e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-205",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-205",
   "text": "Impact of Restricted Voting Share Structure on Firm Value and Performance finds, in a ten-year Canadian panel, that RVS firms did not have lower value, operating performance, or stock performance than comparable non-RVS firms. This qualifies Kaal's U.S. cross-sectional claim that fewer defensive mechanisms correlate with higher valuation and performance. The result is jurisdiction- and structure-specific and does not show that RVS is harmless in every governance environment.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-8683.2010.00808.x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5583610-002"
   },
   "sha256": "49599f53137feac00d6bc3b1f24b4f04e070d6711bca5eb671b75970201c799a",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-204",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-204",
   "text": "Does proxy voting really promote corporate sustainability? reports that proxy-voting agents attribute inconsistent guideline compliance partly to portfolio managers not being aligned with their clients' stated sustainability commitments. This independently instantiates Kaal's manager-agent and shareholder-principal misalignment mechanism. The abstract does not quantify residual loss or establish that every proxy-voting failure is caused by misalignment.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/corg.12464"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-009"
   },
   "sha256": "5453870ef5e152c3573b6561f4efb65dfda883547cf04a3ab0105af0b456909c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-203",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-203",
   "text": "Governance Through Voting reports that directors at firms experiencing major environmental and social incidents receive 9.8 percent more negative shareholder votes. This supplies an empirical example of the director-election control available to traditional shareholders in Kaal's contrast with ICO investors. The abstract does not examine ICOs or establish that dissent alone changes firm conduct.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6265083"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3117224-005"
   },
   "sha256": "b75a1f1dce717d70fdc1273af2569c8706813973a38d44227a2dc9821453e71a",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-202",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-202",
   "text": "The Governance Gambit states that the GENIUS Act creates a federal prudential framework for dollar-backed payment stablecoins while prohibiting issuers from paying token-holder interest or yield. This qualifies Kaal's account of the Act as an enabling stablecoin framework by identifying a simultaneous statutory boundary. The proposition does not address merchant-issued structures or whether governance rewards satisfy that boundary.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6349800"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5454054-004"
   },
   "sha256": "c4b676b037bd31daca6a95f001a1da92fc5d5294f8047a4cf7377ea9723caaac",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-201",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-201",
   "text": "Formula-Constrained Economic Systems Without Governance Voting identifies participation asymmetry, governance capture, coercion, and long-term instability as structural limits of voting-based governance in decentralized digital systems. This is consistent with Kaal's narrower decision to leave the UDLC governance architecture unspecified. The proposition does not establish that no voting design can meet the UDLC requirements or validate a formula-constrained alternative.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6277238"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5887242-006"
   },
   "sha256": "2ab593f98f8e58cf82ef716db82e567a477add88b33ada7298c9f6a06a35728e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-200",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-200",
   "text": "The Geometry of Decision-Making in DAOs states that calling DAO token voting decentralized governance is a misnomer because most DAOs exhibit extreme centralization in token allocation. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that most current DAOs do not deliver their promised decentralized power. The proposition does not measure organizational communication or identify every cause of centralization.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6683938"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4529715-004"
   },
   "sha256": "175eaf8de7a04089ae093efd83eece83136b2902fa519a247797686875f8109f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-199",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-199",
   "text": "Auditing governance concentration beyond token allocation states that launch allocation, raw holder counts, and token-inequality metrics do not establish token-governance decentralization. This adds a live-control audit qualification to Kaal's claim that governance concentrated in a handful of foundation actors fails to produce decentralized governance. The proposition does not show that any single metric or launch structure determines legitimacy.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fbloc.2026.1853465"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4015908-023"
   },
   "sha256": "9310313c93dcb0f6328f65e5dfa75f03be75609cac74e6c53108c4aa78a9d8bf",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-198",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-198",
   "text": "Token-based Voting Systems states that concave transformations can map token holdings into votes while preserving participation incentives and compressing excessive voting power. This extends Kaal's non-linear microdemocratic allocation mechanism with a token-weight transformation. The abstract proposition does not establish equivalence to capped priority allocation or report comparative voting outcomes.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6269359"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3709041-017"
   },
   "sha256": "2ee933ad16a6024aa48380eb747d7937f03ab8fd00529bbd03537fb6fe1614f2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-197",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-197",
   "text": "Token-based Voting Systems states that standard token-vote aggregation can produce oligarchic dominance or voter apathy. This independently corresponds to Kaal's criticism that one-token-one-vote concentrates power in large holders and produces suboptimal outcomes. The abstract proposition does not quantify either pathology or establish that every token-voting design has it.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6269359"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-030"
   },
   "sha256": "2187df4ff0d708365cb4480fe5cfe6e98d52096cb5ec94460f322c5cf065ab57",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-196",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-196",
   "text": "Decentralized Autonomous Organization and Blockchain-Based Incentivization Framework for Community-Based Facilities Management proposes blockchain voting for transparent collective decisions about shared building upkeep. This operationalizes Kaal's process-centered DAO definition in a smart-building setting. The abstract reports prototype-oriented evaluation rather than comparative proof of decentralization or governance quality.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6751782"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-030"
   },
   "sha256": "3857812b57f49c97d64974a64afc821a8245dca05d9b3474f3cb9ba45095e3ba",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-195",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-195",
   "text": "DAO 3.0: The Harmony Framework states that jurisdiction-specific DAO integration is fragmented and often fails to address DAOs' global, cross-border nature. This extends Kaal's more specific account of jurisdiction-bound legal representation as a centralizing feature of existing DAO solutions by supplying the cross-border scope mismatch. The abstract does not test representation requirements or show that every jurisdiction-specific structure fails.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6904101"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-023"
   },
   "sha256": "6ccc8e18bfb96205ba04dc346debe51643209cd5f927a463147044e98029e4b1",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-194",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-194",
   "text": "DAO 3.0: The Harmony Framework identifies undefined DAO legal personality and potentially unlimited member liability as linked legal-structuring problems. This independently agrees with Kaal's distinction that a DAO may or may not have legal identity and with his condition that a favorable jurisdiction grant entity-level limited liability. The abstract does not establish how often these defects arise or validate the proposed framework.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6904101"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-031"
   },
   "sha256": "2cfb38f15e864ce0bf6c9963dd25c6b5ff19f9231f7561e3d85f742972be7d54",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-193",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-193",
   "text": "Decentralised Governance in Football states that blockchain-based DAO governance can enhance transparency and accountability in football clubs, alongside democratised decision-making and automated smart contracts. This extends Kaal's account of DAO accountability into a distinct organizational setting. The abstract does not isolate immutable recording as the causal mechanism or measure the claimed accountability gain.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.31585/jbba-8-1-(2)2025"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4900880-036"
   },
   "sha256": "8e88cb2925c01934fdaf422a91452fb94f472177743ae3f0f970575127da6f25",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-192",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-192",
   "text": "A Design Science Approach for Agentic AI in Network Engineering provides human-in-the-loop controls to maintain regulatory and organizational compliance in autonomous network management. This independently agrees with Kaal's maintained-human-oversight condition for agentic institutional design by instantiating human oversight as an operative compliance control. The abstract describes a network-engineering framework and does not measure compliance effectiveness or validate Kaal's phase-specific reputation architecture.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.176978431.15223796/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6244278-039"
   },
   "sha256": "ded3d18fe28e706a27a9c2d1fe3cddfb87b30ad56be43f786a29d3a1ac4e2297",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-191",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-191",
   "text": "Privacy-Preserving Smart and Secure Contract Solutions for Digital Supply Chain Payments states that blockchain smart contracts often expose sensitive transaction metadata, contractual terms, and risk indicators, limiting adoption in multi-party supply chains. This qualifies Kaal's transparency mechanism by identifying a concrete confidentiality and adoption cost of public contractual terms. The abstract does not test opportunism or establish that every smart-contract architecture exposes the same information.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9416.ijaibdcms-v6i4p127"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-026"
   },
   "sha256": "667db910bb019aa1da2b6ad056282f6cf06c09637f5515b7bd1d30f6871fc9a0",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-190",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-190",
   "text": "AI Agents in Payments identifies technical, legal, and societal risks including cybersecurity vulnerabilities, liability gaps, regulatory non-compliance, and economic disruption. This independently extends Kaal's monitoring-framework risk gap with a concrete taxonomy in the same payment-agent domain. The abstract proposition does not assess the monitoring framework itself or its scalability and adaptability omissions.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1017/err.2026.10103"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-011"
   },
   "sha256": "b1b9d6d2588b5e32e26d3dbe6d86434bb64a7fba1ee8ff3e336267b34a575d81",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-189",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-189",
   "text": "Explainable AI and Blockchain for Dual-Currency Payments states that high transaction fees, settlement delays, currency fluctuations, and restricted wallet access impair financial inclusion and income security for cross-border digital labor in Bangladesh. This supplies a jurisdiction-specific cost barrier consistent with the premise of Kaal's micropayment claim. The abstract does not attribute the costs specifically to centralized systems or establish that blockchain processing removes them.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-8004820/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5095633-026"
   },
   "sha256": "1d0cc91a826e4392be44ba3f4f9ee3824233c1a39b4128cd1fadd0551c2f3688",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-188",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-188",
   "text": "Integrating AI and Blockchain Technologies for Advanced Financial Systems states that blockchain provides immutable transaction records and decentralized verification. This independently agrees with Kaal's ledger-integrity mechanism for recording and verifying agent transactions. The abstract does not establish that every agent decision is recorded, that all records are publicly visible, or that immutability alone produces accountability.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.174961755.51277488/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-033"
   },
   "sha256": "598767c895ccc5cd8a49e6ed3b44f5c180b28c5ea8d9585532afeec818d224a8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-187",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-187",
   "text": "Agentic Recommendation Optimization states that AI systems increasingly evaluate information on behalf of humans and return a decision rather than merely presenting material for human evaluation. This supplies a concrete decision-stage account consistent with Kaal's distinction between humans as terminal consumers and agents as proximate drivers. The essay does not measure the prevalence of this shift or its economy-wide effects.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6284098"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6421319-005"
   },
   "sha256": "f9a6e550497e7917ebfc6ce2674def7e1590e2b3bdde3979c9b177e76163ba66",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-186",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-186",
   "text": "When Agentic AI Met the Common Law of Agency argues that the common-law definition of agency does not plausibly apply to agentic AI. This qualifies Kaal's framework-level representation mechanism: a Legal Identity can authorize delegated and automated action, but technical attribution does not by itself give an AI system common-law capacity as an agent. The abstract separately notes that apparent-authority doctrines may still attach consequences.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6838660"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "1fed238dfc7b04b781c05d1da7f6261ef1c9af940cfb0d9588c0126f83771616",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-185",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-185",
   "text": "Cross-Jurisdictional Compliance for Agentic AI Systems argues that cross-jurisdictional compliance fails when regulatory responsibility allocations do not align with the technical stack, so satisfying one stringent regime is insufficient. This independently agrees with Kaal's claim that one compliance design cannot scale across divergent jurisdictions. The abstract concerns agentic-AI responsibility in four anchor jurisdictions and does not establish the same rule for every LER deployment.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6822138"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5454054-039"
   },
   "sha256": "6b840ec42a78d31f8bdafdfd970f9e9ab881b4d7c31aa15b18378f502a56cb99",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-184",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-184",
   "text": "Cross-Agent Governance Alignment identifies alignment correctness, policy confidentiality, governance determinism, evidentiary sufficiency, composable security, and forward secrecy as essential properties for cross-organizational agent governance in regulated settings. This qualifies Kaal's real-time cryptographic-attestation claim with additional confidentiality and state-security requirements. The proposition presents design requirements rather than evidence that a deployed system satisfies them.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6299461"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886442-018"
   },
   "sha256": "e35ce6b9388e71ab15f1eb48d2844b98b98130234de5bcef5e7253cab891b078",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-183",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-183",
   "text": "From Rogue Employees to Rogue Agents states that AI-agent monitoring and insider-threat detection share a baseline-profiling and deviation-flagging architecture that encodes trust assumptions. This supplies a concrete monitoring architecture to which Kaal's training-bias and novel-deviation limitation applies. The proposition does not test either bias or detection of deviations outside the baseline.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6355658"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-028"
   },
   "sha256": "9e5127a1b3230b90fca839f422627fb11d3052a1acf0cde8f6b8e435db9d118c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-182",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-182",
   "text": "A Super-Agent-Based Framework for Reputation Management introduces a reward mechanism to induce super-agents to contribute resources, maintain reputation, form communities, and provide truthful reputation information. This independently agrees with Kaal's mechanism linking reputational governance incentives to mutually beneficial community participation. The abstract does not establish incentive compatibility against collusion or false reporting.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12026"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4067783-029"
   },
   "sha256": "e0826f7ffe0c366bd58ca16d2f1e2d263f7d6e498bf1b9eebf49c0169e4f819e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-181",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-181",
   "text": "A Super-Agent-Based Framework for Reputation Management states that super-agents can form communities and build service reputation from the opinions of members with similar interests and judgment criteria. This extends Kaal's community-standing account of reputation governance with a concrete peer-group formation mechanism. The abstract does not state how that reputation allocates decision power or how objectively it can be measured.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12026"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5254152-004"
   },
   "sha256": "113f3bea486ddf7c06d4678f6e54ef565ef25295e21c9b4f0753887d0a53e7c3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-180",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-180",
   "text": "A Super-Agent-Based Framework for Reputation Management assigns higher-capability super-agents the role of maintaining service-reputation information and sharing it with lower-capability consumer agents inside decentralized systems. This supplies a capability-tiered information-layer design that qualifies Kaal's requirement that reputation repositories and oracles themselves be decentralized. The abstract does not establish how the manager layer avoids concentrated control or manipulation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12026"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782205-036"
   },
   "sha256": "deeb54e4078ad83d5aab4e0f8a3e0ed2de5082ade5f1f9aaed9e4a15c153ddcd",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-179",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-179",
   "text": "Sustainability in Multi-Agent LLM System identifies significant energy consumption and environmental impact in multi-agent LLM systems. This independently supports the premise that energy infrastructure is a binding constraint on AI production and makes Kaal's infrastructure-level response relevant. The proposition does not test decentralization, open-source models, or antitrust as remedies.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4018/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch007"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6421319-012"
   },
   "sha256": "7743ef22b80fa4345bcb67171d4af4fa4b7c3479010f84bc0686cbb3f9d90f00",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-178",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-178",
   "text": "Drivers of AI-Powered Digital Banking Adoption states that trust, perceived value, and institutional-credibility concerns limit customer adoption of advanced AI banking features despite efficiency and personalization gains. This independently agrees with Kaal's claim that digitized trust does not stably substitute for institutional trust. The proposition does not establish that the same mechanism governs every platform or DLT business.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.64753/jcasc.v10i4.3714"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3266953-003"
   },
   "sha256": "582ce9a1e0afd3f6c8bde677d495375c5621e66241a695a1562043a9e9d1ba9d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-177",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-177",
   "text": "A Multi-agent Security Framework for AI-Assisted Software Development specifies seven specialized agents spanning threat modeling, secure design, code generation, testing, CI/CD, runtime security, and compliance. This supplies a concrete implementation instance for Kaal's critique of specialized AI monitoring services. The abstract proposition does not establish computational scalability or adaptation as agents and threats diversify.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9416.ijaibdcms-v7i1p105"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-022"
   },
   "sha256": "a8d8e96cec7b39739ae5e6afae7aca42f8730fa07435ff6475a21d3823b75421",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-176",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-176",
   "text": "Agentic AI for Enterprise Systems states that reactive, rule-based monitoring cannot adequately address operational complexity, cascading interdependencies, and compliance obligations in enterprise financial systems. This independently agrees with Kaal's critique of reactive monitoring and supplies financial-system complexity as a mechanism. The proposition does not by itself validate the proposed replacement architecture.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7061998"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-012"
   },
   "sha256": "2c857205dbdb4790ee528c43dab1bb3e191fa9dfaa7d51be35bc53563ced3ca4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-175",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-175",
   "text": "Enterprise AI-Enabled Multi-Agent Threat Hunting deploys Isolation Forest anomaly detection and user behavioral analytics in an autonomous threat-hunting framework. This is a concrete deployment to which Kaal's bias and novel-deviation limitation applies. The cited proposition does not show resilience to biased training data or previously unseen deviations.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.175825523.37911598/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-028"
   },
   "sha256": "920e00bba37fcdb7779952b8a139248d78d40ba9d251c12fee7c5c22f91c9661",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-174",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-174",
   "text": "LICET specifies per-event session keys, biometric temporal signatures, zero-knowledge proofs, and a hash-chained tamper-evident ledger as a concrete intent-attestation architecture. This extends Kaal's actual-versus-stated-intent distinction and real-time attestation claim with a physiological authorization mechanism. The abstract reports a design and deployment, not adversarial validation of coercion resistance.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7018458"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-026"
   },
   "sha256": "d95d698287977992ca4741798bf083d72579de8eccc254cfa77825ed8b97995d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-173",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-173",
   "text": "LICET states that consequential autonomous-agent authorization must verify voluntary intent in addition to identity. This directly qualifies Kaal's delegated-identity attribution mechanism and corresponds to his separation of actual Will from Stated Will. The abstract proposes an authorization requirement but does not establish its legal validity across jurisdictions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7018458"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "c65609dbc54460dc829d27b869e01411b6ab112ad39b5de3ec4487f91356df76",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-172",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-172",
   "text": "The Four Layers of AI Governance argues that autonomous-agent safety shifts from alignment of an individual model toward governance of agent collectives. This qualifies Kaal's treatment of objective-function governance as a foundational policy problem by identifying an additional collective-governance layer. It does not negate the need to govern individual agent objectives.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6891958"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6607458-030"
   },
   "sha256": "ad3097a3b1a27554be8409f6afe79649dd27b708ce68cc36b7ff371e2d2740a6",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-171",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-171",
   "text": "The Backbone Pattern specifies a graph substrate as a bounded coordination layer for topology, mandates, and traceability rather than as the repository for all agentic state. This extends Kaal's infrastructure-neutral settlement-substrate claim with a concrete separation-of-concerns design. The abstract does not test stake-weighted validation or Kaal's full computative-agent primitive.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6649179"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6655138-049"
   },
   "sha256": "2bbf3d299377907502724d3a1ea24bd53478f6df656d90234612264684c31a0e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-170",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-170",
   "text": "AgenticMemory identifies session-boundary memory loss as a mechanism by which accumulated agent knowledge fails to persist. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that episodic interactions and ephemeral evaluation prevent an agent's track record from reshaping later behavior. The source addresses memory persistence, not the separate incentive design needed to turn persistent history into reputational consequence.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6265798"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6244278-006"
   },
   "sha256": "5f7e86b62a5fd1dbc85a58db86cb0d6af8e771d5ab9e7c21e04f2e46a3b39c5b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-169",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-169",
   "text": "Creative Destruction or Structural Scarring? states that prior automation displaced routine labor while complementing cognitive work, generating a skill premium. This independently agrees with Kaal's mechanism linking routine-task automation to wage divergence in favor of high-skill non-routine work. The proposition is historical background and does not establish the paper's later claims about transformative-AI transition paths.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6776778"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4900880-021"
   },
   "sha256": "948d92e5d45098966f645d9280a9e9a9da585267cc7e777cb8b642255127dcda",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-168",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-168",
   "text": "FATA identifies tool orchestration, dynamic coordination, and framework interoperability as critical practical challenges for agentic systems and describes a framework-independent control plane. This extends Kaal's incentive-based account of interoperability throttling with an implementation-layer mechanism. The abstract does not establish that proprietary enclosure caused the engineering friction.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.175099921.10546764/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886442-032"
   },
   "sha256": "b657e74b56697bee149b7e7bb0ba2d124f2a5a3878aa1ea66f4f9381dbca5acc",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-167",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-167",
   "text": "Toward AI Agent Behavior Research identifies a lack of systematic frameworks for understanding how AI agents make decisions. This broadens Kaal's longitudinal judicial-AI evidence gap to agent-decision behavior more generally. The abstract proposition does not measure decision quality, backlog effects, trust, or long-term outcomes.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6162386"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-029"
   },
   "sha256": "0ac6d96c99bf4f1ac7bc0b1ff1217d5a9581fda15c6996fe1651fdfbc59715b1",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-166",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-166",
   "text": "Discrete Judgment Intermediate states that its AI-oversight architecture combines universal principles with jurisdictional overlays. This independently corresponds to Kaal's hybrid universal-baseline and locally tailored governance structure. The abstract does not establish that the overlays preserve innovation or outperform harmonized substantive rules.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6903578"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-036"
   },
   "sha256": "a70f696f7d20e6e33a3391ef7d89bd149969651c6910dddb7b7e4754f75f3e90",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-165",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-165",
   "text": "Decentralized multi-agent reinforcement learning based on best-response policies reports that most studies use fully centralized learning to ease transfer from single-agent systems. This extends Kaal's centralization critique beyond federated-learning aggregators by identifying ease of methodological transfer as another centralization pressure. The abstract does not establish the communication-load or vulnerability effects Kaal identifies.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2024.1229026"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4796714-028"
   },
   "sha256": "20b8e47dce0ce812060e9a36faf0c6e9f900783d33fa4756a0a7203bd03536f9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-164",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-164",
   "text": "The Verifiable Responsible Agent Framework states that loss caused by an autonomous AI agent may leave no liable subject because the model is not a legal person and the deploying operator may be too remote for liability. This independently agrees with Kaal's legal-personality gap and adds operator remoteness as a compensation obstacle. The abstract does not resolve liability allocation.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6963058"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-009"
   },
   "sha256": "a5aab1d0f95269e46b23a69d3035ba3cab221861e460d6c93262ef9dcfc15ba9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-163",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-163",
   "text": "Sovereign Agent Identity identifies an infrastructure asymmetry: institutions have agentic systems participating in consequential decisions, while affected individuals lack equivalent agentic presence or a way to be addressed as participants rather than subjects. This supplies an independent access problem to which Kaal's self-sovereign identity mechanism is responsive. The abstract does not show that verifiable credentials alone cure the broader imbalance.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6414818"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4941807-007"
   },
   "sha256": "221ce88a9b8ef4d713d02cf98f9cbe7cd5b720ae96bf6a722228465180237055",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-162",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-162",
   "text": "AgentLedger states that the reasoning chain producing an AI-agent action is simply gone in the deployment baseline it addresses. This qualifies Kaal's real-time attestation claim by limiting it to architectures that actually retain and attest decision traces. The abstract proposition does not show that such attestation is impossible.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6417378"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6421319-019"
   },
   "sha256": "36e33acfc2a5aff3b6e3b692088b326905ec265ce4d1a0aac8b13b53b3a927c2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-161",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-161",
   "text": "AgentLedger states that AI agents are being deployed in consequential settings faster than governance tools are being built. This independently corresponds to Kaal's governance-capability lag for evolving and deployed AI. The abstract does not establish that every legacy governance system lacks every required tool.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6417378"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4796714-003"
   },
   "sha256": "0c8f6871085054187e6467307503ef1a6ffc2d4621e749708c1086cf6cb18ab5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-160",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-160",
   "text": "Securing brand trust and reputation in an era of political polarisation and AI-driven disinformation identifies generative AI as an additional mechanism that lets malicious actors disseminate increasingly realistic disinformation at unprecedented scale to inflict financial and reputational harm. This extends Kaal's engagement-driven Web2 misinformation mechanism with a content-generation amplifier. The abstract does not isolate platform architecture as the cause or measure distrust in legal institutions.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.69554/pjfe5856"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4957318-007"
   },
   "sha256": "6bee8becf4382953bf2055c1b67088b4a55d3f85326dae00b13974e2738edce7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-159",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-159",
   "text": "Adversarial Auditing formulates AI-generated corporate-greenwashing detection as a three-player minimax game and reports theoretical analysis showing bounded training dynamics. This supplies a concrete theoretical adversarial-analysis method relevant to Kaal's resilience requirement. It does not replace testnet evidence against specified Sybil, collusion, or reputation-gaming participation levels.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6162666"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5225296-040"
   },
   "sha256": "c3269ed5377b641c93c864d4984f731f96871880b2492ee8ff6191cbb0875fb8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-158",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-158",
   "text": "Context Engineering: A Principal-Agent Theory of AI-Augmented Knowledge Work identifies costly, imperfectly observable, expertise-dependent context work as a mechanism creating moral hazard and screening problems inside firms. This extends Kaal's costly-monitoring account from ownership-control conflicts to human effort in AI-augmented knowledge work. The abstract does not establish that the same friction persists between artificial agents operating on fully attested substrates.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6506600"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3373393-003"
   },
   "sha256": "6e9b4cbabdbb77323a9b740929c5616918b9e377dba9c8f004a5aaceca53d9cc",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-157",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-157",
   "text": "Embedding ethical governance in personal AI systems states that personal AI can offer privacy and autonomy while shifting ethical, legal, and governance responsibility from organizations to individual users. This qualifies Kaal's individual-accountability design by identifying responsibility transfer as a burden as well as a governance mechanism. The abstract does not establish that individual allocation is fair, enforceable, or sufficient.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s43681-026-01193-1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4941807-006"
   },
   "sha256": "67bed115367242d68a8f243be82bce4b98afa712ebfee6f5d0b4ac5818f90117",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-156",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-156",
   "text": "PRAC3 argues that consent, credit, and compensation frameworks do not adequately address voice-identity risks created when context, authorship, and control separate. This supplies a voice-data instance of multidimensional governance limits, extending Kaal's critique of unitary reputation scoring across privacy, regulation, and ethical change. The abstract does not evaluate DcentAI, WDAG, or a replacement scoring architecture.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1609/aies.v8i3.36721"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5095633-038"
   },
   "sha256": "736e69d4eb0174acbff236f9d0644dfb9c7f4a309d032e10f1c4cd632655779d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-155",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-155",
   "text": "Separation of Execution, Optimization, and Governance in AI Agent Systems argues that conflating those functions creates self-reinforcing hallucinations, optimization drift, and forensically useless audit trails. This supplies a structural separation mechanism extending Kaal's warning about fragmented internal monitoring without external audit standards. It does not test Kaal's decentralized-governance design or show that separation alone resolves the failures.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6568638"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-019"
   },
   "sha256": "b612010da4a21b3ad62a5a6ac9c78493550ecfd20174931fdef7aabfcfb1781f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
