   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-087",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-087",
   "text": "Blockchain and Smart Contracts for Know Your Customer states that globally transmitted KYC information relies mostly on internal controls, producing slower and error-prone data management and governance. This independently corresponds to the slower and error-prone legacy-system component of Kaal's claim. The abstract does not quantify cost or demonstrate that blockchain performs better.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.47330/cbc.2021.jtvv4544"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3409548-023"
   },
   "sha256": "3fb483135d333444837edfb6f113a39536445c833beef4ae680ce1093f185ee5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-086",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-086",
   "text": "Smart Contracts in Blockchain Technology: A Critical Review states that decentralized anchoring of smart-contract scripts can make execution of predetermined procedures visible externally. This independently supplies a smart-contract-specific transparency mechanism corresponding to Kaal's broader blockchain-transparency claim. The proposition does not establish truth, trust, intermediary replacement, or effectiveness.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/info14020117"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-016"
   },
   "sha256": "d2a36f825973b995c33dd4708a9c0fb477650fbdf3d0c3dc4228f4eacd849b77",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-085",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-085",
   "text": "La revolución digital en el Código Civil peruano identifies normative challenges from integrating smart contracts into Peruvian civil law and calls for explicit legislative recognition. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that legal frameworks must adapt to growing smart-contract use. The abstract does not specify which reforms are sufficient and does not generalize beyond Peru.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.26439/iusetpraxis2025.n060.8291"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-024"
   },
   "sha256": "2f0590c2e628530dbabee3eff4027c084eb4d883087c41731bc6dff12f94256f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-084",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-084",
   "text": "An annotation assisted smart contracts generation method proposes an annotation-guided Char-RNN method for automatic smart-contract generation. This independently supplies a concrete generation-layer design corresponding to Kaal's distinction between execution and generation layers. The abstract does not test HDCA or establish that the generated contracts are legally or technically reliable.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-2977216/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6655138-005"
   },
   "sha256": "ef81f1e9af79846ade3636e6c6051e7a80042f123d0e892c693a2d565d371a63",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-083",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-083",
   "text": "To what extent can smart contracts replace traditional contracts in construction project? identifies translation of construction contracts into computer languages as a major challenge that remains uninvestigated. This independently corresponds to Kaal's coded-versus-natural-language translation burden. The source addresses the opposite translation direction and a construction-sector research gap; it does not establish overwhelming translation costs for courts or regulators.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1108/ecam-04-2023-0379"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3441904-031"
   },
   "sha256": "0952f64ed52893c6e9ac479c1479a0e513d21faaad246afc39a64bd38c9187c7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-082",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-082",
   "text": "Why Smart Contracts Reported as Vulnerable were not Exploited states that only a few reported vulnerabilities are exploited. This independently qualifies Kaal's identification of smart-contract vulnerabilities as a material loss channel by separating vulnerability prevalence from realized exploitation. The abstract does not dispute the size of losses when exploitation occurs or provide a denominator.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.21953189.v2"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4734750-003"
   },
   "sha256": "70b02bce33248469b19bb63ec0be36bd8bc62565477d564ead0dea4c5cc40ef1",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-081",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-081",
   "text": "Smart contracts are likely to have limited application reports that some governments have amended legislation to include blockchain transactions and that more must follow to provide legal certainty. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claims of regulatory uncertainty and partial legislative adaptation. The abstract does not identify the governments, provisions, or global sufficiency of those amendments.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1108/oxan-db258271"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-024"
   },
   "sha256": "a200650f38dc44043e6fec44bd54fac8fe4e9585a9905b660e00732f3b963a39",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-080",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-080",
   "text": "Advancing Blockchain Security states that formal verification is required to guarantee smart-contract accuracy and dependability. This supplies a concrete safeguard corresponding to Kaal's warning that contract simplicity alone cannot guarantee correct execution. The proposition does not show that verification succeeds or eliminates all bugs.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4883840"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3405401-020"
   },
   "sha256": "c0d474976ab6284eb4755503d3a07d190da35ed41c83ec52525eb2d69391870e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-079",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-079",
   "text": "Transformation of Real-World Contracts to Smart Contracts identifies the complexity of translating multilingual natural-language contracts into digital smart contracts. This independently corresponds to Kaal's broader coded-versus-natural-language translation burden. The source addresses the opposite translation direction and does not establish overwhelming costs for courts or regulators.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15071514"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3441904-031"
   },
   "sha256": "0d8e69925bd2e9f2af6947ec08fe175a3ab36733bf47b4b93685a6f9497560e3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-078",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-078",
   "text": "The Effectiveness of Echidna in Detecting Reentrancy states that smart-contract vulnerabilities, particularly reentrancy, have caused hundreds of millions of dollars in losses across Ethereum. This independently corresponds to Kaal's identification of smart-contract vulnerabilities as a material loss channel. The proposition does not verify Kaal's 2021 figure, cumulative global estimate, or growth conclusion.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.47191/etj/v11i05.60"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4734750-003"
   },
   "sha256": "c079094dfb716559d6e5b6840c377ba617281f8dc448da3c185d1cb26d629c08",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-077",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-077",
   "text": "Book-Smart, Not Street-Smart states that smart contracts privilege technical form while excluding the social contexts and complex human uses within which contracts operate. This independently corresponds to Kaal's criticism that code-is-law substitutes technical protocols for interpretive flexibility and normative deliberation. The proposition does not assess Kaal's proposed legal remedy.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.17351/ests2017.107"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-019"
   },
   "sha256": "a69ed6cac463caa211f7a1b47bf1691f41769a63da606c50198e3d41fb688a50",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-076",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-076",
   "text": "Book-Smart, Not Street-Smart argues that smart contracts rest on a thin conception of what law does despite features that may serve fairness. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that binary code execution omits legal systems' fairness and mitigation functions. The proposition does not establish the specific absence of appeal or recourse.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.17351/ests2017.107"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-020"
   },
   "sha256": "937aa5bedf17069e55d8edf61e993d792fdcd9fb9d7960c1b1fdf9e2e4c99547",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-075",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-075",
   "text": "Pre-Execution AI Gating for Smart Contracts attributes more than three billion dollars in stolen funds during 2025 to the preceding blockchain-agent mistake channel. This extends Kaal's dated evidence that smart-contract vulnerabilities create material losses. The amount is an abstract-level source assertion and does not independently verify Kaal's 2021 or cumulative estimates.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6267278"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4734750-003"
   },
   "sha256": "8482cec22674813aa83cb028d992b9a9aec9810f029079a17a24a0bd2e6640c8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-074",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-074",
   "text": "Pre-Execution AI Gating for Smart Contracts states that blockchain AI agents execute transactions in milliseconds and that their mistakes are permanent. This independently corresponds to Kaal's irreversibility mechanism for mistakes in code-is-law systems. The abstract does not identify the error type or establish that every blockchain lacks corrective authority.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6267278"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-021"
   },
   "sha256": "db1658d62de47b71d7214cdc0ca844eafe63682efb3b6f172b0eb16d971b4c7b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-073",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-073",
   "text": "Pre-Execution AI Gating for Smart Contracts states that AI agents are making financial decisions on blockchains without human oversight. This independently identifies a concrete setting for Kaal's requirement of a decentralized human backstop to code. The proposition does not establish that those agents lack transactional integrity or that Kaal's proposed backstop is effective.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6267278"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808873-042"
   },
   "sha256": "b803e40865788c5365220e8faa6ccb6458825ef0245a54536d5fefb0a66674a0",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-072",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-072",
   "text": "Smart contracts on blockchain for insurance and Takaful industry reports that insurance and Takaful companies using smart contracts experience fewer fraudulent claims, greater transparency, and automated claim payments with minimal human intervention. This independently extends Kaal's insurance-claims automation mechanism. The abstract does not establish Kaal's separate speed, accuracy, or administrative-cost conclusions.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21511/ins.15(2).2024.08"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5254152-007"
   },
   "sha256": "f4d59d663cca266a75f18b5d2722961ca2abb1344e0f96bf9f849c81b2e07875",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-071",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-071",
   "text": "Smart contracts on blockchain for insurance and Takaful industry reports that blockchain smart contracts can reduce claim-verification costs while increasing trust through a shared source of truth. This independently corresponds to Kaal's insurance-processing and intermediary-cost mechanisms. The abstract does not quantify the reduction or establish settlement speed and accuracy.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.21511/ins.15(2).2024.08"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5254152-007"
   },
   "sha256": "81c180316cf638e8ed0b274fa18c69cfec1c710952ea13d22cabcc9149011e3f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-070",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-070",
   "text": "Legal Compliance Protocols for Blockchain Smart Contracts proposes routers for real-time monitoring and automated legal enforcement within a hybrid legal-technical infrastructure. This supplies a concrete design response to Kaal's criticism that infrastructure-level permissioning lacks real-time enforcement across distributed nodes. The abstract presents a concept, not validated deployment or effectiveness evidence.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.172954185.58874599/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-016"
   },
   "sha256": "cfde8d3c42d6453df4cf8baffd7cd12d24d0c05382ee1eb22c5f9b6de9ebaa1b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-069",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-069",
   "text": "Legal Compliance Protocols for Blockchain Smart Contracts states that blockchain applications can execute protocols mandated by legal authorities to increase conformity, transparency, and accountability. This independently corresponds to Kaal's mechanism of coding regulatory conditions into smart-contract execution. The abstract does not quantify supervisory cost reductions or establish that coded protocols guarantee complete compliance.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.172954185.58874599/v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2992962-025"
   },
   "sha256": "659ad7522300a81a6e5a37944ec84ad88676463078bb1e9e8c81dec0b2f4bb23",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level extension",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-068",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-068",
   "text": "Background of Financial Technology and Smart Contracts reports that FinTech smart-contract research concentrates on security, scalability, legal-regulatory, privacy, and latency challenges, with proposed solutions still far from effective. This independently corresponds to Kaal's identification of regulatory uncertainty around blockchain and smart contracts, while showing that the uncertainty sits among broader technical constraints. The proposition does not establish that legal frameworks worldwide must change.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.32545/encyclopedia201911.0007.v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-024"
   },
   "sha256": "1960483c550d22dab0ba7628563be28c32bc8055bf7e79a5a20bae5e6ad602cd",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-067",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-067",
   "text": "Blockchain e contratos inteligentes no direito privado internacional argues that self-executing agreements in decentralized virtual environments challenge classical contract-law categories, including freedom of contract and clause interpretation. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that smart contracts disrupt foundational contract doctrine. The proposition does not specify offer, acceptance, consideration, immutability, or comparative common- and civil-law effects.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.18634/incj.28v.1i.1611"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-001"
   },
   "sha256": "4c3900ee87f8f8983da76a64a969bbfc2b6654a93b694d5b9a17edbfc67ba7a4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-066",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-066",
   "text": "From stack to structure reports that Ethereum's general-purpose EVM enabled rapid development of a decentralized-application ecosystem implemented through smart contracts. This independently corresponds to Kaal's account of Ethereum as a platform on which developers can build applications directly. The proposition does not establish that most developers chose the EVM or that ICOs obtained a uniform protocol.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.12681/eadd/62071"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3067615-004"
   },
   "sha256": "1c44e6de1373fd887be31e79828bb35a9721eb79e3df349a4d2421180d9b053e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-065",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-065",
   "text": "The Dark Side of Smart Contracts states that smart contracts may govern transactions more cheaply than traditional contracts, firm hierarchies, and relational contracts. This independently corresponds to Kaal's Coasean account of smart contracts as a lower-cost substitute for firm organization. The proposition is qualified and does not establish minimal costs for transactions of every size or high transaction security.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4905724"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3441904-011"
   },
   "sha256": "494abb52d0a4cd4065d1d8f11ead95c0f8361df008bd2545b0526e1294f313c9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-064",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-064",
   "text": "An ontology-driven approach to security analysis of Sui Move smart contracts argues that mature Ethereum tools often detect syntactic vulnerabilities while missing divergences between intended design and on-chain implementation, leaving functionality open to manipulation. This independently corresponds to the implementation-flaw mechanism in Kaal's account of the DAO failure. The proposition does not study the DAO incident or its reported loss.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.12681/eadd/62314"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2922176-035"
   },
   "sha256": "ce0e9547c66d4f2e6bab3004a30dbfe401fd88a2941f777a93de527064d8a3f5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-063",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-063",
   "text": "An ontology-driven approach to security analysis of Sui Move smart contracts states that attackers exploit smart-contract vulnerabilities to steal large sums and that defensive methodologies have developed in response. This independently corresponds to Kaal's identification of smart-contract vulnerabilities as a material loss channel. The abstract proposition does not verify Kaal's 2021 or cumulative dollar estimates, or establish a growth rate.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.12681/eadd/62314"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4734750-003"
   },
   "sha256": "d4da773313709b0743976e83bbd5f756aad90f2d808cba30aa32ce0b1243cd36",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-062",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-062",
   "text": "Beyond Traditional Contracts reports that the legal status of smart contracts in Indian commercial law remains doctrinally fragmented. This independently supplies an India-specific instance of the regulatory uncertainty Kaal identifies around blockchain and smart contracts. The proposition does not establish the condition of financial-services law globally or prove that every jurisdiction must change its framework.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65393/ijlrv6i1011"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227933-024"
   },
   "sha256": "00c2d464ebfd000ad9352cc9820507a91b53e927bc180790f958e0d6c66d5cf0",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-061",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-061",
   "text": "AI Tokenomics and the Firm describes generative AI as lowering the cost of prediction, language, and code generation. This independently corresponds to Kaal's marginal-cost-collapse mechanism for abundant digital realizations. The abstract proposition does not establish that marginal cost reaches zero, that supply becomes horizontal, or that pricing shifts entirely to generative capacity.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6692978"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6607458-016"
   },
   "sha256": "bcadf0ed1b653feceeea4c4144796146076b0599adf037d921319b6ac4b8d237",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-060",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-060",
   "text": "Quantitative Cooperation Analysis among Cross-chain Smart Contracts reports that numerous cooperating smart contracts form a complex interaction network that is difficult to evaluate. This independently corresponds to Kaal's premise that multi-party smart-contract arrangements create distinctive complexity, while qualifying the suggestion that blockchain infrastructure alone makes the arrangement sufficiently verifiable. The abstract does not establish whether the proposed evaluation method is unhackable.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.14583399"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2959730-021"
   },
   "sha256": "06e343cdb257b0ec161187c38655166da86f4619e95abf9a94b55f527a4e3eb8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-059",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-059",
   "text": "On Observing Contracts identifies a semantic gap through which smart-contract behavior can diverge from intended deontic behavior. This independently supports the mechanism in Kaal's claim that code and natural-language logic differ in ways that can frustrate human intent. The abstract proposition does not establish that this divergence occurs across all smart-contract settings.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3233/978-1-61499-935-5-21"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3405401-010"
   },
   "sha256": "ba3b68359d9e4f90ab63edfd125b5ac1f4943dc7e5985a1849dff9b052612e38",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-058",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-058",
   "text": "All Smart Contracts Are Ambiguous argues that technical facts can depend on socially determined facts, so smart-contract code can remain ambiguous. This independently qualifies the idea that code eliminates interpretive uncertainty and corresponds to Kaal's concern about how courts interpret coded agreements. The proposition does not specify Kaal's reasonable-human interpretive test or a particular legal doctrine.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.31228/osf.io/v37zd"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2992962-017"
   },
   "sha256": "181e14f342eb1c1089505504aae54699812e7de70e510ce692acc72a28a5d605",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-057",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-057",
   "text": "Adaptive Tokenomics argues that token projects lack an established field integrating economics, control theory, game theory, and constitutional design. This independently corresponds to Kaal's claim that productive decentralized networks require dynamic design, checks and balances, and game-theoretic incentives. The proposition is a field-level diagnosis, not evidence that any particular design keeps a network productive.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6364158"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782205-008"
   },
   "sha256": "086497ec52aa3e0ff24dd523eecbbc7cdf40ec1f864266ed4fabef2f76113891",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-056",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-056",
   "text": "Do Smart Contracts Incur Higher Transaction Costs than Traditional Contracts? argues that adapting institutional arrangements can cause smart contracts to incur higher transaction costs than traditional contracts. This directly challenges Kaal's proposition that smart contracting removes almost all transaction costs. The abstract frames the result as a possibility grounded in transaction-cost economics and does not provide an empirical cost estimate.",
   "responseType": "contradiction",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4491727"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3405401-006"
   },
   "sha256": "8aa15de087343bdd281f78c2e81f29b835ef7be2c7597375ef7c12f8ac9f52ac",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level contradiction",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-055",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-055",
   "text": "End-to-End Smart Legal Contracts argues that smart contracts need a firm private-law foundation before regulation is considered. This independently corresponds to Kaal's sequencing claim that contract-law doctrine must be adjusted before smart contracts are fully accommodated. The abstract proposition does not identify which formation, interpretation, conditions, or remedies rules require adjustment.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780192858467.003.0003"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2939127-027"
   },
   "sha256": "42c8fda414efec3beec544ae9008b0f07cae3670c7cfbe3ed2b3f0916f9f27e3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-054",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-054",
   "text": "Interpretation of Contracts and Smart Contracts distinguishes deterministic computer language, in which one meaning and result are contemplated, from natural language, which remains open to multiple meanings. This independently supports the language-logic mechanism in Kaal's claim about smart-contract interpretation. The proposition does not by itself establish that smart contracts cannot accommodate human business interaction in every setting.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.54648/erpl2018054"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3406323-032"
   },
   "sha256": "fe684ffcf3147f85381189e07c7666901a7e6428874052df7a68bb33db833a88",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level agreement",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-053",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-053",
   "text": "Smart Contracts as Execution Instead of Expression argues that human-readable contractual terms, interpretation, and contract-law correction help make execution conform to party expectations. That independently corresponds to Kaal's insistence on human-readable terms and interpretive flexibility alongside code. The abstract proposition does not establish Kaal's proposed Codex rule or the specific common-law mutual-understanding standard.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780192858467.003.0010"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5554218-031"
   },
   "sha256": "251f7183520df2af259ffbd689bed70af68635418fc626901da3843486b7c880",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-052",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-052",
   "text": "Legal regulation of artificial intelligence: international experience argues prospectively that European AI and robotics standards will support industry development while ensuring human-rights compliance. That assessment qualifies Kaal's warning that strict AI regulation may stifle innovation by presenting standards as potentially development-supporting. The proposition is normative and forward-looking, not an observed industry effect, and it is not limited to legal AI applications.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.32837/11300.33094"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-026"
   },
   "sha256": "eb82f5afe2bdda2c97190e63c75b5f8c2158c1ecfb44740caddee1b92f07413c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-051",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-051",
   "text": "Artificial Intelligence Governance and Regulation states that the more lenient United States approach promotes innovation but produces legal ambiguity. This independently corresponds to the lenient-regulation side of Kaal's two-sided legal-AI governance tradeoff. The source identifies ambiguity, not unchecked bias, as the cost, so it qualifies rather than duplicates Kaal's consequence and does not establish causation from the abstract alone.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.20473/jgar.v5i2.65194"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-026"
   },
   "sha256": "b11334ca3027282d9681632497b7d25c5707c6e126d8654b2d461b616874d236",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-050",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-050",
   "text": "Artificial Intelligence Governance and Regulation reports that stringent EU-style regulation can improve legal clarity and public trust while raising compliance costs and hindering innovation. This corresponds to the strict-regulation side of Kaal's two-sided legal-AI governance tradeoff and qualifies it by identifying claimed benefits alongside innovation costs. The abstract calls the support empirical but does not expose the design or permit causal inference.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.20473/jgar.v5i2.65194"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-026"
   },
   "sha256": "440355e0ab6949250662233f179acb698b6e0db21a822daa4a8534eac4c876c3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-049",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-049",
   "text": "Artificial Intelligence Regulation: Approaches and Implications argues that rapidly evolving AI technology and threats require specifically adapted regulation. This supports the pacing diagnosis in Kaal's claim that static frameworks cannot keep pace with technological innovation. It does not establish that dynamic autonomous governance is the necessary remedy, and the abstract supplies no comparative evidence about centralized and decentralized approaches.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.62768/adjuris/2024/1/10"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6192998-003"
   },
   "sha256": "1e13f5e7da6559d3d73c4c1b554c4fd414435188e0e94b98e01e4b724a22b13e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-048",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-048",
   "text": "Legal Regulation of Artificial Intelligence argues that an AI application should be classified as high risk when it can significantly affect individuals and prohibited when incompatible with fundamental rights. This independently corresponds to Kaal's account of fundamental-rights exposure as the basis for high-risk treatment under the EU AI Act. The abstract proposition is broader than judicial proceedings and does not establish the specific transparency, bias-audit, or human-oversight requirements in Kaal's claim.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.29039/conferencearticle_66c472706831a3.12783599"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-024"
   },
   "sha256": "db29bfceefd1bea1df89164788ac703b52c9334b2f397c2d63667533a46a583e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-047",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-047",
   "text": "No accounting for corporate governance identifies a concrete proxy-voting failure: voting rights were not tracked when securities were lent, leading to improper vote counts. This extends Kaal's requirement that voting systems count votes robustly and transparently by showing that entitlement tracking is part of accurate counting. The evidence concerns corporate proxy voting, not quadratic voting or DAO manipulation.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1108/18325910910986990"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4529715-019"
   },
   "sha256": "ceaf9c4b9a913e09b4b5acd608c8b5399ce6d65bfb39542dc599c509e9e6c6c9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-046",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-046",
   "text": "DAO and tokenized governance reports that Italian law leaves limited room for tokenized corporate participation and that the Fintech Decree introduces centralizing effects. This provides a jurisdiction-specific extension of Kaal's claim that fitting DAOs into existing legal frameworks can introduce points of centralization. The abstract does not identify the precise representative mechanism Kaal describes and does not support generalization beyond the Italian setting.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.82018/9791221183467-18"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-001"
   },
   "sha256": "e15f5168dc5d9a35c87c915a4f9502bd90cbb1dcc2189610ed2a9e49a2f40e01",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-045",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-045",
   "text": "DAO and tokenized governance treats decentralization as a measurement problem and proposes criteria based on minimum independent decision centers and the relationship among protocol, users, and developers. That framework qualifies Kaal's hierarchy-centered account of decentralization by identifying additional dimensions that a metric may need to capture. The proposition is conceptual and normative, not an empirical validation of either metric.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.82018/9791221183467-18"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808859-025"
   },
   "sha256": "1c8c03eab87527dea65af9f4edd8401dfce9e27d8a8686f4f9638aaf535b800f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-044",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-044",
   "text": "Bridging Social Capital and Financial Incentives identifies unresolved legal responsibility and speculative governance-token dynamics as obstacles to DAO trust, participation, and legitimacy. This independently corresponds to Kaal's source-bound catalog of unresolved DAO legal questions and extends it by stating participation and legitimacy consequences. The abstract does not resolve the legal questions or quantify the token effects.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-03273-7_6"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2939127-032"
   },
   "sha256": "73a6e7a46a3115cd02af6e4e09d06e0deace239c73635bcb1c12278ffcc97285",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-043",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-043",
   "text": "A meta-analysis of corporate governance and firm performance reports that stronger governance indices and greater board independence are associated with higher firm performance. That finding qualifies any broad reading of Kaal's claim that director independence did not produce effective risk monitoring in major U.S. financial failures. The meta-analysis proposition addresses firm performance, not risk monitoring under information concealment, and it does not by itself establish causation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.22495/jgrv9i1art2"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1558614-020"
   },
   "sha256": "4ce37b264f91b935d709741ac0ea24672aba3b20670f0a4c4f306d83ae9b376b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-042",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-042",
   "text": "DAO as Anthropological Experiment identifies the conventional explanation of DAO failure: poor mechanism design, insufficient incentives, and inadequate participation tools. Those failure mechanisms overlap with Kaal's emphasis on incentive alignment and his warning that bad DAO governance can create more risk than it mitigates. The proposition establishes the literature's baseline diagnosis, not a causal result, and the article's subject-model account may qualify that baseline.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6797979"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-019"
   },
   "sha256": "17b7a58c249e56236985c7aa4fd0cfe60dbe842927dad12a060b3ccd52f04a12",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-041",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-041",
   "text": "Institutional Voting in the UK: Is Mandatory Voting the Answer? argues that compelling institutions to vote is unlikely to produce informed voting, even though informed voting is necessary for effective governance involvement. This is a narrow example consistent with Kaal's skepticism that top-down corporate-governance mandates produce genuine change. It does not establish the broader claim for other reforms or prove management hostility.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-8683.00038"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2922176-009"
   },
   "sha256": "6e637a4363c5308c06359f18627468f908720fc077373c7a9e0489218ee3f5c8",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-040",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-040",
   "text": "An Empirical Evaluation of Quadratic Voting Counterfactuals in Token-weighted Governance states that the dominant one-token-one-vote rule lets capital ownership translate directly into DAO governance power. That mechanism agrees with Kaal's source-bound account of one-token-one-vote as allocating disproportionate power to large token holders. This proposition alone does not measure attack incidence, participation equality, or the performance of reputation voting.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7103878"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3652481-030"
   },
   "sha256": "bfd1d8e59b7e97ee30d9cede5dfeb6a22bbf77fe1937078b28bf6558cab6c332",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-039",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-039",
   "text": "Smart Contract-Based Decentralized Energy Trading Platform with AI-Enhanced Decision Support instantiates blockchain as a decentralized, immutable transaction record for peer-to-peer energy exchange. Its simulated deployment supplies a concrete application of Kaal's descriptive blockchain claim. The system is permissioned and the abstract does not establish public-chain decentralization, universal security, or blockchain provenance for the AI module's decisions.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6240059"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4021599-002"
   },
   "sha256": "41f5076dafa27133457897b59094495ae1eba476354b508ede1382601f508798",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-038",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-038",
   "text": "Token Economy 2.0 independently states that generative AI is pushing the marginal cost of information production toward zero. That matches the directional component of Kaal's source-bound claim about intelligence and digital goods. The same source reports pressure on trust mechanisms and greater information asymmetry, and it expressly says its cross-sectional staking results are not causal, so the relationship does not validate Kaal's broader economic conclusions.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6690839"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886442-011"
   },
   "sha256": "78d542d3797b4b1ed6582f779809d10f667b068cd451119f1b62e6f79d048b46",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-037",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-037",
   "text": "A Three Tier Architecture for Decentralized Insurance Governance identifies another structural weakness in decentralized insurance: existing protocols either reproduce centralized parameter authority or treat critical actuarial inputs as externally fixed. This extends Kaal's collateralization and actuarial-compatibility critiques by locating the governance problem inside parameter formation. The paper is theoretical and does not validate Kaal's proposed reputation mechanism or report market deployment outcomes.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6114506"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3949098-001"
   },
   "sha256": "cf1fe36f12500a3a245ffa856bb870d755ad1ace7ba86aba7d522161557540c9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-036",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-036",
   "text": "Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs) as New Entrepreneurial Firms argues that DAOs can reduce traditional agency costs and improve transparency while creating new coordination, participation, and systemic risks. The proposition supports Kaal's account of DAOs as a challenge to agency-based organization, but it also reinforces his warning that governance without effective mechanisms can become slow and inefficient. The abstract synthesizes studies and theory; it does not establish causal effects.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6455658"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3411897-008"
   },
   "sha256": "e5fa37b1c47918fff8171593c042a45b32ceffce1e2946196fd2affdfffa8457",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-035",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-035",
   "text": "Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs) as New Entrepreneurial Firms independently characterizes DAOs as blockchain-enabled organizational forms with decentralized governance, token incentives, and automated smart-contract execution. That description agrees with Kaal's source-bound account of DAOs as governance structures instantiated in software and smart contracts. The abstract is conceptual and does not establish performance, legal status, or complete decentralization.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6455658"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3406323-007"
   },
   "sha256": "623ee84195ff2f6493b23a3b1faa01ac5599cc0b37b28bdc14332a3880d8d8b3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-034",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-034",
   "text": "Enhancing Land Governance Through Decentralized Land Record System on Blockchain proposes a smart-contract system to make ownership records secure, transparent, and verifiable. The design supports auditability, but its abstract does not establish that transparent records alone align incentives, induce correct behavior, or eliminate corruption. Kaal's source-bound limitation therefore qualifies the proposal rather than contradicting its narrower recordkeeping contribution.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4018/979-8-3693-6557-1.ch008"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782214-013"
   },
   "sha256": "34ed21cc08e9951305bd479668dc31dacc150af703cd58978cbf93510a093556",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-033",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-033",
   "text": "Design and evaluation of a decentralized urban governance system with embedded AI and blockchain-enabled IoT supplies a concrete architecture in which embedded AI proposes governance actions and blockchain smart contracts validate execution. This is consistent with Kaal's prediction that AI and blockchain will supply governance solutions. The validation is technical and controlled, expressly excludes real-world policy implementation, and does not establish feasibility for corporate boards, so the relationship is a narrow qualification.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.3389/frsc.2025.1623412"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2922176-034"
   },
   "sha256": "8d18eb90c44b0e43b8562a41aa0549c5bb80cc7358978197103bd856cecca635",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-032",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-032",
   "text": "Decentralized AI-Powered Apartment Governance System Using Blockchain applies cryptographic hashing to complaints and related actions so the resulting record is transparent and tamper-evident. That is a concrete implementation of the record-integrity mechanism Kaal describes. The abstract does not establish that transparency by itself prevents corruption or motivates correct conduct, so the evidence supports traceability while preserving Kaal's aligned-incentives limitation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.22214/ijraset.2026.79053"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227967-019"
   },
   "sha256": "1482d16df165f51150192d524280c6a014ec618d04002a5972ba9d0654377c57",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-031",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-031",
   "text": "From Product Search to Preference Articulation: The Economics of Agentic Commerce independently states that generative AI is moving digital commerce toward agentic search in which consumers delegate product discovery to AI agents. This supports the proximate-agency shift identified in Kaal's source-bound economics claim. The abstract does not establish that humans cease to be the proximate driver throughout the agentic layer or that traditional demand-side economics loses descriptive accuracy, so the relationship is a narrow qualification rather than full validation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7208880"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6421319-005"
   },
   "sha256": "f6832504abd1feaeaf9aec9e9e106af3e443e8b64f0da77742985e7f8ed071ee",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-030",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-030",
   "text": "Trust and Reputation in Markets independently identifies reputational incentives as one way to address trust problems in economic transactions. This supports the cooperation mechanism in Kaal's business-reputation claim. The source also lists long-term relations and institutional regulation as alternatives, so it does not establish Kaal's stronger comparative proposition that reputation is the proper incentive rather than money.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190494087.013.20"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782210-001"
   },
   "sha256": "b8d74bc0ad5cf6c6fcb0654ade9a88d7ddfaab3ba23b219f64a78e8c51a3f7d4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-029",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-029",
   "text": "Reputation and Gossip in Game Theory independently states that past behavior can qualify an actor for reward and cooperation or for punishment and revenge. This supports the durable-record incentive mechanism in Kaal's reputation-governance claim. The source does not independently establish non-fungibility, DAO scope, or a positive-sum outcome, so the correspondence is limited to the incentive mechanism.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190494087.013.12"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3799320-032"
   },
   "sha256": "a56eeff1f50f53806bde850cafcb4573bfce5c5d3f6a488ae100dac336bd84a1",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-028",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-028",
   "text": "Reputation and Gossip in Game Theory states the baseline proposition that past behavior may guide future behavior. Kaal's source-bound claim adds an important scope condition: in economic networks where real money and sockpuppet creation are present, historical good behavior cannot be assumed to prove future good behavior. The source establishes the baseline signal, while Kaal's claim qualifies when that signal is unsafe to treat as proof.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190494087.013.12"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3266953-032"
   },
   "sha256": "0526cfebb71193fc1bd660d29eaf030e23a6f5fcaa47e3bdd34ac97b57c9bd1d",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-027",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-027",
   "text": "Gossip and Reputation in Social Dilemmas independently states that individuals help their group when reputation pays off later. This directly corresponds to Kaal's mechanism of reputation as a future-oriented commodity that converts present cooperation into a repeated incentive structure. The source supports that mechanism but does not independently establish Kaal's specific contracting architecture.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190494087.013.11"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782193-032"
   },
   "sha256": "e589042178e6979c53eea7e1c1e50f8c3f358946f168e0b453edb8631999ab83",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-026",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-026",
   "text": "Reputation systems and recruitment in online labor markets reports that reputation systems can produce winner-takes-all dynamics in which reputation differences become disconnected from objective ability. This independently identifies the distortion that Kaal's proportional reallocation and transparent-comparison mechanisms are designed to address. The study does not test either Kaal mechanism, so the source qualifies the problem statement rather than validating the proposed solution.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s42001-020-00072-x"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6192998-028"
   },
   "sha256": "2d1990600eb4715b10e3512921b2a5185cd6bace802bad746b2fa08733b773f7",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-025",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-025",
   "text": "On the Reputation of Agent-Based Web Services independently states that selfish services can alter public reputation by obtaining fake feedback. That mechanism supports the manipulation vulnerability underlying Kaal's Web-of-Trust and sockpuppet claims. The abstract does not establish Kaal's specific claims about mutual-validation growth rates or draining all system value, so it supplies a bounded qualification rather than full validation.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v24i1.7516"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3782210-008"
   },
   "sha256": "2d5d57e8ad38965b5c841a63fc225eacaab93923ae472e1b507cc7263c3e490b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-024",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-024",
   "text": "The indexed abstract of Reputation independently describes social reputation as a mechanism for diffusing cooperation, altruism, and norm-abiding behavior. That source-stated mechanism supports the behavioral premise of Kaal's claim that reputation can sustain cooperation. The abstract does not independently establish Kaal's trustless-system scope or his comparison with purely stake-weighted consensus, so the relationship is limited to a qualification of the mechanism component.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.4018/978-1-60566-236-7.ch017"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5225296-011"
   },
   "sha256": "dc93f70c1ebf4f5cf50f689c8fca736d838c0444447b88a601b64b26dba4276c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.62,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-023",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-023",
   "text": "Justin Malonson independently identifies an authorization-and-accountability gap for autonomous AI agents: existing models provide limited means to constrain delegated authority, prove policy compliance, and attribute machine-initiated actions. This qualifies Kaal's source-bound warning that infrastructure-level permissioning does not by itself bridge AI autonomy and accountability. The external abstract supports the general limitation but does not independently establish Kaal's specific claims about smart-contract exploits, permission conflicts, or absent real-time enforcement across distributed nodes.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.14293/pr2199.004107.v1"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-016"
   },
   "sha256": "d377fa802b5c49f2d65226e23a8662a2b69cb13a59564e5120455c190c557123",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-022",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-022",
   "text": "Weikang Li, Dong-Ling Deng independently support Kaal's source-bound position through Quantum delegated and federated learning via quantum homomorphic encryption. The indexed proposition states that by incorporating quantum homomorphic encryption schemes, we present a general framework that enables quantum delegated and federated learning with a computation-theoretical data privacy guarantee. This bears on Kaal's claim that decentralizing data processing across secure nodes, using techniques such as federated learning and homomorphic encryption, circumvents the privacy and security exposure of centralized data management and lowers breach risk. The external work uses federated learning and homomorphic encryption to supply a formal data-privacy guarantee, directly supporting the privacy-preserving mechanism identified by Kaal. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1017/qut.2025.2"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5095633-017"
   },
   "sha256": "2ed7f7146ecd999daaaaabf2b60771e71d72c6cbd33435c3e42ba05674291d67",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-021",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-021",
   "text": "Xiaoyang Liu independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Navigating the AI Energy Challenge: A Sociotechnical Framework and Strategic Solutions for Sustainable Artificial Intelligence. The indexed proposition states that artificial intelligence is at the intersection of innovation and escalating energy demands. This bears on Kaal's claim that centralized AI systems excel at scaling defined work but struggle to cultivate genuine discernment without consequential feedback, and they face escalating constraints in data scarcity, energy demands, regulatory scrutiny, and interconnect bottlenecks. The external work independently identifies escalating energy demand as an AI constraint; it does not address the other data, regulatory, and interconnection constraints in Kaal's compound claim. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1051/shsconf/202521801025"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6244278-034"
   },
   "sha256": "ed7a9c145718be76e65508e260a7ec3c7be73dd7d8dddaffa14df183cd18224c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-020",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-020",
   "text": "Geoff Lundholm independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Agentic Proxies: Governance, Accountability, and the Architecture of a Trustworthy AI Economy [Working Draft -June 2026]. The indexed proposition states that it is a different and more trustworthy class of actor: one whose actions are attributable, whose authority is traceable, and whose failures are governable. This bears on Kaal's claim that representation is built into the framework: one Legal Identity can authorize another as its agent to create or change legal statuses and acts on its behalf by using that identifier, which is how delegated and automated action is attributed to a principal. Attributable actions and traceable authority state the same accountability objective as Kaal's principal-bound representation mechanism; the external proposition does not establish legal personality. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6952119"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "d2fc0ddabeca254e5d6d7ada42eb2d5c834ed100b8e5359d7e2bcf02d2624c00",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-019",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-019",
   "text": "Bin Chen, Zehong Cao independently support Kaal's source-bound position through HLG: Bridging Human Heuristic Knowledge and Deep Reinforcement Learning for Optimal Agent Performance. The indexed proposition states that training an optimal policy in deep reinforcement learning (DRL) remains a significant challenge due to the pitfalls of inefficient sampling in dynamic environments with sparse rewards. This bears on Kaal's claim that deep reinforcement learning demands large amounts of training data, which suggests its algorithms differ fundamentally from human learning, and learning without supervision becomes particularly hard when rewards are sparse, as they typically are in sequence generation tasks. The external work independently identifies inefficient sampling and sparse rewards as a central deep-reinforcement-learning challenge, matching Kaal's stated sparse-reward limitation. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/yyyd1686"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-014"
   },
   "sha256": "9ae7cc9b90378ce9353678b31617041d596bf43e0a0ff37bad18fd72571e12b5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-018",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-018",
   "text": "Antony John Prabhu J extends Kaal's source-bound position through Blockchain-Anchored Audit Trails for Neuro-Symbolic Urban AI: Immutable Accountability for Autonomous Decisions in Smart City Infrastructure. The indexed proposition states that the seven smart contracts implement decision submission, symbolic rule version hashing, neural-symbolic conflict detection, governance voting for rule amendments, citizen decision trace queries, counterfactual incident replay, and cryptographically signed regulatory audit export. This bears on Kaal's claim that an immutable blockchain log of transactions and modifications inside AI systems lets stakeholders trace the lineage of an AI decision back to its original data inputs, which makes errors easier to identify and correct. The proposed contracts operationalize decision lineage through rule hashes, conflict detection, trace queries, replay, and audit export, extending Kaal's general lineage mechanism. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9262.ijaidsml-v6i4p134"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4941807-018"
   },
   "sha256": "562593c74711ba9c4516414e7d970d9fbcfa33d91d3d7d59c37fd5c76e3929fd",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-017",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-017",
   "text": "Heechang Ryu, Hayong Shin, Jinkyoo Park independently support Kaal's source-bound position through REMAX: Relational Representation for Multi-Agent Exploration. The indexed proposition states that training a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) model with a sparse reward is generally difficult because numerous combinations of interactions among agents induce a certain outcome (i.e., success or failure). This bears on Kaal's claim that deep reinforcement learning demands large amounts of training data, which suggests its algorithms differ fundamentally from human learning, and learning without supervision becomes particularly hard when rewards are sparse, as they typically are in sequence generation tasks. The source reports that sparse rewards make multi-agent reinforcement learning generally difficult because many interaction combinations can generate an outcome, supporting Kaal's broader sparse-reward limitation. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/bjtx8963"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-014"
   },
   "sha256": "54a57bd35e1a0b331b351ea447342f9ba07c9048964666aeda8ccfbc84375e7e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-016",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-016",
   "text": "Antony John Prabhu J qualifies Kaal's source-bound position through Blockchain-Anchored Audit Trails for Neuro-Symbolic Urban AI: Immutable Accountability for Autonomous Decisions in Smart City Infrastructure. The indexed proposition states that this paper proposes a blockchain-anchored audit trail architecture for neuro-symbolic urban AI, comprising seven interoperable smart contracts deployed on a permissioned blockchain that capture every autonomous decision produced by a neuro-symbolic smart city AI as a structured, hash-chained, publicly auditable on-chain event. This bears on Kaal's claim that infrastructure level permissioning neglects critical risks such as smart contract exploits, bugs, and permission conflicts, and proposes no real time enforcement across distributed nodes, which weakens its claim to bridge AI autonomy and accountability. The permissioned-blockchain architecture is a concrete attempt to bridge autonomy and accountability, but its abstract does not resolve Kaal's stated smart-contract exploit, permission-conflict, and distributed-enforcement risks. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9262.ijaidsml-v6i4p134"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-016"
   },
   "sha256": "8c4619804b0a2179be5962e07b1ce89c2808e06c88abb776f05b747e63f6dbc2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-015",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-015",
   "text": "Vikram Dham independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Who Sent This Agent? Cryptographic Organizational Identity for the Autonomous Agent Economy. The indexed proposition states that fiat payment rails provide accountability but cannot support micro-transactions — minimum fees of ~$0.30 make sub-dollar payments uneconomical, and human authorization requirements are incompatible with autonomous operation. This bears on Kaal's claim that the high intermediary fees of centralized fiat payment systems make those systems economically viable only at higher transaction volumes, which creates barriers to entry that decentralized payment systems do not face. The external proposition independently identifies minimum fiat-rail fees as making sub-dollar autonomous payments uneconomical, directly supporting Kaal's transaction-fee barrier mechanism. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6470738"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3406323-015"
   },
   "sha256": "83401104a699cf535794d87e0a9316410161c7282d2d2d920423a91c1f7cb83e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-014",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-014",
   "text": "Yue Yang, Bernd Meyer, Frits de Nijs independently support Kaal's source-bound position through Using Assistance Rewards Without Introducing Bias: Overcoming Sparse Rewards in Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning. The indexed proposition states that reinforcement learning agents may fail to learn good policies when their reward function is too sparse. This bears on Kaal's claim that deep reinforcement learning demands large amounts of training data, which suggests its algorithms differ fundamentally from human learning, and learning without supervision becomes particularly hard when rewards are sparse, as they typically are in sequence generation tasks. The external proposition states that reinforcement-learning agents may fail to learn good policies when rewards are too sparse, directly supporting Kaal's sparse-reward condition. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/ngix7900"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-014"
   },
   "sha256": "0ce6ed549c2bb3cb7db64bba5b928a50e31258a80763caa72732791cd9b3a11f",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-013",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-013",
   "text": "Antony John Prabhu J extends Kaal's source-bound position through Blockchain-Anchored Audit Trails for Neuro-Symbolic Urban AI: Immutable Accountability for Autonomous Decisions in Smart City Infrastructure. The indexed proposition states that the neuro-symbolic AI architecture, which combines symbolic safety rule enforcement with neural perception to produce interpretable multi-domain urban decisions, generates structured decision records encoding the symbolic rules invoked, the neural confidence scores, the sensor input state, and the action taken records that are substantive enough for blockchain commitment to support genuine accountability rather than opaque hash logging. This bears on Kaal's claim that an immutable blockchain log of transactions and modifications inside AI systems lets stakeholders trace the lineage of an AI decision back to its original data inputs, which makes errors easier to identify and correct. Structured records of rules, confidence, inputs, and actions provide the substantive lineage needed for Kaal's traceability mechanism rather than relying on opaque hashes alone. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9262.ijaidsml-v6i4p134"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4941807-018"
   },
   "sha256": "b5a1bafe05bfd46adb219a4ade461366dc67661cfb66db42d6bd5fc2de1e58f4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-012",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-012",
   "text": "Geoff Lundholm independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Agentic Proxies: Governance, Accountability, and the Architecture of a Trustworthy AI Economy [Working Draft -June 2026]. The indexed proposition states that it is AI-to-AI delegation-the transfer of authority, mandate, and responsibility across chains of autonomous agents that act at machine speed, across vendor boundaries, without continuous human involvement. This bears on Kaal's claim that representation is built into the framework: one Legal Identity can authorize another as its agent to create or change legal statuses and acts on its behalf by using that identifier, which is how delegated and automated action is attributed to a principal. The source identifies authority, mandate, and responsibility moving across AI-agent delegation chains, directly matching Kaal's representation problem while not itself specifying a legal-identity implementation. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6952119"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "3bdeb267fe92331ec20c6c1639a79ed63c965a9cc002bb29d323866a8740c8ed",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-011",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-011",
   "text": "Rizwan Tanveer independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Explainability, Transparency, and Accountability in AI Systems. The indexed proposition states that first, post-hoc explainability methods, including LIME and SHAP, are operationally available but do not guarantee the faithfulness, stability, or comprehensibility of their explanations, and are themselves subject to adversarial manipulation. This bears on Kaal's claim that post hoc explainability techniques do not by themselves establish trustworthiness; the explanations they produce must additionally be verified against human knowledge. The external work independently identifies faithfulness, stability, comprehensibility, and adversarial-manipulation limits in post-hoc explainers, supporting Kaal's warning that explanation output alone is not trustworthiness. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6767278"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-012"
   },
   "sha256": "8ca785374e43facba4c5aab2b8e5d502b5e9c6703ca2b4783ecab750e2f6cd00",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-010",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-010",
   "text": "Grace Ndlovu, Samuel Johnson qualify Kaal's source-bound position through Agent-Based Machine Learning Frameworks for Autonomous Predictive Decision Systems. The indexed proposition states that artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), and autonomous intelligent systems are transforming predictive decision-making across industries such as manufacturing, healthcare, finance, transportation, cybersecurity, and smart cities. This bears on Kaal's claim that the combined total addressable market of the industries disrupted by AI, counting healthcare, finance, retail, manufacturing, logistics, transportation, and customer service, is likely in the hundreds of trillions of dollars. The external source independently identifies the same broad set of industries undergoing AI transformation, supporting Kaal's market-scope premise but not his total-addressable-market magnitude. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.67228/3142788x/ijmlpa-2025pii6e3d"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4755632-004"
   },
   "sha256": "26cdd51775b989d685e8f114d2d7dc73b3408f7524d4ac08d0a1b59f68d72c50",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-009",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-009",
   "text": "Geoff Lundholm qualifies Kaal's source-bound position through Agentic Proxies: Governance, Accountability, and the Architecture of a Trustworthy AI Economy [Working Draft -June 2026]. The indexed proposition states that none establish whether a delegated action remained continuously authorised across a multi-agent workflow. This bears on Kaal's claim that representation is built into the framework: one Legal Identity can authorize another as its agent to create or change legal statuses and acts on its behalf by using that identifier, which is how delegated and automated action is attributed to a principal. The source identifies continuous authorization across multi-agent workflows as an unresolved requirement, qualifying Kaal's representation mechanism with a continuity condition. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6952119"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "03485e2f1650077e83f761dc2f226cbf09b04b58e65554d8e8dd5ce845156bd9",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-008",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-008",
   "text": "Jennifer She, Jayesh K. Gupta, Mykel J. Kochenderfer independently support Kaal's source-bound position through Agent-Time Attention for Sparse Rewards Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning. The indexed proposition states that sparse and delayed rewards pose a challenge to single agent reinforcement learning. This bears on Kaal's claim that deep reinforcement learning demands large amounts of training data, which suggests its algorithms differ fundamentally from human learning, and learning without supervision becomes particularly hard when rewards are sparse, as they typically are in sequence generation tasks. The source independently characterizes sparse and delayed rewards as a learning challenge; Kaal's claim additionally addresses data demand and sequence generation. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/ybxd5829"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-014"
   },
   "sha256": "829f90a8fb98255c5c05fef312f79d4deadedebcba477d99d5c68bacd6b6042b",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-007",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-007",
   "text": "Geoff Lundholm extends Kaal's source-bound position through Agentic Proxies: Governance, Accountability, and the Architecture of a Trustworthy AI Economy [Working Draft -June 2026]. The indexed proposition states that an AI agent operating under explicit, bounded, verifiable delegation is not a constrained version of a fully autonomous agent. This bears on Kaal's claim that representation is built into the framework: one Legal Identity can authorize another as its agent to create or change legal statuses and acts on its behalf by using that identifier, which is how delegated and automated action is attributed to a principal. Explicit, bounded, verifiable delegation extends Kaal's legal representation mechanism with operational constraints for consequential AI agents. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6952119"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5886342-014"
   },
   "sha256": "8d504992ce6d6b264b9ab7765cf74bfd4b155a2aacff70efeff7ed251925ddd6",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-006",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-006",
   "text": "Bin Chen, Zehong Cao extend Kaal's source-bound position through HLG: Bridging Human Heuristic Knowledge and Deep Reinforcement Learning for Optimal Agent Performance. The indexed proposition states that our proposed HLG outperforms PPO and PROLONET with at least 25% improvement in training efficiency and exploration capability based on MinGrid environments with sparse reward signals. This bears on Kaal's claim that deep reinforcement learning demands large amounts of training data, which suggests its algorithms differ fundamentally from human learning, and learning without supervision becomes particularly hard when rewards are sparse, as they typically are in sequence generation tasks. The reported training-efficiency improvement is a proposed mitigation within sparse-reward environments and therefore extends, rather than negates, Kaal's underlying limitation. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/yyyd1686"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-014"
   },
   "sha256": "abdf8d9c0de720732ed80a93d620211069cdcab242f4e384c0f43cc3c5f552c3",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-005",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-005",
   "text": "Antony John Prabhu J extends Kaal's source-bound position through Blockchain-Anchored Audit Trails for Neuro-Symbolic Urban AI: Immutable Accountability for Autonomous Decisions in Smart City Infrastructure. The indexed proposition states that the architecture satisfies the EU AI Act Article 19 mandatory logging requirements for high-risk AI systems, the GDPR Article 22 right to explanation for automated decisions, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework governance function, while providing a citizen challenge mechanism and a tamper-proof governance record that centralized smart city AI architectures cannot offer by design. This bears on Kaal's claim that reputation infrastructure adequate to computative exchange requires four elements: verification against stated objectives, a tamper-resistant accessible record, a mapping from verifications to a predictive reputation score, and a non-transferability constraint, which distributed-ledger systems satisfy by construction. The source extends tamper-resistant accessible records into regulatory logging, explanation, challenge, and governance functions; it does not implement Kaal's complete reputation-score architecture. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9262.ijaidsml-v6i4p134"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/6607458-034"
   },
   "sha256": "93dc43a07541845c8030f784624624e72f16dffab7791adc440d3f68ec8be9d5",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-004",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-004",
   "text": "Anudit Nagar, Cuong Tran, Ferdinando Fioretto qualify Kaal's source-bound position through Privacy-Preserving and Accountable Multi-agent Learning. The indexed proposition states that this paper addresses these challenges and presents Privacy-preserving and Accountable Distributed Learning (PA-DL), a fully decentralized framework that relies on Differential Privacy to guarantee strong privacy protection of the agents data, and Ethereum smart contracts to ensure accountability. This bears on Kaal's claim that infrastructure level permissioning neglects critical risks such as smart contract exploits, bugs, and permission conflicts, and proposes no real time enforcement across distributed nodes, which weakens its claim to bridge AI autonomy and accountability. The proposed privacy-preserving, smart-contract-accountable architecture is a concrete response to the accountability gap Kaal identifies, but the abstract does not evaluate smart-contract exploits, permission conflicts, or real-time enforcement. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/rxtl1109"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-016"
   },
   "sha256": "0fe3a3933286a27199ef37c4d017c33aa7e61d44c1a6f51f6553b1c50827b0e2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-003",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-003",
   "text": "Andrea Agiollo, Luciano Cavalcante Siebert, Pradeep K. Murukannaiah, Andrea Omicini qualify Kaal's source-bound position through From large language models to small logic programs: building global explanations from disagreeing local post-hoc explainers. The indexed proposition states that however, to the best of our knowledge, a thorough comparison among available explainability techniques is currently missing, as well as approaches for constructing global post-hoc explanations leveraging the local information. This bears on Kaal's claim that post hoc explainability techniques do not by themselves establish trustworthiness; the explanations they produce must additionally be verified against human knowledge. The reported absence of thorough comparisons and global post-hoc synthesis reinforces the incompleteness of explanation output, while it does not independently establish Kaal's human-knowledge verification requirement. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10458-024-09663-8"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5541658-012"
   },
   "sha256": "39a725603e43de39890bd9bebf6d11fc193ec2139c462b164a4cc4a5237ae59e",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-002",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-002",
   "text": "Antony John Prabhu J independently supports Kaal's source-bound position through Blockchain-Anchored Audit Trails for Neuro-Symbolic Urban AI: Immutable Accountability for Autonomous Decisions in Smart City Infrastructure. The indexed proposition states that autonomous AI systems making real-time decisions over traffic management, energy dispatch, and emergency services in smart city infrastructure exert direct and consequential influence over citizen welfare, yet current smart city AI architectures provide no tamper-proof record of what the system decided, which rules or neural pathways drove the decision, and whether the decision record has been modified after the fact. This bears on Kaal's claim that recording every AI agent decision and transaction on a blockchain produces a permanent tamper proof record that enables public verification and fosters accountability, because no single entity can alter the historical record. The source identifies the absence of tamper-proof AI decision records as an accountability defect, directly supporting Kaal's blockchain-record mechanism. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "agreement",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.63282/3050-9262.ijaidsml-v6i4p134"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-033"
   },
   "sha256": "301271ada3d433cb7fdfa0d8c43e38ad3c61565b82b95943997bf1e62fc361d2",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-08-001",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-08-001",
   "text": "Matteo Baldoni, Cristina Baroglio, Olivier Boissier, Roberto Micalizio, Stefano Tedeschi extend Kaal's source-bound position through Engineering Business Processes through Accountability and Agents. The indexed proposition states that we claim that an explicit representation of accountability and responsibility relationships can increase the robustness of such systems by guiding and systematizing both design and development. This bears on Kaal's claim that internal monitoring by AI agent developers and owners is fragmented and unreliable because there are no auditing standards against external benchmarks and no accountability mechanisms for deviations such as insider manipulation or third party agent risk. The external source proposes explicit accountability and responsibility relationships as a robustness mechanism, providing a design response to Kaal's finding that fragmented monitoring lacks accountability standards. The response is limited to the indexed proposition and does not imply review of the full external work.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-08",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.65109/lysk8282"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/5245185-019"
   },
   "sha256": "3c0515fdf0ed7731d06b4670a8b6faac70e4ad6a826c5711bf4d688b0c889c70",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.5,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-02-002",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-02-002",
   "text": "Maas independently supports the pacing premise in Kaal's critique of static AI-governance frameworks. The chapter's central thesis is that global AI-governance architectures must adapt so they remain robust to ongoing and future changes in technology, law, and international politics. This aligns with Kaal's claim that predefined rules cannot keep pace with AI development, while Maas states the adaptive prescription rather than directly measuring the failure of static frameworks.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-02",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/9780191988455.003.0001"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4796714-001"
   },
   "sha256": "2c70bf73a439f05fca76893cf4ab1c4501659b27528b4921e1d70bfc2138207c",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.1849,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-02-001",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-02-001",
   "text": "Pujadas, Valderrama, and Venters independently support Kaal's account of APIs as a mechanism for weakening vertically controlled value chains. Their abstract reports that, instead of locking in complementors, web APIs integrate capabilities across organizations for the co-production of services and products. This agrees with Kaal's claim that modular API connections can substitute for controlling an entire value chain, while the external source emphasizes capability integration and reduced lock-in rather than Kaal's cost-and-ease rationale.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-02",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2023.104931"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3808859-013"
   },
   "sha256": "fb69e9e1e87589746f7ee0b9237e28d24b3ba201d811939b21c552de457aabc6",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "substantively reviewed abstract-level qualification",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.2428,
   "mappingAmbiguous": false
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-01-006",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-01-006",
   "text": "Digital Transformation and Data Governance: Strategies for Regulatory Compliance and Secure AI-Driven Business Operations presents the following source proposition: The paper first examines the evolving landscape of AI regulation, emphasizing global frameworks such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and emerging AI governance policies. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Managing machine learning assets and complying with laws such as GDPR and CCPA becomes significantly harder under decentralized governance, because distributed data and operations complicate tracking data flows, enforcing privacy controls, and demonstrating compliance during audits. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-01",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.54660/.ijfmr.2021.2.1.43-55"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4796714-032"
   },
   "sha256": "eec3dadfc2a565893a886c17eb27466bde4e3673bc10a8dadb1edcc729d3f1a4",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.3527,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-01-005",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-01-005",
   "text": "Integrating law, technology, and design: teaching data protection and privacy law in a digital age presents the following source proposition: The data protection lawyer of the future will be a key intermediary of innovation—or ‘transaction engineer’—who facilitates and coordinates new forms of business and other social relationships in rapidly evolving multi-disciplinary settings. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that A transaction engineer is a crucial intermediary who brings together, in a safe environment, parties holding different but mutually compatible interests and expertise. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-01",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/idpl/ipac012"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3227967-007"
   },
   "sha256": "903fed0e572ad4441483dc1804873ff0752628bc9fdd67f849fda33b8e4467ee",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.399,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-08-01-004",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-08-01-004",
   "text": "Use of Artificial Intelligence in Terms of Open Innovation Process and Management presents the following source proposition: The increasing interest in artificial intelligence has led to a heightened interest in its potential applications in many industries. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Private fund advisers' increasing use of blockchain technology, artificial intelligence, and big data is a distinct source of downward pressure on the traditional 2/20 fee structure that commentators have not examined. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-08-01",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
