  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-7844",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-7844",
   "text": "Accountability in permissioned blockchains: through the ledger, the code and the people presents the following source proposition: The authors explore how implementing a digital transformation impacts the governance of network transactions. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that The distributed consensus model, in which network nodes verify and validate transactions before execution, makes it extremely rare for a fraudulent transaction to be recorded in the blockchain, and it does so without compromising the privacy of the parties. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
