  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-7458",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-7458",
   "text": "The AI Governance Market: Institutional Frameworks, Regulatory Architecture, and the Economics of Trust in Artificial Intelligence Systems presents the following source proposition: The rapid diffusion of artificial intelligence (AI) across economic sectors has generated substantial efficiency gains while simultaneously amplifying systemic risks related to safety, accountability, bias, and societal trust. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Because qubits hold multiple states simultaneously, quantum computers can perform complex calculations far faster than classical machines, and in a quantum economy this computational power can be used to solve intricate economic models, optimize resource allocation, and improve decision making across sectors. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
