   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-6499",
   "text": "A Lawyer’s Divorce: Will Decentralized Ledgers and Smart Contracts Succeed In Cutting Out the Middleman? presents the following source proposition: These technological changes are revolutionizing not only the science-oriented industries, but also the humanities. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Legacy insurers will not underwrite smart contracts that sit outside the traditional legal framework, and even if they eventually enter the DApp market their actuarial risk assessment methods may be only partially compatible with rapidly evolving decentralized products. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
