   "text": "Blockchain: What It Is, What It Does, and Why You Probably Don’t Need One presents the following source proposition: Conventional systems rely on the reputation of central authorities and record-keepers to achieve consensus. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that A truly decentralized public blockchain must satisfy three simultaneous requirements: no points of centralization and corruption, full security at the level of proof of work, and transaction throughput above one hundred thousand transactions per second. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
