   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.4123,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-6060",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-6060",
   "text": "Do we still need financial intermediation? The case of decentralized finance – DeFi presents the following source proposition: Design/methodology/approach The authors based their work on a twofold qualitative methodology, first analysing publicly available secondary data, particularly from white papers and DeFi Pulse (a website providing data on DeFi solutions) and then running two focus group discussions. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Centralized financial technology does not complete the disintermediation it promises, because users still deal with a technology company as intermediary instead of a financial institution, which leaves room for the decentralized solutions DeFi attempts to provide. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-07-31",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1108/qram-03-2021-0051"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3606663-026"
   },
   "sha256": "2c9d6b8bee0c393df0c6ba02b97fb7bd1599e506e66e66f8488a9c717efa14e0",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.3782,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-606",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-606",
   "text": "Agent-based modeling for decentralized autonomous organizations and decentralized finance should be assessed against Kaal's source-bound claim that Reward modeling learned through interaction with users carries two structural pathologies: majority views disproportionately influence the learned reward function, and the agent may engage in reward hacking. The current metadata indicates a plausible connection through decentralized autonomous organization, but the defensible response is a qualification until the source text confirms agreement, scope, methods, and limitations.",
   "responseType": "qualification",
   "datePublished": "2026-07-31",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa0fbb27886e60f2e4305bc874fd9aceb6eb56c4"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/4855607-015"
   },
   "sha256": "83270a5c6ce85c3a4b590a367a11e28f27ed7d9870a268a626f6c51bafe12e0d",
   "evidenceLevel": "metadata only",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.4565,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-6059",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-6059",
   "text": "Do we still need financial intermediation? The case of decentralized finance – DeFi presents the following source proposition: The aim of this research is to assess the role of intermediation in the light of DeFi, analysing how humans and algorithms will interact. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Centralized financial technology does not complete the disintermediation it promises, because users still deal with a technology company as intermediary instead of a financial institution, which leaves room for the decentralized solutions DeFi attempts to provide. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
   "datePublished": "2026-07-31",
   "creativeWorkStatus": "Affirmed",
   "respondsTo": {
    "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1108/qram-03-2021-0051"
   },
   "extends": {
    "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/3606663-026"
   },
   "sha256": "eb295d4622eddddaf0b19ea98fb5725c961a32c0a4e27388419236f469f92523",
   "evidenceLevel": "abstract indexed",
   "reviewTier": "moderate-confidence claim review",
   "mappingConfidence": 0.3869,
   "mappingAmbiguous": true
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/positions/2026-07-31-6058",
   "@type": "Claim",
   "identifier": "kaal:position:2026-07-31-6058",
   "text": "Do we still need financial intermediation? The case of decentralized finance – DeFi presents the following source proposition: However, some crucial tasks cannot be left solely to an algorithm and, consequently, most DeFi applications still require human decisions. This proposition is pertinent to Kaal's source-bound claim that Centralized financial technology does not complete the disintermediation it promises, because users still deal with a technology company as intermediary instead of a financial institution, which leaves room for the decentralized solutions DeFi attempts to provide. The proposed response is an extension: the relationship should remain limited to the retrieved source proposition and the mapped Kaal claim unless fuller source review supports a broader conclusion.",
   "responseType": "extension",
